window.SUBS={"note": {"en": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.860\nThe graded lab started from a Jupyter notebook about Velonecy,\n\n00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:06.260\na bike sharing network in a simulated city.\n\n00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:10.446\nThe goal was to turn it into a Flask application where the user chooses the\n\n00:00:10.446 --> 00:00:14.353\nQuadtree capacity, and the page shows the resulting split of the city,\n\n00:00:14.353 --> 00:00:15.580\nall running in Docker.\n\n00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:17.379\nThe flow is short.\n\n00:00:17.379 --> 00:00:20.858\nThe form sends the capacity in the query string with GET,\n\n00:00:20.858 --> 00:00:23.971\nso a result has its own URL that can be bookmarked.\n\n00:00:23.971 --> 00:00:28.549\nThe route validates the value, generates the stations, builds the Quadtree,\n\n00:00:28.549 --> 00:00:33.371\ndraws the figure into a PNG encoded in base64, and renders it in the same page.\n\n00:00:34.071 --> 00:00:35.224\nWhat is a Quadtree?\n\n00:00:35.224 --> 00:00:38.197\nA square region can hold at most capacity points.\n\n00:00:38.197 --> 00:00:42.141\nWhen one more point arrives, it splits into four equal quadrants,\n\n00:00:42.141 --> 00:00:43.476\nand so on recursively.\n\n00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:47.541\nSo dense areas, like the city centre, end up with many small cells,\n\n00:00:47.541 --> 00:00:49.300\nwhile empty areas stay large.\n\n00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:53.912\nIt makes spatial searches fast, such as finding the nearest station or every\n\n00:00:53.912 --> 00:00:55.307\nstation in a rectangle.\n\n00:00:56.007 --> 00:00:58.891\nThe project follows the same structure as lab two:\n\n00:00:58.891 --> 00:01:03.276\nan application factory, one blueprint for the routes, and a business module,\n\n00:01:03.276 --> 00:01:05.814\nquadtree.py, that knows nothing about Flask.\n\n00:01:05.814 --> 00:01:09.506\nThe validation limits, one to one hundred with eight by default,\n\n00:01:09.506 --> 00:01:13.428\nlive in the configuration rather than being hard coded in the route.\n\n00:01:14.128 --> 00:01:15.965\nParse capacity returns a pair:\n\n00:01:15.965 --> 00:01:17.864\nthe value, or an error message.\n\n00:01:17.864 --> 00:01:19.885\nAn empty value means the default.\n\n00:01:19.885 --> 00:01:24.539\nText that isn't an integer is refused, and so is a value outside the bounds.\n\n00:01:24.539 --> 00:01:26.315\nThe bounds aren't decoration:\n\n00:01:26.315 --> 00:01:29.377\na capacity of zero would make cells split forever,\n\n00:01:29.377 --> 00:01:32.316\nand a huge one would give a single useless cell.\n\n00:01:33.016 --> 00:01:36.790\nThe route reads the capacity from request.args, the query string.\n\n00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:41.028\nOn an error, it shows the same page with the message and the value typed,\n\n00:01:41.028 --> 00:01:44.917\nand returns status four hundred, bad request, by returning a tuple.\n\n00:01:44.917 --> 00:01:48.052\nOtherwise it runs the pipeline and renders the result.\n\n00:01:48.752 --> 00:01:52.869\nThe stations are generated around four centres of interest with a normal\n\n00:01:52.869 --> 00:01:55.442\ndistribution, plus forty random new stations.\n\n00:01:55.442 --> 00:01:57.615\nThe random generator has a fixed seed,\n\n00:01:57.615 --> 00:02:02.418\nso the city is identical on every request and only the capacity changes the picture.\n\n00:02:02.418 --> 00:02:04.934\nThen the pyquadtree library builds the tree,\n\n00:02:04.934 --> 00:02:09.279\nwith max elements equal to the capacity and a maximum depth as a safety net.\n\n00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:12.201\nThree details matter for the figure.\n\n00:02:12.201 --> 00:02:14.608\nThe Agg backend draws without a screen,\n\n00:02:14.608 --> 00:02:17.385\nwhich is required in a server or a container.\n\n00:02:17.385 --> 00:02:21.273\nThe PNG is written into memory, not to disk, encoded in base64,\n\n00:02:21.273 --> 00:02:24.359\nand placed directly in an image tag as a data URL.\n\n00:02:24.359 --> 00:02:27.321\nAnd plt close frees the figure on every request,\n\n00:02:27.321 --> 00:02:30.037\notherwise the server's memory keeps growing.\n\n00:02:30.737 --> 00:02:35.671\nThe Dockerfile copies the requirements and installs them before copying the code,\n\n00:02:35.671 --> 00:02:40.361\nso changing the code reuses the cached package layer and rebuilds in seconds.\n\n00:02:40.361 --> 00:02:44.259\nAnd it runs gunicorn, a production WSGI server with two workers,\n\n00:02:44.259 --> 00:02:48.401\nbound to zero zero zero zero, instead of Flask's development server.\n\n00:02:49.101 --> 00:02:49.908\nOral practice.\n\n00:02:49.908 --> 00:02:53.308\nWhat happens when you lower the capacity from eight to two?\n\n00:02:53.308 --> 00:02:57.918\nCells split as soon as they hold more than two stations, so there are many more,\n\n00:02:57.918 --> 00:03:01.260\nsmaller cells, mostly in dense areas like the city centre,\n\n00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.104\nwhile empty areas barely change.\n\n00:03:03.804 --> 00:03:05.912\nWhy GET and not POST for this form?\n\n00:03:05.912 --> 00:03:10.430\nBecause the request only reads and computes, nothing changes on the server.\n\n00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:14.647\nWith GET, the capacity is in the URL, so the result can be bookmarked,\n\n00:03:14.647 --> 00:03:16.274\nshared and reloaded safely.\n\n00:03:16.974 --> 00:03:17.727\nTo remember:\n\n00:03:17.727 --> 00:03:21.680\nthe Quadtree rule, query string validation with a four hundred,\n\n00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:24.441\na figure drawn in memory and sent as base64,\n\n00:03:24.441 --> 00:03:27.704\nand a Docker image built for caching and production.\n\n00:03:27.704 --> 00:03:29.147\nGood luck for the oral.\n", "fr": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.188\nLe TP noté partait d'un notebook Jupyter sur Velonecy,\n\n00:00:03.188 --> 00:00:06.235\nun réseau de vélos en libre-service dans une ville simulée.\n\n00:00:06.235 --> 00:00:10.210\nL'objectif était d'en faire une application Flask où l'utilisateur choisit la\n\n00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:14.083\ncapacité du Quadtree, et où la page affiche le découpage de la ville qui en\n\n00:00:14.083 --> 00:00:15.580\nrésulte, le tout dans Docker.\n\n00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:17.338\nLe flux est court.\n\n00:00:17.338 --> 00:00:21.038\nLe formulaire envoie la capacité dans la query string avec GET,\n\n00:00:21.038 --> 00:00:24.620\ndonc un résultat a sa propre URL qu'on peut mettre en favori.\n\n00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:28.731\nLa route valide la valeur, génère les stations, construit le Quadtree,\n\n00:00:28.731 --> 00:00:33.371\ndessine la figure dans un PNG encodé en base64, et l'affiche dans la même page.\n\n00:00:34.071 --> 00:00:35.355\nQu'est-ce qu'un Quadtree ?\n\n00:00:35.355 --> 00:00:38.022\nUne zone carrée peut contenir au plus capacity points.\n\n00:00:38.022 --> 00:00:41.578\nQuand un point de plus arrive, elle se divise en quatre quadrants égaux,\n\n00:00:41.578 --> 00:00:43.158\net ainsi de suite récursivement.\n\n00:00:43.158 --> 00:00:45.134\nLes zones denses, comme le centre-ville,\n\n00:00:45.134 --> 00:00:47.307\nfinissent avec beaucoup de petites cellules,\n\n00:00:47.307 --> 00:00:49.430\ntandis que les zones vides restent grandes.\n\n00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:51.455\nÇa rend les recherches spatiales rapides,\n\n00:00:51.455 --> 00:00:55.307\ncomme trouver la station la plus proche ou toutes les stations d'un rectangle.\n\n00:00:56.007 --> 00:00:58.787\nLe projet suit la même structure que le TP2 :\n\n00:00:58.787 --> 00:01:03.544\nune factory d'application, un Blueprint pour les routes, et un module métier,\n\n00:01:03.544 --> 00:01:05.953\nquadtree.py, qui ne sait rien de Flask.\n\n00:01:05.953 --> 00:01:09.351\nLes bornes de validation, de 1 à 100 avec 8 par défaut,\n\n00:01:09.351 --> 00:01:13.428\nsont dans la configuration plutôt que codées en dur dans la route.\n\n00:01:14.128 --> 00:01:15.931\nparse_capacity renvoie une paire :\n\n00:01:15.931 --> 00:01:17.734\nla valeur, ou un message d'erreur.\n\n00:01:17.734 --> 00:01:20.014\nUne valeur vide donne la valeur par défaut.\n\n00:01:20.014 --> 00:01:24.415\nUn texte qui n'est pas un entier est refusé, tout comme une valeur hors des bornes.\n\n00:01:24.415 --> 00:01:26.324\nLes bornes ne sont pas décoratives :\n\n00:01:26.324 --> 00:01:29.665\nune capacité de zéro ferait se diviser les cellules à l'infini,\n\n00:01:29.665 --> 00:01:32.316\net une énorme donnerait une seule cellule inutile.\n\n00:01:33.016 --> 00:01:36.513\nLa route lit la capacité dans request.args, la query string.\n\n00:01:36.513 --> 00:01:41.117\nEn cas d'erreur, elle affiche la même page avec le message et la valeur saisie,\n\n00:01:41.117 --> 00:01:44.847\net renvoie le code 400, requête invalide, en renvoyant un tuple.\n\n00:01:44.847 --> 00:01:48.052\nSinon, elle lance le traitement et affiche le résultat.\n\n00:01:48.752 --> 00:01:53.200\nLes stations sont générées autour de quatre centres d'intérêt avec une loi normale,\n\n00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.559\nplus quarante nouvelles stations aléatoires.\n\n00:01:55.559 --> 00:01:57.810\nLe générateur aléatoire a une graine fixe,\n\n00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:02.151\ndonc la ville est identique à chaque requête et seule la capacité change l'image.\n\n00:02:02.151 --> 00:02:04.992\nEnsuite la bibliothèque pyquadtree construit l'arbre,\n\n00:02:04.992 --> 00:02:09.279\navec max_elements égal à la capacité et une profondeur maximale comme garde-fou.\n\n00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:12.138\nTrois détails comptent pour la figure.\n\n00:02:12.138 --> 00:02:16.627\nLe backend Agg dessine sans écran, ce qui est obligatoire dans un serveur ou un\n\n00:02:16.627 --> 00:02:17.195\nconteneur.\n\n00:02:17.195 --> 00:02:20.889\nLe PNG est écrit en mémoire, pas sur le disque, encodé en base64,\n\n00:02:20.889 --> 00:02:24.639\net placé directement dans une balise image sous forme de data URL.\n\n00:02:24.639 --> 00:02:27.309\nEt plt.close libère la figure à chaque requête,\n\n00:02:27.309 --> 00:02:30.037\nsinon la mémoire du serveur ne cesse de grossir.\n\n00:02:30.737 --> 00:02:35.211\nLe Dockerfile copie les requirements et les installe avant de copier le code,\n\n00:02:35.211 --> 00:02:39.801\ndonc modifier le code réutilise la couche de paquets en cache et reconstruit en\n\n00:02:39.801 --> 00:02:40.847\nquelques secondes.\n\n00:02:40.847 --> 00:02:44.915\nEt il lance gunicorn, un serveur WSGI de production avec deux workers,\n\n00:02:44.915 --> 00:02:48.401\nlié à 0.0.0.0, au lieu du serveur de développement de Flask.\n\n00:02:49.101 --> 00:02:50.146\nEntraînement à l'oral.\n\n00:02:50.146 --> 00:02:52.661\nQue se passe-t-il si on baisse la capacité de 8 à 2 ?\n\n00:02:52.661 --> 00:02:56.079\nLes cellules se divisent dès qu'elles contiennent plus de deux stations,\n\n00:02:56.079 --> 00:02:58.547\ndonc il y a beaucoup plus de cellules, plus petites,\n\n00:02:58.547 --> 00:03:01.015\nsurtout dans les zones denses comme le centre-ville,\n\n00:03:01.015 --> 00:03:03.104\ntandis que les zones vides changent à peine.\n\n00:03:03.804 --> 00:03:06.172\nPourquoi GET et pas POST pour ce formulaire ?\n\n00:03:06.172 --> 00:03:10.433\nParce que la requête ne fait que lire et calculer, rien ne change sur le serveur.\n\n00:03:10.433 --> 00:03:14.590\nAvec GET, la capacité est dans l'URL, donc le résultat peut être mis en favori,\n\n00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:16.274\npartagé et rechargé sans risque.\n\n00:03:16.974 --> 00:03:17.599\nÀ retenir :\n\n00:03:17.599 --> 00:03:21.468\nla règle du Quadtree, la validation de la query string avec une 400,\n\n00:03:21.468 --> 00:03:24.426\nune figure dessinée en mémoire et envoyée en base64,\n\n00:03:24.426 --> 00:03:27.725\net une image Docker pensée pour le cache et la production.\n\n00:03:27.725 --> 00:03:29.147\nBonne chance pour l'oral.\n"}, "note_fr": {"en": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.064\nThe graded lab started from a Jupyter notebook about Velonecy,\n\n00:00:04.064 --> 00:00:06.605\na bike sharing network in a simulated city.\n\n00:00:06.605 --> 00:00:11.037\nThe goal was to turn it into a Flask application where the user chooses the\n\n00:00:11.037 --> 00:00:15.174\nQuadtree capacity, and the page shows the resulting split of the city,\n\n00:00:15.174 --> 00:00:16.474\nall running in Docker.\n\n00:00:17.174 --> 00:00:18.366\nThe flow is short.\n\n00:00:18.366 --> 00:00:22.138\nThe form sends the capacity in the query string with GET,\n\n00:00:22.138 --> 00:00:25.513\nso a result has its own URL that can be bookmarked.\n\n00:00:25.513 --> 00:00:30.476\nThe route validates the value, generates the stations, builds the Quadtree,\n\n00:00:30.476 --> 00:00:35.705\ndraws the figure into a PNG encoded in base64, and renders it in the same page.\n\n00:00:36.405 --> 00:00:37.667\nWhat is a Quadtree?\n\n00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:40.923\nA square region can hold at most capacity points.\n\n00:00:40.923 --> 00:00:45.242\nWhen one more point arrives, it splits into four equal quadrants,\n\n00:00:45.242 --> 00:00:46.704\nand so on recursively.\n\n00:00:46.704 --> 00:00:51.156\nSo dense areas, like the city centre, end up with many small cells,\n\n00:00:51.156 --> 00:00:53.083\nwhile empty areas stay large.\n\n00:00:53.083 --> 00:00:58.133\nIt makes spatial searches fast, such as finding the nearest station or every\n\n00:00:58.133 --> 00:00:59.661\nstation in a rectangle.\n\n00:01:00.361 --> 00:01:03.016\nThe project follows the same structure as lab two:\n\n00:01:03.016 --> 00:01:07.053\nan application factory, one blueprint for the routes, and a business module,\n\n00:01:07.053 --> 00:01:09.390\nquadtree.py, that knows nothing about Flask.\n\n00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:12.789\nThe validation limits, one to one hundred with eight by default,\n\n00:01:12.789 --> 00:01:16.400\nlive in the configuration rather than being hard coded in the route.\n\n00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.155\nParse capacity returns a pair:\n\n00:01:19.155 --> 00:01:21.277\nthe value, or an error message.\n\n00:01:21.277 --> 00:01:23.537\nAn empty value means the default.\n\n00:01:23.537 --> 00:01:28.741\nText that isn't an integer is refused, and so is a value outside the bounds.\n\n00:01:28.741 --> 00:01:30.726\nThe bounds aren't decoration:\n\n00:01:30.726 --> 00:01:34.150\na capacity of zero would make cells split forever,\n\n00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:37.436\nand a huge one would give a single useless cell.\n\n00:01:38.136 --> 00:01:42.055\nThe route reads the capacity from request.args, the query string.\n\n00:01:42.055 --> 00:01:46.457\nOn an error, it shows the same page with the message and the value typed,\n\n00:01:46.457 --> 00:01:50.497\nand returns status four hundred, bad request, by returning a tuple.\n\n00:01:50.497 --> 00:01:53.753\nOtherwise it runs the pipeline and renders the result.\n\n00:01:54.453 --> 00:01:58.893\nThe stations are generated around four centres of interest with a normal\n\n00:01:58.893 --> 00:02:01.668\ndistribution, plus forty random new stations.\n\n00:02:01.668 --> 00:02:04.012\nThe random generator has a fixed seed,\n\n00:02:04.012 --> 00:02:09.193\nso the city is identical on every request and only the capacity changes the picture.\n\n00:02:09.193 --> 00:02:11.906\nThen the pyquadtree library builds the tree,\n\n00:02:11.906 --> 00:02:16.593\nwith max elements equal to the capacity and a maximum depth as a safety net.\n\n00:02:17.293 --> 00:02:19.734\nThree details matter for the figure.\n\n00:02:19.734 --> 00:02:22.378\nThe Agg backend draws without a screen,\n\n00:02:22.378 --> 00:02:25.428\nwhich is required in a server or a container.\n\n00:02:25.428 --> 00:02:29.699\nThe PNG is written into memory, not to disk, encoded in base64,\n\n00:02:29.699 --> 00:02:33.088\nand placed directly in an image tag as a data URL.\n\n00:02:33.088 --> 00:02:36.342\nAnd plt close frees the figure on every request,\n\n00:02:36.342 --> 00:02:39.324\notherwise the server's memory keeps growing.\n\n00:02:40.024 --> 00:02:45.056\nThe Dockerfile copies the requirements and installs them before copying the code,\n\n00:02:45.056 --> 00:02:49.839\nso changing the code reuses the cached package layer and rebuilds in seconds.\n\n00:02:49.839 --> 00:02:53.814\nAnd it runs gunicorn, a production WSGI server with two workers,\n\n00:02:53.814 --> 00:02:58.038\nbound to zero zero zero zero, instead of Flask's development server.\n\n00:02:58.738 --> 00:02:59.595\nOral practice.\n\n00:02:59.595 --> 00:03:03.209\nWhat happens when you lower the capacity from eight to two?\n\n00:03:03.209 --> 00:03:08.110\nCells split as soon as they hold more than two stations, so there are many more,\n\n00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:11.662\nsmaller cells, mostly in dense areas like the city centre,\n\n00:03:11.662 --> 00:03:13.622\nwhile empty areas barely change.\n\n00:03:14.322 --> 00:03:16.810\nWhy GET and not POST for this form?\n\n00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:22.140\nBecause the request only reads and computes, nothing changes on the server.\n\n00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:27.114\nWith GET, the capacity is in the URL, so the result can be bookmarked,\n\n00:03:27.114 --> 00:03:29.033\nshared and reloaded safely.\n\n00:03:29.733 --> 00:03:30.569\nTo remember:\n\n00:03:30.569 --> 00:03:34.955\nthe Quadtree rule, query string validation with a four hundred,\n\n00:03:34.955 --> 00:03:38.019\na figure drawn in memory and sent as base64,\n\n00:03:38.019 --> 00:03:41.640\nand a Docker image built for caching and production.\n\n00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.242\nGood luck for the oral.\n", "fr": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.352\nLe TP noté partait d'un notebook Jupyter sur Velonecy,\n\n00:00:03.352 --> 00:00:06.578\nun réseau de vélos en libre-service dans une ville simulée.\n\n00:00:06.578 --> 00:00:10.788\nL'objectif était d'en faire une application Flask où l'utilisateur choisit la\n\n00:00:10.788 --> 00:00:14.889\ncapacité du Quadtree, et où la page affiche le découpage de la ville qui en\n\n00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:16.474\nrésulte, le tout dans Docker.\n\n00:00:17.174 --> 00:00:18.321\nLe flux est court.\n\n00:00:18.321 --> 00:00:22.332\nLe formulaire envoie la capacité dans la query string avec GET,\n\n00:00:22.332 --> 00:00:26.217\ndonc un résultat a sa propre URL qu'on peut mettre en favori.\n\n00:00:26.217 --> 00:00:30.674\nLa route valide la valeur, génère les stations, construit le Quadtree,\n\n00:00:30.674 --> 00:00:35.705\ndessine la figure dans un PNG encodé en base64, et l'affiche dans la même page.\n\n00:00:36.405 --> 00:00:37.811\nQu'est-ce qu'un Quadtree ?\n\n00:00:37.811 --> 00:00:40.731\nUne zone carrée peut contenir au plus capacity points.\n\n00:00:40.731 --> 00:00:44.625\nQuand un point de plus arrive, elle se divise en quatre quadrants égaux,\n\n00:00:44.625 --> 00:00:46.356\net ainsi de suite récursivement.\n\n00:00:46.356 --> 00:00:48.520\nLes zones denses, comme le centre-ville,\n\n00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.899\nfinissent avec beaucoup de petites cellules,\n\n00:00:50.899 --> 00:00:53.225\ntandis que les zones vides restent grandes.\n\n00:00:53.225 --> 00:00:55.442\nÇa rend les recherches spatiales rapides,\n\n00:00:55.442 --> 00:00:59.661\ncomme trouver la station la plus proche ou toutes les stations d'un rectangle.\n\n00:01:00.361 --> 00:01:02.920\nLe projet suit la même structure que le TP2 :\n\n00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:07.300\nune factory d'application, un Blueprint pour les routes, et un module métier,\n\n00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.518\nquadtree.py, qui ne sait rien de Flask.\n\n00:01:09.518 --> 00:01:12.646\nLes bornes de validation, de 1 à 100 avec 8 par défaut,\n\n00:01:12.646 --> 00:01:16.400\nsont dans la configuration plutôt que codées en dur dans la route.\n\n00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.116\nparse_capacity renvoie une paire :\n\n00:01:19.116 --> 00:01:21.132\nla valeur, ou un message d'erreur.\n\n00:01:21.132 --> 00:01:23.681\nUne valeur vide donne la valeur par défaut.\n\n00:01:23.681 --> 00:01:28.602\nUn texte qui n'est pas un entier est refusé, tout comme une valeur hors des bornes.\n\n00:01:28.602 --> 00:01:30.737\nLes bornes ne sont pas décoratives :\n\n00:01:30.737 --> 00:01:34.472\nune capacité de zéro ferait se diviser les cellules à l'infini,\n\n00:01:34.472 --> 00:01:37.436\net une énorme donnerait une seule cellule inutile.\n\n00:01:38.136 --> 00:01:41.768\nLa route lit la capacité dans request.args, la query string.\n\n00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:46.550\nEn cas d'erreur, elle affiche la même page avec le message et la valeur saisie,\n\n00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:50.423\net renvoie le code 400, requête invalide, en renvoyant un tuple.\n\n00:01:50.423 --> 00:01:53.753\nSinon, elle lance le traitement et affiche le résultat.\n\n00:01:54.453 --> 00:01:59.251\nLes stations sont générées autour de quatre centres d'intérêt avec une loi normale,\n\n00:01:59.251 --> 00:02:01.794\nplus quarante nouvelles stations aléatoires.\n\n00:02:01.794 --> 00:02:04.222\nLe générateur aléatoire a une graine fixe,\n\n00:02:04.222 --> 00:02:08.905\ndonc la ville est identique à chaque requête et seule la capacité change l'image.\n\n00:02:08.905 --> 00:02:11.969\nEnsuite la bibliothèque pyquadtree construit l'arbre,\n\n00:02:11.969 --> 00:02:16.593\navec max_elements égal à la capacité et une profondeur maximale comme garde-fou.\n\n00:02:17.293 --> 00:02:19.665\nTrois détails comptent pour la figure.\n\n00:02:19.665 --> 00:02:24.596\nLe backend Agg dessine sans écran, ce qui est obligatoire dans un serveur ou un\n\n00:02:24.596 --> 00:02:25.220\nconteneur.\n\n00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:29.276\nLe PNG est écrit en mémoire, pas sur le disque, encodé en base64,\n\n00:02:29.276 --> 00:02:33.395\net placé directement dans une balise image sous forme de data URL.\n\n00:02:33.395 --> 00:02:36.329\nEt plt.close libère la figure à chaque requête,\n\n00:02:36.329 --> 00:02:39.324\nsinon la mémoire du serveur ne cesse de grossir.\n\n00:02:40.024 --> 00:02:44.587\nLe Dockerfile copie les requirements et les installe avant de copier le code,\n\n00:02:44.587 --> 00:02:49.268\ndonc modifier le code réutilise la couche de paquets en cache et reconstruit en\n\n00:02:49.268 --> 00:02:50.335\nquelques secondes.\n\n00:02:50.335 --> 00:02:54.482\nEt il lance gunicorn, un serveur WSGI de production avec deux workers,\n\n00:02:54.482 --> 00:02:58.038\nlié à 0.0.0.0, au lieu du serveur de développement de Flask.\n\n00:02:58.738 --> 00:02:59.848\nEntraînement à l'oral.\n\n00:02:59.848 --> 00:03:02.522\nQue se passe-t-il si on baisse la capacité de 8 à 2 ?\n\n00:03:02.522 --> 00:03:06.155\nLes cellules se divisent dès qu'elles contiennent plus de deux stations,\n\n00:03:06.155 --> 00:03:08.779\ndonc il y a beaucoup plus de cellules, plus petites,\n\n00:03:08.779 --> 00:03:11.402\nsurtout dans les zones denses comme le centre-ville,\n\n00:03:11.402 --> 00:03:13.622\ntandis que les zones vides changent à peine.\n\n00:03:14.322 --> 00:03:17.116\nPourquoi GET et pas POST pour ce formulaire ?\n\n00:03:17.116 --> 00:03:22.143\nParce que la requête ne fait que lire et calculer, rien ne change sur le serveur.\n\n00:03:22.143 --> 00:03:27.047\nAvec GET, la capacité est dans l'URL, donc le résultat peut être mis en favori,\n\n00:03:27.047 --> 00:03:29.033\npartagé et rechargé sans risque.\n\n00:03:29.733 --> 00:03:30.427\nÀ retenir :\n\n00:03:30.427 --> 00:03:34.720\nla règle du Quadtree, la validation de la query string avec une 400,\n\n00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:38.002\nune figure dessinée en mémoire et envoyée en base64,\n\n00:03:38.002 --> 00:03:41.663\net une image Docker pensée pour le cache et la production.\n\n00:03:41.663 --> 00:03:43.242\nBonne chance pour l'oral.\n"}, "tp1": {"en": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.524\nWelcome to lab one.\n\n00:00:01.524 --> 00:00:04.599\nIn this video we build our first real Flask feature:\n\n00:00:04.599 --> 00:00:08.087\na page with a form that sends a temperature and a humidity,\n\n00:00:08.087 --> 00:00:11.872\nand displays the heat index, which is how hot it actually feels.\n\n00:00:11.872 --> 00:00:16.248\nAlong the way we cover routes, GET and POST, reading form data, templates,\n\n00:00:16.248 --> 00:00:16.899\nand Docker.\n\n00:00:17.599 --> 00:00:18.879\nHere is the whole journey.\n\n00:00:18.879 --> 00:00:20.405\nThe browser shows an HTML form.\n\n00:00:20.405 --> 00:00:23.902\nWhen you click the button, it sends a POST request to slash heat index.\n\n00:00:23.902 --> 00:00:27.644\nFlask calls our view function, which reads the two values from request.form,\n\n00:00:27.644 --> 00:00:29.515\npasses them to the heat index formula,\n\n00:00:29.515 --> 00:00:31.928\nand finally renders the template with the result.\n\n00:00:31.928 --> 00:00:33.258\nThe same URL does two jobs:\n\n00:00:33.258 --> 00:00:37.000\na GET request shows the empty form, and a POST request does the calculation.\n\n00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:39.531\nEverything starts with one line:\n\n00:00:39.531 --> 00:00:41.876\napp equals Flask, double underscore name.\n\n00:00:41.876 --> 00:00:43.935\nThis creates the application object.\n\n00:00:43.935 --> 00:00:47.024\nThe name argument tells Flask where our package lives,\n\n00:00:47.024 --> 00:00:50.971\nso it can find the templates folder and the static folder next to it.\n\n00:00:51.671 --> 00:00:55.176\nThe app.route decorator links a URL to a Python function.\n\n00:00:55.176 --> 00:00:58.619\nWhatever the function returns becomes the HTTP response.\n\n00:00:58.619 --> 00:01:02.430\nIn the second route, the part in angle brackets is a variable:\n\n00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:07.103\nif you open slash user slash Mateo, Flask passes Mateo as the name argument,\n\n00:01:07.103 --> 00:01:08.948\nand the page says Hello Mateo.\n\n00:01:09.648 --> 00:01:10.982\nNow the real feature.\n\n00:01:10.982 --> 00:01:13.906\nBy default, a route only accepts GET requests,\n\n00:01:13.906 --> 00:01:16.512\nso we explicitly allow both GET and POST.\n\n00:01:16.512 --> 00:01:20.771\nInside the function, request.method tells us which one we received.\n\n00:01:20.771 --> 00:01:24.330\nOn a GET, we skip the calculation and result stays None.\n\n00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:25.729\nOn a POST, we compute.\n\n00:01:26.429 --> 00:01:29.986\nrequest.form is a dictionary of everything the form sent,\n\n00:01:29.986 --> 00:01:32.607\nkeyed by the name attribute of each input.\n\n00:01:32.607 --> 00:01:33.231\nImportant:\n\n00:01:33.231 --> 00:01:36.788\nevery value is a string, even if the user typed a number.\n\n00:01:36.788 --> 00:01:38.909\nThat is why we wrap them in float.\n\n00:01:38.909 --> 00:01:42.841\nIf we forgot, the formula would try to multiply text and crash.\n\n00:01:43.541 --> 00:01:46.239\nThe formula itself is a plain Python function:\n\n00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:50.462\na polynomial of the temperature and the humidity with nine coefficients.\n\n00:01:50.462 --> 00:01:52.163\nIt knows nothing about Flask.\n\n00:01:52.163 --> 00:01:53.571\nThat separation matters:\n\n00:01:53.571 --> 00:01:57.384\nthe route deals with HTTP, and the function deals with the maths,\n\n00:01:57.384 --> 00:02:00.376\nso each one is easy to read and to test on its own.\n\n00:02:01.076 --> 00:02:03.903\nrender template fills an HTML file with Jinja.\n\n00:02:03.903 --> 00:02:08.819\nThe form posts to the same URL, and each input's name matches the key we read in\n\n00:02:08.819 --> 00:02:09.249\nPython.\n\n00:02:09.249 --> 00:02:13.551\nBelow the form, an if block only shows the result when it is not None,\n\n00:02:13.551 --> 00:02:15.947\nso the first visit shows an empty form.\n\n00:02:15.947 --> 00:02:20.249\nDouble curly braces print a value, and Jinja escapes it automatically,\n\n00:02:20.249 --> 00:02:22.584\nwhich protects us from HTML injection.\n\n00:02:23.284 --> 00:02:26.140\nTo run it anywhere, we put it in a Docker image.\n\n00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:29.947\nWe start from a slim Python image, copy the code, install Flask,\n\n00:02:29.947 --> 00:02:34.349\nand start it with flask run, using environment variables for the app file,\n\n00:02:34.349 --> 00:02:35.658\nthe host and the port.\n\n00:02:35.658 --> 00:02:37.145\nThe key line is the host:\n\n00:02:37.145 --> 00:02:38.335\nzero zero zero zero.\n\n00:02:38.335 --> 00:02:42.916\nInside a container, one two seven zero zero one only accepts connections from\n\n00:02:42.916 --> 00:02:47.556\ninside the container itself, so the port we publish would never reach the app.\n\n00:02:48.256 --> 00:02:52.147\nLet's be honest about the weak points, because the examiner may ask.\n\n00:02:52.147 --> 00:02:54.837\nFirst, if you type letters instead of a number,\n\n00:02:54.837 --> 00:02:58.042\nfloat raises a ValueError and the user gets a 500 error.\n\n00:02:58.042 --> 00:03:01.304\nWe should catch it and return a 400 with a clear message.\n\n00:03:01.304 --> 00:03:05.996\nSecond, the docker compose volume points to the wrong folder, so it has no effect.\n\n00:03:05.996 --> 00:03:10.231\nThird, a small script in the template looks for elements that don't exist.\n\n00:03:10.231 --> 00:03:14.351\nAnd the inputs are text fields, so their min and max limits are ignored.\n\n00:03:15.051 --> 00:03:17.255\nHere is how we fix the first one live.\n\n00:03:17.255 --> 00:03:19.749\nWe wrap the two conversions in a try block.\n\n00:03:19.749 --> 00:03:24.098\nIf a ValueError happens, we render the same template with an error message,\n\n00:03:24.098 --> 00:03:25.374\nand we return a tuple:\n\n00:03:25.374 --> 00:03:29.259\nthe page and the status code four hundred, which means bad request.\n\n00:03:29.259 --> 00:03:32.739\nWe also check that humidity is between zero and one hundred.\n\n00:03:32.739 --> 00:03:36.624\nIn the template, we just display the error variable when it exists.\n\n00:03:37.324 --> 00:03:38.163\nOral practice.\n\n00:03:38.163 --> 00:03:41.337\nWhy do we accept both GET and POST on the same route?\n\n00:03:41.337 --> 00:03:43.492\nBecause GET displays the empty form,\n\n00:03:43.492 --> 00:03:48.283\nand POST sends the data in the body of the request and triggers the calculation.\n\n00:03:48.283 --> 00:03:51.936\nInside the function, request.method tells us which one it is.\n\n00:03:52.636 --> 00:03:53.592\nSecond question:\n\n00:03:53.592 --> 00:03:55.623\nwhy zero zero zero zero in Docker?\n\n00:03:55.623 --> 00:04:00.402\nBecause the loopback address only accepts connections from inside the container.\n\n00:04:00.402 --> 00:04:05.002\nListening on all interfaces lets the port we publish, five thousand and five,\n\n00:04:05.002 --> 00:04:07.033\nreach Flask from the host machine.\n\n00:04:07.733 --> 00:04:08.360\nTo sum up.\n\n00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:13.559\nA route maps a URL to a function, and you need the methods argument to accept POST.\n\n00:04:13.559 --> 00:04:18.007\nForm and query values are always strings, so convert and validate them.\n\n00:04:18.007 --> 00:04:22.955\nTemplates print values with double braces and use curly percent tags for logic.\n\n00:04:22.955 --> 00:04:26.213\nAnd in Docker, always listen on zero zero zero zero.\n\n00:04:26.213 --> 00:04:29.220\nSee you in lab two, where we connect a database.\n", "fr": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.446\nBienvenue dans le TP1.\n\n00:00:01.446 --> 00:00:04.964\nDans cette vidéo, on construit notre première vraie fonctionnalité Flask :\n\n00:00:04.964 --> 00:00:08.340\nune page avec un formulaire qui envoie une température et une humidité,\n\n00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.859\net qui affiche l'indice de chaleur, c'est-à-dire la température ressentie.\n\n00:00:11.859 --> 00:00:15.757\nAu passage, on voit les routes, GET et POST, la lecture des données de formulaire,\n\n00:00:15.757 --> 00:00:16.899\nles templates et Docker.\n\n00:00:17.599 --> 00:00:18.679\nVoici tout le parcours.\n\n00:00:18.679 --> 00:00:20.605\nLe navigateur affiche un formulaire HTML.\n\n00:00:20.605 --> 00:00:24.128\nQuand on clique sur le bouton, il envoie une requête POST vers /heat_index.\n\n00:00:24.128 --> 00:00:27.887\nFlask appelle notre fonction de vue, qui lit les deux valeurs dans request.form,\n\n00:00:27.887 --> 00:00:30.048\nles passe à la formule de l'indice de chaleur,\n\n00:00:30.048 --> 00:00:31.880\npuis rend le template avec le résultat.\n\n00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.289\nLa même URL fait deux choses :\n\n00:00:33.289 --> 00:00:37.000\nune requête GET affiche le formulaire vide, et une requête POST fait le calcul.\n\n00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:39.533\nTout commence par une ligne :\n\n00:00:39.533 --> 00:00:40.923\napp = Flask(__name__).\n\n00:00:40.923 --> 00:00:42.819\nElle crée l'objet application.\n\n00:00:42.819 --> 00:00:46.737\nL'argument __name__ indique à Flask où se trouve notre paquet,\n\n00:00:46.737 --> 00:00:50.971\npour qu'il trouve le dossier templates et le dossier static à côté.\n\n00:00:51.671 --> 00:00:55.482\nLe décorateur app.route relie une URL à une fonction Python.\n\n00:00:55.482 --> 00:00:58.721\nCe que la fonction renvoie devient la réponse HTTP.\n\n00:00:58.721 --> 00:01:02.977\nDans la deuxième route, la partie entre chevrons est une variable :\n\n00:01:02.977 --> 00:01:06.979\nsi on ouvre /user/Mateo, Flask passe Mateo comme argument name,\n\n00:01:06.979 --> 00:01:08.948\net la page affiche Hello Mateo.\n\n00:01:09.648 --> 00:01:11.775\nPassons à la vraie fonctionnalité.\n\n00:01:11.775 --> 00:01:17.031\nPar défaut, une route n'accepte que GET, donc on autorise explicitement GET et POST.\n\n00:01:17.031 --> 00:01:20.848\nDans la fonction, request.method indique laquelle on a reçue.\n\n00:01:20.848 --> 00:01:24.227\nSur un GET, on saute le calcul et result reste à None.\n\n00:01:24.227 --> 00:01:25.729\nSur un POST, on calcule.\n\n00:01:26.429 --> 00:01:30.082\nrequest.form est un dictionnaire de tout ce que le formulaire a envoyé,\n\n00:01:30.082 --> 00:01:32.294\nindexé par l'attribut name de chaque champ.\n\n00:01:32.294 --> 00:01:32.860\nImportant :\n\n00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.770\ntoutes les valeurs sont des chaînes, même si l'utilisateur a tapé un nombre.\n\n00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:38.879\nC'est pour ça qu'on les passe dans float.\n\n00:01:38.879 --> 00:01:42.841\nSi on l'oubliait, la formule essaierait de multiplier du texte et planterait.\n\n00:01:43.541 --> 00:01:46.739\nLa formule elle-même est une simple fonction Python :\n\n00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:50.963\nun polynôme de la température et de l'humidité avec neuf coefficients.\n\n00:01:50.963 --> 00:01:52.592\nElle ne sait rien de Flask.\n\n00:01:52.592 --> 00:01:54.100\nCette séparation compte :\n\n00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:57.117\nla route gère le HTTP, la fonction gère les maths,\n\n00:01:57.117 --> 00:02:00.376\ndonc chacune est facile à lire et à tester séparément.\n\n00:02:01.076 --> 00:02:03.811\nrender_template remplit un fichier HTML avec Jinja.\n\n00:02:03.811 --> 00:02:05.796\nLe formulaire poste vers la même URL,\n\n00:02:05.796 --> 00:02:09.390\net le name de chaque champ correspond à la clé qu'on lit en Python.\n\n00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:13.520\nSous le formulaire, un bloc if n'affiche le résultat que s'il n'est pas None,\n\n00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.202\ndonc la première visite montre un formulaire vide.\n\n00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:20.439\nLes doubles accolades affichent une valeur, et Jinja l'échappe automatiquement,\n\n00:02:20.439 --> 00:02:22.584\nce qui nous protège de l'injection HTML.\n\n00:02:23.284 --> 00:02:26.734\nPour l'exécuter partout, on le met dans une image Docker.\n\n00:02:26.734 --> 00:02:30.911\nOn part d'une image Python slim, on copie le code, on installe Flask,\n\n00:02:30.911 --> 00:02:35.511\net on lance flask run, avec des variables d'environnement pour le fichier de\n\n00:02:35.511 --> 00:02:37.145\nl'appli, l'hôte et le port.\n\n00:02:37.145 --> 00:02:38.840\nLa ligne clé, c'est l'hôte :\n\n00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:39.324\n0.0.0.0.\n\n00:02:39.324 --> 00:02:43.985\nDans un conteneur, 127.0.0.1 n'accepte que les connexions venant du conteneur\n\n00:02:43.985 --> 00:02:47.556\nlui-même, donc le port publié n'atteindrait jamais l'appli.\n\n00:02:48.256 --> 00:02:52.044\nSoyons honnêtes sur les points faibles, car l'examinateur peut demander.\n\n00:02:52.044 --> 00:02:54.780\nD'abord, si on tape des lettres au lieu d'un nombre,\n\n00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:58.200\nfloat lève une ValueError et l'utilisateur reçoit une erreur 500.\n\n00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.619\nIl faudrait l'attraper et renvoyer une 400 avec un message clair.\n\n00:03:01.619 --> 00:03:05.197\nEnsuite, le volume du docker compose pointe vers le mauvais dossier,\n\n00:03:05.197 --> 00:03:06.407\ndonc il ne sert à rien.\n\n00:03:06.407 --> 00:03:10.248\nPuis un petit script du template cherche des éléments qui n'existent pas.\n\n00:03:10.248 --> 00:03:14.351\nEt les champs sont de type texte, donc leurs limites min et max sont ignorées.\n\n00:03:15.051 --> 00:03:17.589\nVoici comment corriger le premier en direct.\n\n00:03:17.589 --> 00:03:20.243\nOn entoure les deux conversions d'un bloc try.\n\n00:03:20.243 --> 00:03:24.742\nSi une ValueError survient, on rend le même template avec un message d'erreur,\n\n00:03:24.742 --> 00:03:26.126\net on renvoie un tuple :\n\n00:03:26.126 --> 00:03:29.299\nla page et le code 400, qui veut dire requête invalide.\n\n00:03:29.299 --> 00:03:32.240\nOn vérifie aussi que l'humidité est entre 0 et 100.\n\n00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:36.624\nDans le template, on affiche simplement la variable error quand elle existe.\n\n00:03:37.324 --> 00:03:38.642\nEntraînement à l'oral.\n\n00:03:38.642 --> 00:03:41.576\nPourquoi accepter GET et POST sur la même route ?\n\n00:03:41.576 --> 00:03:44.031\nParce que GET affiche le formulaire vide,\n\n00:03:44.031 --> 00:03:48.703\net POST envoie les données dans le corps de la requête et déclenche le calcul.\n\n00:03:48.703 --> 00:03:51.936\nDans la fonction, request.method indique lequel c'est.\n\n00:03:52.636 --> 00:03:53.744\nDeuxième question :\n\n00:03:53.744 --> 00:03:55.492\npourquoi 0.0.0.0 dans Docker ?\n\n00:03:55.492 --> 00:04:00.272\nParce que l'adresse de bouclage n'accepte que les connexions venant de l'intérieur\n\n00:04:00.272 --> 00:04:01.030\ndu conteneur.\n\n00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:04.644\nÉcouter sur toutes les interfaces permet au port publié, 5005,\n\n00:04:04.644 --> 00:04:07.033\nd'atteindre Flask depuis la machine hôte.\n\n00:04:07.733 --> 00:04:08.374\nPour résumer.\n\n00:04:08.374 --> 00:04:10.395\nUne route associe une URL à une fonction,\n\n00:04:10.395 --> 00:04:12.809\net il faut l'argument methods pour accepter POST.\n\n00:04:12.809 --> 00:04:16.308\nLes valeurs de formulaire et de query string sont toujours des chaînes,\n\n00:04:16.308 --> 00:04:18.230\ndonc on les convertit et on les valide.\n\n00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:22.123\nLes templates affichent les valeurs avec des doubles accolades et utilisent les\n\n00:04:22.123 --> 00:04:24.193\nbalises accolade-pourcent pour la logique.\n\n00:04:24.193 --> 00:04:26.509\nEt dans Docker, on écoute toujours sur 0.0.0.0.\n\n00:04:26.509 --> 00:04:29.220\nRendez-vous au TP2, où on connecte une base de données.\n"}, "tp1_fr": {"en": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.735\nWelcome to lab one.\n\n00:00:01.735 --> 00:00:05.387\nIn this video we build our first real Flask feature:\n\n00:00:05.387 --> 00:00:09.532\na page with a form that sends a temperature and a humidity,\n\n00:00:09.532 --> 00:00:14.028\nand displays the heat index, which is how hot it actually feels.\n\n00:00:14.028 --> 00:00:19.226\nAlong the way we cover routes, GET and POST, reading form data, templates,\n\n00:00:19.226 --> 00:00:19.998\nand Docker.\n\n00:00:20.698 --> 00:00:22.359\nHere is the whole journey.\n\n00:00:22.359 --> 00:00:24.340\nThe browser shows an HTML form.\n\n00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:28.876\nWhen you click the button, it sends a POST request to slash heat index.\n\n00:00:28.876 --> 00:00:33.732\nFlask calls our view function, which reads the two values from request.form,\n\n00:00:33.732 --> 00:00:36.159\npasses them to the heat index formula,\n\n00:00:36.159 --> 00:00:39.290\nand finally renders the template with the result.\n\n00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:41.015\nThe same URL does two jobs:\n\n00:00:41.015 --> 00:00:45.870\na GET request shows the empty form, and a POST request does the calculation.\n\n00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:48.245\nEverything starts with one line:\n\n00:00:48.245 --> 00:00:50.392\napp equals Flask, double underscore name.\n\n00:00:50.392 --> 00:00:52.276\nThis creates the application object.\n\n00:00:52.276 --> 00:00:55.103\nThe name argument tells Flask where our package lives,\n\n00:00:55.103 --> 00:00:58.715\nso it can find the templates folder and the static folder next to it.\n\n00:00:59.415 --> 00:01:02.821\nThe app.route decorator links a URL to a Python function.\n\n00:01:02.821 --> 00:01:06.166\nWhatever the function returns becomes the HTTP response.\n\n00:01:06.166 --> 00:01:09.871\nIn the second route, the part in angle brackets is a variable:\n\n00:01:09.871 --> 00:01:14.411\nif you open slash user slash Mateo, Flask passes Mateo as the name argument,\n\n00:01:14.411 --> 00:01:16.204\nand the page says Hello Mateo.\n\n00:01:16.904 --> 00:01:18.365\nNow the real feature.\n\n00:01:18.365 --> 00:01:21.565\nBy default, a route only accepts GET requests,\n\n00:01:21.565 --> 00:01:24.418\nso we explicitly allow both GET and POST.\n\n00:01:24.418 --> 00:01:29.079\nInside the function, request.method tells us which one we received.\n\n00:01:29.079 --> 00:01:32.975\nOn a GET, we skip the calculation and result stays None.\n\n00:01:32.975 --> 00:01:34.506\nOn a POST, we compute.\n\n00:01:35.206 --> 00:01:39.128\nrequest.form is a dictionary of everything the form sent,\n\n00:01:39.128 --> 00:01:42.017\nkeyed by the name attribute of each input.\n\n00:01:42.017 --> 00:01:42.705\nImportant:\n\n00:01:42.705 --> 00:01:46.627\nevery value is a string, even if the user typed a number.\n\n00:01:46.627 --> 00:01:48.967\nThat is why we wrap them in float.\n\n00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:53.301\nIf we forgot, the formula would try to multiply text and crash.\n\n00:01:54.001 --> 00:01:56.552\nThe formula itself is a plain Python function:\n\n00:01:56.552 --> 00:02:00.546\na polynomial of the temperature and the humidity with nine coefficients.\n\n00:02:00.546 --> 00:02:02.154\nIt knows nothing about Flask.\n\n00:02:02.154 --> 00:02:03.485\nThat separation matters:\n\n00:02:03.485 --> 00:02:07.091\nthe route deals with HTTP, and the function deals with the maths,\n\n00:02:07.091 --> 00:02:09.919\nso each one is easy to read and to test on its own.\n\n00:02:10.619 --> 00:02:13.673\nrender template fills an HTML file with Jinja.\n\n00:02:13.673 --> 00:02:18.985\nThe form posts to the same URL, and each input's name matches the key we read in\n\n00:02:18.985 --> 00:02:19.450\nPython.\n\n00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:24.097\nBelow the form, an if block only shows the result when it is not None,\n\n00:02:24.097 --> 00:02:26.687\nso the first visit shows an empty form.\n\n00:02:26.687 --> 00:02:31.335\nDouble curly braces print a value, and Jinja escapes it automatically,\n\n00:02:31.335 --> 00:02:33.858\nwhich protects us from HTML injection.\n\n00:02:34.558 --> 00:02:37.729\nTo run it anywhere, we put it in a Docker image.\n\n00:02:37.729 --> 00:02:41.957\nWe start from a slim Python image, copy the code, install Flask,\n\n00:02:41.957 --> 00:02:46.845\nand start it with flask run, using environment variables for the app file,\n\n00:02:46.845 --> 00:02:48.299\nthe host and the port.\n\n00:02:48.299 --> 00:02:49.950\nThe key line is the host:\n\n00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:51.272\nzero zero zero zero.\n\n00:02:51.272 --> 00:02:56.358\nInside a container, one two seven zero zero one only accepts connections from\n\n00:02:56.358 --> 00:03:01.511\ninside the container itself, so the port we publish would never reach the app.\n\n00:03:02.211 --> 00:03:06.641\nLet's be honest about the weak points, because the examiner may ask.\n\n00:03:06.641 --> 00:03:09.703\nFirst, if you type letters instead of a number,\n\n00:03:09.703 --> 00:03:13.351\nfloat raises a ValueError and the user gets a 500 error.\n\n00:03:13.351 --> 00:03:17.064\nWe should catch it and return a 400 with a clear message.\n\n00:03:17.064 --> 00:03:22.406\nSecond, the docker compose volume points to the wrong folder, so it has no effect.\n\n00:03:22.406 --> 00:03:27.227\nThird, a small script in the template looks for elements that don't exist.\n\n00:03:27.227 --> 00:03:31.917\nAnd the inputs are text fields, so their min and max limits are ignored.\n\n00:03:32.617 --> 00:03:34.906\nHere is how we fix the first one live.\n\n00:03:34.906 --> 00:03:37.497\nWe wrap the two conversions in a try block.\n\n00:03:37.497 --> 00:03:42.014\nIf a ValueError happens, we render the same template with an error message,\n\n00:03:42.014 --> 00:03:43.340\nand we return a tuple:\n\n00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:47.376\nthe page and the status code four hundred, which means bad request.\n\n00:03:47.376 --> 00:03:50.990\nWe also check that humidity is between zero and one hundred.\n\n00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:55.026\nIn the template, we just display the error variable when it exists.\n\n00:03:55.726 --> 00:03:56.542\nOral practice.\n\n00:03:56.542 --> 00:03:59.633\nWhy do we accept both GET and POST on the same route?\n\n00:03:59.633 --> 00:04:01.732\nBecause GET displays the empty form,\n\n00:04:01.732 --> 00:04:06.398\nand POST sends the data in the body of the request and triggers the calculation.\n\n00:04:06.398 --> 00:04:09.955\nInside the function, request.method tells us which one it is.\n\n00:04:10.655 --> 00:04:11.687\nSecond question:\n\n00:04:11.687 --> 00:04:13.880\nwhy zero zero zero zero in Docker?\n\n00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:19.041\nBecause the loopback address only accepts connections from inside the container.\n\n00:04:19.041 --> 00:04:24.008\nListening on all interfaces lets the port we publish, five thousand and five,\n\n00:04:24.008 --> 00:04:26.202\nreach Flask from the host machine.\n\n00:04:26.902 --> 00:04:27.664\nTo sum up.\n\n00:04:27.664 --> 00:04:33.996\nA route maps a URL to a function, and you need the methods argument to accept POST.\n\n00:04:33.996 --> 00:04:39.412\nForm and query values are always strings, so convert and validate them.\n\n00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:45.438\nTemplates print values with double braces and use curly percent tags for logic.\n\n00:04:45.438 --> 00:04:49.405\nAnd in Docker, always listen on zero zero zero zero.\n\n00:04:49.405 --> 00:04:53.066\nSee you in lab two, where we connect a database.\n", "fr": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.643\nBienvenue dans le TP1.\n\n00:00:01.643 --> 00:00:05.822\nDans cette vidéo, on construit notre première vraie fonctionnalité Flask :\n\n00:00:05.822 --> 00:00:09.832\nune page avec un formulaire qui envoie une température et une humidité,\n\n00:00:09.832 --> 00:00:14.012\net qui affiche l'indice de chaleur, c'est-à-dire la température ressentie.\n\n00:00:14.012 --> 00:00:18.643\nAu passage, on voit les routes, GET et POST, la lecture des données de formulaire,\n\n00:00:18.643 --> 00:00:19.998\nles templates et Docker.\n\n00:00:20.698 --> 00:00:22.100\nVoici tout le parcours.\n\n00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.599\nLe navigateur affiche un formulaire HTML.\n\n00:00:24.599 --> 00:00:29.170\nQuand on clique sur le bouton, il envoie une requête POST vers /heat_index.\n\n00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:34.046\nFlask appelle notre fonction de vue, qui lit les deux valeurs dans request.form,\n\n00:00:34.046 --> 00:00:36.850\nles passe à la formule de l'indice de chaleur,\n\n00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.227\npuis rend le template avec le résultat.\n\n00:00:39.227 --> 00:00:41.055\nLa même URL fait deux choses :\n\n00:00:41.055 --> 00:00:45.870\nune requête GET affiche le formulaire vide, et une requête POST fait le calcul.\n\n00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:48.247\nTout commence par une ligne :\n\n00:00:48.247 --> 00:00:49.520\napp = Flask(__name__).\n\n00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.255\nElle crée l'objet application.\n\n00:00:51.255 --> 00:00:54.840\nL'argument __name__ indique à Flask où se trouve notre paquet,\n\n00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.715\npour qu'il trouve le dossier templates et le dossier static à côté.\n\n00:00:59.415 --> 00:01:03.118\nLe décorateur app.route relie une URL à une fonction Python.\n\n00:01:03.118 --> 00:01:06.266\nCe que la fonction renvoie devient la réponse HTTP.\n\n00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:10.402\nDans la deuxième route, la partie entre chevrons est une variable :\n\n00:01:10.402 --> 00:01:14.290\nsi on ouvre /user/Mateo, Flask passe Mateo comme argument name,\n\n00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:16.204\net la page affiche Hello Mateo.\n\n00:01:16.904 --> 00:01:19.232\nPassons à la vraie fonctionnalité.\n\n00:01:19.232 --> 00:01:24.986\nPar défaut, une route n'accepte que GET, donc on autorise explicitement GET et POST.\n\n00:01:24.986 --> 00:01:29.164\nDans la fonction, request.method indique laquelle on a reçue.\n\n00:01:29.164 --> 00:01:32.862\nSur un GET, on saute le calcul et result reste à None.\n\n00:01:32.862 --> 00:01:34.506\nSur un POST, on calcule.\n\n00:01:35.206 --> 00:01:39.233\nrequest.form est un dictionnaire de tout ce que le formulaire a envoyé,\n\n00:01:39.233 --> 00:01:41.673\nindexé par l'attribut name de chaque champ.\n\n00:01:41.673 --> 00:01:42.297\nImportant :\n\n00:01:42.297 --> 00:01:46.608\ntoutes les valeurs sont des chaînes, même si l'utilisateur a tapé un nombre.\n\n00:01:46.608 --> 00:01:48.933\nC'est pour ça qu'on les passe dans float.\n\n00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:53.301\nSi on l'oubliait, la formule essaierait de multiplier du texte et planterait.\n\n00:01:54.001 --> 00:01:57.025\nLa formule elle-même est une simple fonction Python :\n\n00:01:57.025 --> 00:02:01.019\nun polynôme de la température et de l'humidité avec neuf coefficients.\n\n00:02:01.019 --> 00:02:02.559\nElle ne sait rien de Flask.\n\n00:02:02.559 --> 00:02:03.986\nCette séparation compte :\n\n00:02:03.986 --> 00:02:06.838\nla route gère le HTTP, la fonction gère les maths,\n\n00:02:06.838 --> 00:02:09.919\ndonc chacune est facile à lire et à tester séparément.\n\n00:02:10.619 --> 00:02:13.575\nrender_template remplit un fichier HTML avec Jinja.\n\n00:02:13.575 --> 00:02:15.719\nLe formulaire poste vers la même URL,\n\n00:02:15.719 --> 00:02:19.602\net le name de chaque champ correspond à la clé qu'on lit en Python.\n\n00:02:19.602 --> 00:02:24.064\nSous le formulaire, un bloc if n'affiche le résultat que s'il n'est pas None,\n\n00:02:24.064 --> 00:02:26.961\ndonc la première visite montre un formulaire vide.\n\n00:02:26.961 --> 00:02:31.540\nLes doubles accolades affichent une valeur, et Jinja l'échappe automatiquement,\n\n00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:33.858\nce qui nous protège de l'injection HTML.\n\n00:02:34.558 --> 00:02:38.389\nPour l'exécuter partout, on le met dans une image Docker.\n\n00:02:38.389 --> 00:02:43.027\nOn part d'une image Python slim, on copie le code, on installe Flask,\n\n00:02:43.027 --> 00:02:48.135\net on lance flask run, avec des variables d'environnement pour le fichier de\n\n00:02:48.135 --> 00:02:49.950\nl'appli, l'hôte et le port.\n\n00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:51.832\nLa ligne clé, c'est l'hôte :\n\n00:02:51.832 --> 00:02:52.370\n0.0.0.0.\n\n00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:57.546\nDans un conteneur, 127.0.0.1 n'accepte que les connexions venant du conteneur\n\n00:02:57.546 --> 00:03:01.511\nlui-même, donc le port publié n'atteindrait jamais l'appli.\n\n00:03:02.211 --> 00:03:06.523\nSoyons honnêtes sur les points faibles, car l'examinateur peut demander.\n\n00:03:06.523 --> 00:03:09.638\nD'abord, si on tape des lettres au lieu d'un nombre,\n\n00:03:09.638 --> 00:03:13.531\nfloat lève une ValueError et l'utilisateur reçoit une erreur 500.\n\n00:03:13.531 --> 00:03:17.424\nIl faudrait l'attraper et renvoyer une 400 avec un message clair.\n\n00:03:17.424 --> 00:03:21.496\nEnsuite, le volume du docker compose pointe vers le mauvais dossier,\n\n00:03:21.496 --> 00:03:22.874\ndonc il ne sert à rien.\n\n00:03:22.874 --> 00:03:27.246\nPuis un petit script du template cherche des éléments qui n'existent pas.\n\n00:03:27.246 --> 00:03:31.917\nEt les champs sont de type texte, donc leurs limites min et max sont ignorées.\n\n00:03:32.617 --> 00:03:35.254\nVoici comment corriger le premier en direct.\n\n00:03:35.254 --> 00:03:38.010\nOn entoure les deux conversions d'un bloc try.\n\n00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:42.683\nSi une ValueError survient, on rend le même template avec un message d'erreur,\n\n00:03:42.683 --> 00:03:44.121\net on renvoie un tuple :\n\n00:03:44.121 --> 00:03:47.417\nla page et le code 400, qui veut dire requête invalide.\n\n00:03:47.417 --> 00:03:50.472\nOn vérifie aussi que l'humidité est entre 0 et 100.\n\n00:03:50.472 --> 00:03:55.026\nDans le template, on affiche simplement la variable error quand elle existe.\n\n00:03:55.726 --> 00:03:57.009\nEntraînement à l'oral.\n\n00:03:57.009 --> 00:03:59.866\nPourquoi accepter GET et POST sur la même route ?\n\n00:03:59.866 --> 00:04:02.257\nParce que GET affiche le formulaire vide,\n\n00:04:02.257 --> 00:04:06.806\net POST envoie les données dans le corps de la requête et déclenche le calcul.\n\n00:04:06.806 --> 00:04:09.955\nDans la fonction, request.method indique lequel c'est.\n\n00:04:10.655 --> 00:04:11.851\nDeuxième question :\n\n00:04:11.851 --> 00:04:13.739\npourquoi 0.0.0.0 dans Docker ?\n\n00:04:13.739 --> 00:04:18.900\nParce que l'adresse de bouclage n'accepte que les connexions venant de l'intérieur\n\n00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:19.719\ndu conteneur.\n\n00:04:19.719 --> 00:04:23.621\nÉcouter sur toutes les interfaces permet au port publié, 5005,\n\n00:04:23.621 --> 00:04:26.202\nd'atteindre Flask depuis la machine hôte.\n\n00:04:26.902 --> 00:04:27.682\nPour résumer.\n\n00:04:27.682 --> 00:04:30.142\nUne route associe une URL à une fonction,\n\n00:04:30.142 --> 00:04:33.083\net il faut l'argument methods pour accepter POST.\n\n00:04:33.083 --> 00:04:37.343\nLes valeurs de formulaire et de query string sont toujours des chaînes,\n\n00:04:37.343 --> 00:04:39.684\ndonc on les convertit et on les valide.\n\n00:04:39.684 --> 00:04:44.425\nLes templates affichent les valeurs avec des doubles accolades et utilisent les\n\n00:04:44.425 --> 00:04:46.945\nbalises accolade-pourcent pour la logique.\n\n00:04:46.945 --> 00:04:49.766\nEt dans Docker, on écoute toujours sur 0.0.0.0.\n\n00:04:49.766 --> 00:04:53.066\nRendez-vous au TP2, où on connecte une base de données.\n"}, "tp2": {"en": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:00.919\nLab two.\n\n00:00:00.919 --> 00:00:06.235\nWe take the Chinook music database, a SQLite file with artists, albums and tracks,\n\n00:00:06.235 --> 00:00:10.060\nand we read it from Flask through an ORM, Flask SQLAlchemy.\n\n00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:14.403\nWe also organise the project properly, with an application factory,\n\n00:00:14.403 --> 00:00:17.061\na Blueprint and a separate configuration.\n\n00:00:17.761 --> 00:00:18.910\nFirst, the layout.\n\n00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:20.760\nConfig.py holds the settings.\n\n00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.419\nRun.py is the entry point.\n\n00:00:22.419 --> 00:00:26.567\nThe app package contains the factory, the models, and the routes,\n\n00:00:26.567 --> 00:00:28.546\nand there is a single template.\n\n00:00:28.546 --> 00:00:33.587\nEach file has one job, which makes the project easy to navigate and to explain.\n\n00:00:34.287 --> 00:00:36.542\nThe configuration is a small class.\n\n00:00:36.542 --> 00:00:40.667\nThe database path is built from the location of the file itself,\n\n00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:44.469\nso it works no matter which folder you launch the app from.\n\n00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:47.755\nThe URL starts with sqlite colon slash slash slash.\n\n00:00:47.755 --> 00:00:52.266\nIf we moved to PostgreSQL, this URL is the only thing we would change:\n\n00:00:52.266 --> 00:00:54.843\nthat is the portability an ORM gives us.\n\n00:00:55.543 --> 00:00:59.933\nThe db object is created once, at module level, without any application.\n\n00:00:59.933 --> 00:01:03.286\nThen create app builds the Flask app, loads the config,\n\n00:01:03.286 --> 00:01:05.298\nand binds db to it with init app.\n\n00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:07.005\nThis is the factory pattern:\n\n00:01:07.005 --> 00:01:11.578\nwe can build several apps, for example one for tests with another database.\n\n00:01:12.278 --> 00:01:15.256\nNotice the import of the Blueprint inside the function.\n\n00:01:15.256 --> 00:01:18.831\nRoutes.py imports the models, which import db from this very file.\n\n00:01:18.831 --> 00:01:22.839\nIf we imported routes at the top, Python would loop between the two files.\n\n00:01:22.839 --> 00:01:27.117\nImporting it inside the function, after db exists, avoids that circular import.\n\n00:01:27.117 --> 00:01:29.392\nThen we register the Blueprint on the app.\n\n00:01:30.092 --> 00:01:31.059\nNow the models.\n\n00:01:31.059 --> 00:01:32.800\nEach class maps to a table.\n\n00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.733\nBecause Chinook already exists, we set table name explicitly,\n\n00:01:36.733 --> 00:01:40.150\nand we use the real column names, Artist Id and Name.\n\n00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:43.890\nIn Album, Artist Id is a foreign key to the artists table:\n\n00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.469\nthat is the link stored in the database.\n\n00:01:47.169 --> 00:01:49.226\nThe foreign key lives in the database.\n\n00:01:49.226 --> 00:01:52.257\nThe relationship lines are the Python side of that link.\n\n00:01:52.257 --> 00:01:55.288\nThey let us write artist.albums to get a list of albums,\n\n00:01:55.288 --> 00:01:56.803\nand album.artist to go back.\n\n00:01:56.803 --> 00:01:58.968\nBack populates keeps both sides in sync.\n\n00:01:58.968 --> 00:02:00.592\nWe never write a join by hand.\n\n00:02:01.292 --> 00:02:02.576\nEach route is short.\n\n00:02:02.576 --> 00:02:07.906\nArtist.query.order by Name.all is the ORM version of select star from artists order\n\n00:02:07.906 --> 00:02:08.420\nby name.\n\n00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:11.374\nWe get a list of Artist objects, not raw rows,\n\n00:02:11.374 --> 00:02:15.677\nand pass it to the template with a mode telling it what to display.\n\n00:02:16.377 --> 00:02:19.236\nThe third page lists artists with their albums.\n\n00:02:19.236 --> 00:02:23.373\nDone naively, that is one query for the artists plus one per artist:\n\n00:02:23.373 --> 00:02:24.772\nthe N plus one problem.\n\n00:02:24.772 --> 00:02:28.786\nSelect in load fixes it by loading every album in one extra query,\n\n00:02:28.786 --> 00:02:30.672\nwith where artist id in a list.\n\n00:02:30.672 --> 00:02:34.139\nThe join keeps only artists that have at least one album,\n\n00:02:34.139 --> 00:02:37.363\nand distinct removes the duplicates the join creates.\n\n00:02:38.063 --> 00:02:39.988\nThe template switches on the mode.\n\n00:02:39.988 --> 00:02:43.725\nInside the artists with albums branch, we loop over artist.albums,\n\n00:02:43.725 --> 00:02:45.253\nthanks to the relationship.\n\n00:02:45.253 --> 00:02:49.556\nA Jinja for loop can have an else branch, which runs when the list is empty,\n\n00:02:49.556 --> 00:02:50.858\nhere to print No album.\n\n00:02:51.558 --> 00:02:52.911\nA likely live change:\n\n00:02:52.911 --> 00:02:54.587\nadd a page for one artist.\n\n00:02:54.587 --> 00:02:57.808\nThe int converter in the URL only matches numbers.\n\n00:02:57.808 --> 00:03:02.963\nGet or 404 fetches the artist by primary key, or returns a clean not found page.\n\n00:03:02.963 --> 00:03:06.829\nThen we reuse the same template in artists with albums mode,\n\n00:03:06.829 --> 00:03:08.504\nwith a list of one artist.\n\n00:03:09.204 --> 00:03:10.073\nOral practice.\n\n00:03:10.073 --> 00:03:11.686\nWhat does an ORM give you?\n\n00:03:11.686 --> 00:03:14.975\nYou manipulate Python classes instead of SQL strings,\n\n00:03:14.975 --> 00:03:18.884\nthe same code runs on several engines by changing only the URL,\n\n00:03:18.884 --> 00:03:21.118\nand it integrates nicely with Flask.\n\n00:03:21.118 --> 00:03:24.966\nThe price is a small overhead, around a millisecond per query.\n\n00:03:25.666 --> 00:03:27.447\nAnd how would you add an artist?\n\n00:03:27.447 --> 00:03:30.787\nCreate an Artist object, add it to the session, then commit.\n\n00:03:30.787 --> 00:03:35.407\nNothing is written until the commit, and rollback would cancel the pending changes.\n\n00:03:36.107 --> 00:03:36.892\nTo remember:\n\n00:03:36.892 --> 00:03:42.317\nthe factory and init app pattern, mapping onto an existing database with table name\n\n00:03:42.317 --> 00:03:47.742\nand real column names, the difference between the foreign key and the relationship,\n\n00:03:47.742 --> 00:03:51.011\nand select in load against the N plus one problem.\n\n00:03:51.011 --> 00:03:52.253\nWeak points to own:\n\n00:03:52.253 --> 00:03:57.743\nthe legacy query API, a route with spaces in its URL, and unpinned package versions.\n\n00:03:57.743 --> 00:03:58.789\nNext, lab three:\n\n00:03:58.789 --> 00:04:00.489\ntime series with InfluxDB.\n", "fr": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:00.646\nTP2.\n\n00:00:00.646 --> 00:00:05.011\nOn prend la base musicale Chinook, un fichier SQLite avec des artistes,\n\n00:00:05.011 --> 00:00:10.114\ndes albums et des morceaux, et on la lit depuis Flask via un ORM, Flask-SQLAlchemy.\n\n00:00:10.114 --> 00:00:14.479\nOn organise aussi proprement le projet, avec une factory d'application,\n\n00:00:14.479 --> 00:00:17.061\nun Blueprint et une configuration séparée.\n\n00:00:17.761 --> 00:00:19.228\nD'abord, l'arborescence.\n\n00:00:19.228 --> 00:00:21.183\nconfig.py contient les réglages.\n\n00:00:21.183 --> 00:00:22.955\nrun.py est le point d'entrée.\n\n00:00:22.955 --> 00:00:26.682\nLe paquet app contient la factory, les modèles et les routes,\n\n00:00:26.682 --> 00:00:28.332\net il y a un seul template.\n\n00:00:28.332 --> 00:00:32.976\nChaque fichier a un seul rôle, ce qui rend le projet facile à parcourir et à\n\n00:00:32.976 --> 00:00:33.587\nexpliquer.\n\n00:00:34.287 --> 00:00:36.865\nLa configuration est une petite classe.\n\n00:00:36.865 --> 00:00:42.219\nLe chemin de la base est construit à partir de l'emplacement du fichier lui-même,\n\n00:00:42.219 --> 00:00:46.317\ndonc ça marche quel que soit le dossier d'où on lance l'appli.\n\n00:00:46.317 --> 00:00:48.300\nL'URL commence par sqlite:///.\n\n00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:52.266\nSi on passait à PostgreSQL, c'est la seule chose à changer :\n\n00:00:52.266 --> 00:00:54.843\nc'est la portabilité qu'apporte un ORM.\n\n00:00:55.543 --> 00:00:59.827\nL'objet db est créé une seule fois, au niveau du module, sans application.\n\n00:00:59.827 --> 00:01:02.316\nEnsuite create_app construit l'appli Flask,\n\n00:01:02.316 --> 00:01:05.037\ncharge la config et y attache db avec init_app.\n\n00:01:05.037 --> 00:01:06.542\nC'est le pattern factory :\n\n00:01:06.542 --> 00:01:08.625\non peut construire plusieurs applis,\n\n00:01:08.625 --> 00:01:11.578\npar exemple une pour les tests avec une autre base.\n\n00:01:12.278 --> 00:01:15.394\nRemarquez l'import du Blueprint à l'intérieur de la fonction.\n\n00:01:15.394 --> 00:01:19.021\nroutes.py importe les modèles, qui importent db depuis ce même fichier.\n\n00:01:19.021 --> 00:01:23.313\nSi on importait routes en haut, Python tournerait en boucle entre les deux fichiers.\n\n00:01:23.313 --> 00:01:27.144\nL'importer dans la fonction, une fois db créé, évite cet import circulaire.\n\n00:01:27.144 --> 00:01:29.392\nPuis on enregistre le Blueprint sur l'appli.\n\n00:01:30.092 --> 00:01:31.428\nMaintenant les modèles.\n\n00:01:31.428 --> 00:01:33.577\nChaque classe correspond à une table.\n\n00:01:33.577 --> 00:01:37.235\nComme Chinook existe déjà, on fixe __tablename__ explicitement,\n\n00:01:37.235 --> 00:01:40.662\net on utilise les vrais noms de colonnes, ArtistId et Name.\n\n00:01:40.662 --> 00:01:44.495\nDans Album, ArtistId est une clé étrangère vers la table artists :\n\n00:01:44.495 --> 00:01:46.469\nc'est le lien stocké dans la base.\n\n00:01:47.169 --> 00:01:48.737\nLa clé étrangère vit dans la base.\n\n00:01:48.737 --> 00:01:51.274\nLes lignes relationship sont le côté Python de ce lien.\n\n00:01:51.274 --> 00:01:54.641\nElles permettent d'écrire artist.albums pour obtenir la liste des albums,\n\n00:01:54.641 --> 00:01:56.486\net album.artist pour revenir en arrière.\n\n00:01:56.486 --> 00:01:58.747\nback_populates garde les deux côtés synchronisés.\n\n00:01:58.747 --> 00:02:00.592\nOn n'écrit jamais de jointure à la main.\n\n00:02:01.292 --> 00:02:02.684\nChaque route est courte.\n\n00:02:02.684 --> 00:02:07.092\nArtist.query.order_by(Artist.Name).all() est la version ORM de SELECT * FROM\n\n00:02:07.092 --> 00:02:08.368\nartists ORDER BY Name.\n\n00:02:08.368 --> 00:02:11.849\nOn obtient une liste d'objets Artist, pas des lignes brutes,\n\n00:02:11.849 --> 00:02:15.677\net on la passe au template avec un mode qui lui dit quoi afficher.\n\n00:02:16.377 --> 00:02:19.367\nLa troisième page liste les artistes avec leurs albums.\n\n00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:23.391\nFait naïvement, c'est une requête pour les artistes plus une par artiste :\n\n00:02:23.391 --> 00:02:24.261\nle problème N+1.\n\n00:02:24.261 --> 00:02:28.229\nselectinload le corrige en chargeant tous les albums en une seule requête\n\n00:02:28.229 --> 00:02:30.893\nsupplémentaire, avec WHERE ArtistId IN une liste.\n\n00:02:30.893 --> 00:02:34.373\nLa jointure ne garde que les artistes qui ont au moins un album,\n\n00:02:34.373 --> 00:02:37.363\net distinct supprime les doublons que crée la jointure.\n\n00:02:38.063 --> 00:02:39.883\nLe template change selon le mode.\n\n00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:43.523\nDans la branche artistes avec albums, on boucle sur artist.albums,\n\n00:02:43.523 --> 00:02:44.626\ngrâce à la relation.\n\n00:02:44.626 --> 00:02:49.204\nUne boucle for Jinja peut avoir une branche else, exécutée quand la liste est vide,\n\n00:02:49.204 --> 00:02:50.858\nici pour afficher Aucun album.\n\n00:02:51.558 --> 00:02:53.524\nUne modification probable en direct :\n\n00:02:53.524 --> 00:02:55.277\najouter une page pour un artiste.\n\n00:02:55.277 --> 00:02:58.358\nLe convertisseur int dans l'URL n'accepte que des nombres.\n\n00:02:58.358 --> 00:03:01.014\nget_or_404 récupère l'artiste par sa clé primaire,\n\n00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:03.192\nou renvoie une page « not found » propre.\n\n00:03:03.192 --> 00:03:06.751\nEnsuite on réutilise le même template en mode artistes avec albums,\n\n00:03:06.751 --> 00:03:08.504\navec une liste d'un seul artiste.\n\n00:03:09.204 --> 00:03:10.513\nEntraînement à l'oral.\n\n00:03:10.513 --> 00:03:11.643\nQu'apporte un ORM ?\n\n00:03:11.643 --> 00:03:14.854\nOn manipule des classes Python au lieu de chaînes SQL,\n\n00:03:14.854 --> 00:03:19.077\nle même code tourne sur plusieurs moteurs en changeant seulement l'URL,\n\n00:03:19.077 --> 00:03:20.802\net il s'intègre bien à Flask.\n\n00:03:20.802 --> 00:03:24.966\nLe prix, c'est un léger surcoût, environ une milliseconde par requête.\n\n00:03:25.666 --> 00:03:27.289\nEt comment ajouter un artiste ?\n\n00:03:27.289 --> 00:03:31.008\nOn crée un objet Artist, on l'ajoute à la session, puis on fait commit.\n\n00:03:31.008 --> 00:03:35.407\nRien n'est écrit avant le commit, et rollback annulerait les changements en attente.\n\n00:03:36.107 --> 00:03:36.793\nÀ retenir :\n\n00:03:36.793 --> 00:03:41.158\nle pattern factory et init_app, le mapping sur une base existante avec\n\n00:03:41.158 --> 00:03:43.902\n__tablename__ et les vrais noms de colonnes,\n\n00:03:43.902 --> 00:03:47.020\nla différence entre clé étrangère et relationship,\n\n00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:49.452\net selectinload contre le problème N+1.\n\n00:03:49.452 --> 00:03:51.073\nPoints faibles à assumer :\n\n00:03:51.073 --> 00:03:54.939\nl'ancienne API query, une route avec des espaces dans son URL,\n\n00:03:54.939 --> 00:03:57.309\net des versions de paquets non figées.\n\n00:03:57.309 --> 00:03:58.182\nEnsuite, TP3 :\n\n00:03:58.182 --> 00:04:00.489\nles séries temporelles avec InfluxDB.\n"}, "tp2_fr": {"en": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:00.921\nLab two.\n\n00:00:00.921 --> 00:00:06.263\nWe take the Chinook music database, a SQLite file with artists, albums and tracks,\n\n00:00:06.263 --> 00:00:10.107\nand we read it from Flask through an ORM, Flask SQLAlchemy.\n\n00:00:10.107 --> 00:00:14.471\nWe also organise the project properly, with an application factory,\n\n00:00:14.471 --> 00:00:17.142\na Blueprint and a separate configuration.\n\n00:00:17.842 --> 00:00:18.971\nFirst, the layout.\n\n00:00:18.971 --> 00:00:20.789\nConfig.py holds the settings.\n\n00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:22.418\nRun.py is the entry point.\n\n00:00:22.418 --> 00:00:26.493\nThe app package contains the factory, the models, and the routes,\n\n00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:28.436\nand there is a single template.\n\n00:00:28.436 --> 00:00:33.389\nEach file has one job, which makes the project easy to navigate and to explain.\n\n00:00:34.089 --> 00:00:36.037\nThe configuration is a small class.\n\n00:00:36.037 --> 00:00:39.600\nThe database path is built from the location of the file itself,\n\n00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.885\nso it works no matter which folder you launch the app from.\n\n00:00:42.885 --> 00:00:45.724\nThe URL starts with sqlite colon slash slash slash.\n\n00:00:45.724 --> 00:00:49.621\nIf we moved to PostgreSQL, this URL is the only thing we would change:\n\n00:00:49.621 --> 00:00:51.847\nthat is the portability an ORM gives us.\n\n00:00:52.547 --> 00:00:57.188\nThe db object is created once, at module level, without any application.\n\n00:00:57.188 --> 00:01:00.733\nThen create app builds the Flask app, loads the config,\n\n00:01:00.733 --> 00:01:02.860\nand binds db to it with init app.\n\n00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:04.665\nThis is the factory pattern:\n\n00:01:04.665 --> 00:01:09.499\nwe can build several apps, for example one for tests with another database.\n\n00:01:10.199 --> 00:01:13.437\nNotice the import of the Blueprint inside the function.\n\n00:01:13.437 --> 00:01:17.323\nRoutes.py imports the models, which import db from this very file.\n\n00:01:17.323 --> 00:01:21.680\nIf we imported routes at the top, Python would loop between the two files.\n\n00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.332\nImporting it inside the function, after db exists, avoids that circular import.\n\n00:01:26.332 --> 00:01:28.805\nThen we register the Blueprint on the app.\n\n00:01:29.505 --> 00:01:30.559\nNow the models.\n\n00:01:30.559 --> 00:01:32.457\nEach class maps to a table.\n\n00:01:32.457 --> 00:01:36.744\nBecause Chinook already exists, we set table name explicitly,\n\n00:01:36.744 --> 00:01:40.469\nand we use the real column names, Artist Id and Name.\n\n00:01:40.469 --> 00:01:44.545\nIn Album, Artist Id is a foreign key to the artists table:\n\n00:01:44.545 --> 00:01:47.356\nthat is the link stored in the database.\n\n00:01:48.056 --> 00:01:50.729\nThe foreign key lives in the database.\n\n00:01:50.729 --> 00:01:54.668\nThe relationship lines are the Python side of that link.\n\n00:01:54.668 --> 00:01:58.608\nThey let us write artist.albums to get a list of albums,\n\n00:01:58.608 --> 00:02:00.577\nand album.artist to go back.\n\n00:02:00.577 --> 00:02:03.391\nBack populates keeps both sides in sync.\n\n00:02:03.391 --> 00:02:05.501\nWe never write a join by hand.\n\n00:02:06.201 --> 00:02:07.648\nEach route is short.\n\n00:02:07.648 --> 00:02:13.654\nArtist.query.order by Name.all is the ORM version of select star from artists order\n\n00:02:13.654 --> 00:02:14.233\nby name.\n\n00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:17.561\nWe get a list of Artist objects, not raw rows,\n\n00:02:17.561 --> 00:02:22.409\nand pass it to the template with a mode telling it what to display.\n\n00:02:23.109 --> 00:02:26.034\nThe third page lists artists with their albums.\n\n00:02:26.034 --> 00:02:30.265\nDone naively, that is one query for the artists plus one per artist:\n\n00:02:30.265 --> 00:02:31.697\nthe N plus one problem.\n\n00:02:31.697 --> 00:02:35.803\nSelect in load fixes it by loading every album in one extra query,\n\n00:02:35.803 --> 00:02:37.732\nwith where artist id in a list.\n\n00:02:37.732 --> 00:02:41.279\nThe join keeps only artists that have at least one album,\n\n00:02:41.279 --> 00:02:44.577\nand distinct removes the duplicates the join creates.\n\n00:02:45.277 --> 00:02:47.530\nThe template switches on the mode.\n\n00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:51.904\nInside the artists with albums branch, we loop over artist.albums,\n\n00:02:51.904 --> 00:02:53.694\nthanks to the relationship.\n\n00:02:53.694 --> 00:02:58.730\nA Jinja for loop can have an else branch, which runs when the list is empty,\n\n00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:00.255\nhere to print No album.\n\n00:03:00.955 --> 00:03:02.663\nA likely live change:\n\n00:03:02.663 --> 00:03:04.779\nadd a page for one artist.\n\n00:03:04.779 --> 00:03:08.847\nThe int converter in the URL only matches numbers.\n\n00:03:08.847 --> 00:03:15.356\nGet or 404 fetches the artist by primary key, or returns a clean not found page.\n\n00:03:15.356 --> 00:03:20.237\nThen we reuse the same template in artists with albums mode,\n\n00:03:20.237 --> 00:03:22.353\nwith a list of one artist.\n\n00:03:23.053 --> 00:03:23.909\nOral practice.\n\n00:03:23.909 --> 00:03:25.500\nWhat does an ORM give you?\n\n00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.743\nYou manipulate Python classes instead of SQL strings,\n\n00:03:28.743 --> 00:03:32.597\nthe same code runs on several engines by changing only the URL,\n\n00:03:32.597 --> 00:03:34.800\nand it integrates nicely with Flask.\n\n00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.594\nThe price is a small overhead, around a millisecond per query.\n\n00:03:39.294 --> 00:03:41.207\nAnd how would you add an artist?\n\n00:03:41.207 --> 00:03:44.793\nCreate an Artist object, add it to the session, then commit.\n\n00:03:44.793 --> 00:03:49.755\nNothing is written until the commit, and rollback would cancel the pending changes.\n\n00:03:50.455 --> 00:03:51.202\nTo remember:\n\n00:03:51.202 --> 00:03:56.372\nthe factory and init app pattern, mapping onto an existing database with table name\n\n00:03:56.372 --> 00:04:01.542\nand real column names, the difference between the foreign key and the relationship,\n\n00:04:01.542 --> 00:04:04.656\nand select in load against the N plus one problem.\n\n00:04:04.656 --> 00:04:05.839\nWeak points to own:\n\n00:04:05.839 --> 00:04:11.071\nthe legacy query API, a route with spaces in its URL, and unpinned package versions.\n\n00:04:11.071 --> 00:04:12.068\nNext, lab three:\n\n00:04:12.068 --> 00:04:13.687\ntime series with InfluxDB.\n", "fr": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:00.647\nTP2.\n\n00:00:00.647 --> 00:00:05.033\nOn prend la base musicale Chinook, un fichier SQLite avec des artistes,\n\n00:00:05.033 --> 00:00:10.161\ndes albums et des morceaux, et on la lit depuis Flask via un ORM, Flask-SQLAlchemy.\n\n00:00:10.161 --> 00:00:14.548\nOn organise aussi proprement le projet, avec une factory d'application,\n\n00:00:14.548 --> 00:00:17.142\nun Blueprint et une configuration séparée.\n\n00:00:17.842 --> 00:00:19.283\nD'abord, l'arborescence.\n\n00:00:19.283 --> 00:00:21.204\nconfig.py contient les réglages.\n\n00:00:21.204 --> 00:00:22.944\nrun.py est le point d'entrée.\n\n00:00:22.944 --> 00:00:26.606\nLe paquet app contient la factory, les modèles et les routes,\n\n00:00:26.606 --> 00:00:28.227\net il y a un seul template.\n\n00:00:28.227 --> 00:00:32.789\nChaque fichier a un seul rôle, ce qui rend le projet facile à parcourir et à\n\n00:00:32.789 --> 00:00:33.389\nexpliquer.\n\n00:00:34.089 --> 00:00:36.316\nLa configuration est une petite classe.\n\n00:00:36.316 --> 00:00:40.941\nLe chemin de la base est construit à partir de l'emplacement du fichier lui-même,\n\n00:00:40.941 --> 00:00:44.481\ndonc ça marche quel que soit le dossier d'où on lance l'appli.\n\n00:00:44.481 --> 00:00:46.194\nL'URL commence par sqlite:///.\n\n00:00:46.194 --> 00:00:49.620\nSi on passait à PostgreSQL, c'est la seule chose à changer :\n\n00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.847\nc'est la portabilité qu'apporte un ORM.\n\n00:00:52.547 --> 00:00:57.076\nL'objet db est créé une seule fois, au niveau du module, sans application.\n\n00:00:57.076 --> 00:00:59.707\nEnsuite create_app construit l'appli Flask,\n\n00:00:59.707 --> 00:01:02.583\ncharge la config et y attache db avec init_app.\n\n00:01:02.583 --> 00:01:04.175\nC'est le pattern factory :\n\n00:01:04.175 --> 00:01:06.378\non peut construire plusieurs applis,\n\n00:01:06.378 --> 00:01:09.499\npar exemple une pour les tests avec une autre base.\n\n00:01:10.199 --> 00:01:13.587\nRemarquez l'import du Blueprint à l'intérieur de la fonction.\n\n00:01:13.587 --> 00:01:17.530\nroutes.py importe les modèles, qui importent db depuis ce même fichier.\n\n00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:22.195\nSi on importait routes en haut, Python tournerait en boucle entre les deux fichiers.\n\n00:01:22.195 --> 00:01:26.361\nL'importer dans la fonction, une fois db créé, évite cet import circulaire.\n\n00:01:26.361 --> 00:01:28.805\nPuis on enregistre le Blueprint sur l'appli.\n\n00:01:29.505 --> 00:01:30.961\nMaintenant les modèles.\n\n00:01:30.961 --> 00:01:33.303\nChaque classe correspond à une table.\n\n00:01:33.303 --> 00:01:37.291\nComme Chinook existe déjà, on fixe __tablename__ explicitement,\n\n00:01:37.291 --> 00:01:41.026\net on utilise les vrais noms de colonnes, ArtistId et Name.\n\n00:01:41.026 --> 00:01:45.204\nDans Album, ArtistId est une clé étrangère vers la table artists :\n\n00:01:45.204 --> 00:01:47.356\nc'est le lien stocké dans la base.\n\n00:01:48.056 --> 00:01:50.094\nLa clé étrangère vit dans la base.\n\n00:01:50.094 --> 00:01:53.392\nLes lignes relationship sont le côté Python de ce lien.\n\n00:01:53.392 --> 00:01:57.768\nElles permettent d'écrire artist.albums pour obtenir la liste des albums,\n\n00:01:57.768 --> 00:02:00.166\net album.artist pour revenir en arrière.\n\n00:02:00.166 --> 00:02:03.103\nback_populates garde les deux côtés synchronisés.\n\n00:02:03.103 --> 00:02:05.501\nOn n'écrit jamais de jointure à la main.\n\n00:02:06.201 --> 00:02:07.770\nChaque route est courte.\n\n00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:12.737\nArtist.query.order_by(Artist.Name).all() est la version ORM de SELECT * FROM\n\n00:02:12.737 --> 00:02:14.175\nartists ORDER BY Name.\n\n00:02:14.175 --> 00:02:18.096\nOn obtient une liste d'objets Artist, pas des lignes brutes,\n\n00:02:18.096 --> 00:02:22.409\net on la passe au template avec un mode qui lui dit quoi afficher.\n\n00:02:23.109 --> 00:02:26.168\nLa troisième page liste les artistes avec leurs albums.\n\n00:02:26.168 --> 00:02:30.284\nFait naïvement, c'est une requête pour les artistes plus une par artiste :\n\n00:02:30.284 --> 00:02:31.174\nle problème N+1.\n\n00:02:31.174 --> 00:02:35.234\nselectinload le corrige en chargeant tous les albums en une seule requête\n\n00:02:35.234 --> 00:02:37.959\nsupplémentaire, avec WHERE ArtistId IN une liste.\n\n00:02:37.959 --> 00:02:41.518\nLa jointure ne garde que les artistes qui ont au moins un album,\n\n00:02:41.518 --> 00:02:44.577\net distinct supprime les doublons que crée la jointure.\n\n00:02:45.277 --> 00:02:47.407\nLe template change selon le mode.\n\n00:02:47.407 --> 00:02:51.668\nDans la branche artistes avec albums, on boucle sur artist.albums,\n\n00:02:51.668 --> 00:02:52.960\ngrâce à la relation.\n\n00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:58.318\nUne boucle for Jinja peut avoir une branche else, exécutée quand la liste est vide,\n\n00:02:58.318 --> 00:03:00.255\nici pour afficher Aucun album.\n\n00:03:00.955 --> 00:03:03.437\nUne modification probable en direct :\n\n00:03:03.437 --> 00:03:05.650\najouter une page pour un artiste.\n\n00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:09.541\nLe convertisseur int dans l'URL n'accepte que des nombres.\n\n00:03:09.541 --> 00:03:12.895\nget_or_404 récupère l'artiste par sa clé primaire,\n\n00:03:12.895 --> 00:03:15.645\nou renvoie une page « not found » propre.\n\n00:03:15.645 --> 00:03:20.139\nEnsuite on réutilise le même template en mode artistes avec albums,\n\n00:03:20.139 --> 00:03:22.353\navec une liste d'un seul artiste.\n\n00:03:23.053 --> 00:03:24.343\nEntraînement à l'oral.\n\n00:03:24.343 --> 00:03:25.457\nQu'apporte un ORM ?\n\n00:03:25.457 --> 00:03:28.624\nOn manipule des classes Python au lieu de chaînes SQL,\n\n00:03:28.624 --> 00:03:32.788\nle même code tourne sur plusieurs moteurs en changeant seulement l'URL,\n\n00:03:32.788 --> 00:03:34.488\net il s'intègre bien à Flask.\n\n00:03:34.488 --> 00:03:38.594\nLe prix, c'est un léger surcoût, environ une milliseconde par requête.\n\n00:03:39.294 --> 00:03:41.037\nEt comment ajouter un artiste ?\n\n00:03:41.037 --> 00:03:45.030\nOn crée un objet Artist, on l'ajoute à la session, puis on fait commit.\n\n00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:49.755\nRien n'est écrit avant le commit, et rollback annulerait les changements en attente.\n\n00:03:50.455 --> 00:03:51.109\nÀ retenir :\n\n00:03:51.109 --> 00:03:55.268\nle pattern factory et init_app, le mapping sur une base existante avec\n\n00:03:55.268 --> 00:03:57.882\n__tablename__ et les vrais noms de colonnes,\n\n00:03:57.882 --> 00:04:00.853\nla différence entre clé étrangère et relationship,\n\n00:04:00.853 --> 00:04:03.170\net selectinload contre le problème N+1.\n\n00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:04.715\nPoints faibles à assumer :\n\n00:04:04.715 --> 00:04:08.399\nl'ancienne API query, une route avec des espaces dans son URL,\n\n00:04:08.399 --> 00:04:10.657\net des versions de paquets non figées.\n\n00:04:10.657 --> 00:04:11.489\nEnsuite, TP3 :\n\n00:04:11.489 --> 00:04:13.687\nles séries temporelles avec InfluxDB.\n"}, "tp3": {"en": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:00.989\nLab three.\n\n00:00:00.989 --> 00:00:04.937\nWe leave relational databases for a time series database, InfluxDB.\n\n00:00:04.937 --> 00:00:09.003\nThe IUT server collects weather, currency, crypto and system metrics.\n\n00:00:09.003 --> 00:00:13.777\nOur Flask app queries it in the Flux language and draws the curves with Chart.js,\n\n00:00:13.777 --> 00:00:14.896\na bit like Grafana.\n\n00:00:15.596 --> 00:00:16.909\nFirst, the vocabulary.\n\n00:00:16.909 --> 00:00:19.712\nA bucket is a database with a retention period.\n\n00:00:19.712 --> 00:00:21.502\nA measurement is like a table.\n\n00:00:21.502 --> 00:00:24.962\nTags are indexed labels, like the city, that we filter on.\n\n00:00:24.962 --> 00:00:29.317\nFields are the measured values, like the temperature, that we compute on.\n\n00:00:29.317 --> 00:00:31.405\nEvery point has a time and a value.\n\n00:00:32.105 --> 00:00:33.184\nHere is the flow.\n\n00:00:33.184 --> 00:00:35.088\nThe filters come from the URL.\n\n00:00:35.088 --> 00:00:39.023\nFlask builds a Flux query string, sends it with the query API,\n\n00:00:39.023 --> 00:00:43.466\nreceives tables of records, groups them into series with one function,\n\n00:00:43.466 --> 00:00:47.210\nand passes them to the template, where Chart.js draws them.\n\n00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:51.114\nTo connect we need a URL, an organisation and a token.\n\n00:00:51.114 --> 00:00:54.200\nThey come from environment variables with os.getenv,\n\n00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.751\nso no secret is written in the Python code.\n\n00:00:56.751 --> 00:00:59.599\nGet client returns None if the token is missing,\n\n00:00:59.599 --> 00:01:02.981\nso the page can show a clear message instead of crashing.\n\n00:01:03.681 --> 00:01:05.384\nA Flux query is a pipeline.\n\n00:01:05.384 --> 00:01:09.232\nFrom a bucket, pipe forward a time range, which is mandatory:\n\n00:01:09.232 --> 00:01:11.503\nInfluxDB always reads a time window.\n\n00:01:11.503 --> 00:01:15.730\nThen filters on the measurement, on the city tag, and on the field.\n\n00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:19.199\nFinally aggregate window keeps one mean value per hour,\n\n00:01:19.199 --> 00:01:22.101\nwhich means far fewer points to send and plot.\n\n00:01:22.801 --> 00:01:25.846\nQuery flux sends the query and flattens the answer.\n\n00:01:25.846 --> 00:01:28.891\nInfluxDB returns several tables, each with records,\n\n00:01:28.891 --> 00:01:32.592\nso we loop twice and turn each record into a plain dictionary.\n\n00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:34.324\nThe function returns a tuple:\n\n00:01:34.324 --> 00:01:36.175\nok, a message, and the records.\n\n00:01:36.175 --> 00:01:40.593\nIf anything fails, we return the error message so the page can display it.\n\n00:01:40.593 --> 00:01:43.817\nAnd the finally block closes the client in every case.\n\n00:01:44.517 --> 00:01:47.601\nGroup time series turns the flat list into chart series.\n\n00:01:47.601 --> 00:01:51.291\nIt skips non numeric values, names each series from chosen columns,\n\n00:01:51.291 --> 00:01:54.761\nlike the field or the city, and stores each value by timestamp.\n\n00:01:54.761 --> 00:01:58.011\nThen it builds one sorted time axis shared by every series.\n\n00:01:58.011 --> 00:02:00.875\nIf a series has no value at some time, it gets None,\n\n00:02:00.875 --> 00:02:02.693\nand Chart.js simply leaves a gap.\n\n00:02:03.393 --> 00:02:05.479\nThe dropdowns are not hard coded.\n\n00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:09.083\nSchema.tag values asks InfluxDB for every value of a tag,\n\n00:02:09.083 --> 00:02:12.117\nfor example all locations in the weather bucket.\n\n00:02:12.117 --> 00:02:17.175\nSo if a new city is added to the database, it appears in our form automatically.\n\n00:02:17.875 --> 00:02:21.966\nThe query page also prepares a small dataset for machine learning.\n\n00:02:21.966 --> 00:02:25.189\nIt slides a window of three values along the series:\n\n00:02:25.189 --> 00:02:29.280\nthe three values are the inputs, and the next value is the target.\n\n00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.061\nThis is exactly the format we use for forecasting in TP3 bis.\n\n00:02:33.761 --> 00:02:35.567\nWeak points, and they matter.\n\n00:02:35.567 --> 00:02:40.050\nThe city comes from the URL and is pasted directly into the Flux string,\n\n00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:43.474\nso a value with quotes could break or hijack the query:\n\n00:02:43.474 --> 00:02:44.782\nthat is an injection.\n\n00:02:44.782 --> 00:02:47.272\nA new client is created for every query,\n\n00:02:47.272 --> 00:02:52.004\nand the five dashboard queries run one after the other, so the page is slow.\n\n00:02:52.004 --> 00:02:55.179\nAnd the token sits in plain text in docker compose.\n\n00:02:55.179 --> 00:02:56.611\nTP3 bis fixes all four.\n\n00:02:57.311 --> 00:02:58.234\nOral practice.\n\n00:02:58.234 --> 00:03:01.001\nWhy a time series database instead of SQL?\n\n00:03:01.001 --> 00:03:05.744\nBecause sensor data arrives continuously, timestamped, in large volumes.\n\n00:03:05.744 --> 00:03:10.158\nInfluxDB is built for fast writes and for queries over time ranges,\n\n00:03:10.158 --> 00:03:13.386\nwith aggregations like aggregate window built in.\n\n00:03:14.086 --> 00:03:16.855\nWhat's the difference between a tag and a field?\n\n00:03:16.855 --> 00:03:21.124\nA tag is an indexed label, like the location, that we filter and group on.\n\n00:03:21.124 --> 00:03:24.123\nA field is the measured value, like the temperature,\n\n00:03:24.123 --> 00:03:26.719\nwhich we compute on and which is not indexed.\n\n00:03:27.419 --> 00:03:28.172\nTo remember:\n\n00:03:28.172 --> 00:03:33.256\nthe InfluxDB vocabulary, a Flux query as a pipeline starting with from and range,\n\n00:03:33.256 --> 00:03:36.206\nour query function that never crashes the page,\n\n00:03:36.206 --> 00:03:38.905\nand secrets that come from the environment.\n\n00:03:38.905 --> 00:03:39.595\nNext video:\n\n00:03:39.595 --> 00:03:43.423\nTP3 bis, where we clean this up and forecast the next values.\n", "fr": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:00.611\nTP3.\n\n00:00:00.611 --> 00:00:04.881\nOn quitte les bases relationnelles pour une base de séries temporelles, InfluxDB.\n\n00:00:04.881 --> 00:00:07.569\nLe serveur de l'IUT collecte la météo, les devises,\n\n00:00:07.569 --> 00:00:09.519\nles cryptos et des métriques système.\n\n00:00:09.519 --> 00:00:13.789\nNotre appli Flask l'interroge en langage Flux et trace les courbes avec Chart.js,\n\n00:00:13.789 --> 00:00:14.896\nun peu comme Grafana.\n\n00:00:15.596 --> 00:00:16.804\nD'abord, le vocabulaire.\n\n00:00:16.804 --> 00:00:19.372\nUn bucket est une base avec une durée de rétention.\n\n00:00:19.372 --> 00:00:21.185\nUne measurement est comme une table.\n\n00:00:21.185 --> 00:00:25.212\nLes tags sont des étiquettes indexées, comme la ville, sur lesquelles on filtre.\n\n00:00:25.212 --> 00:00:28.183\nLes fields sont les valeurs mesurées, comme la température,\n\n00:00:28.183 --> 00:00:29.492\nsur lesquelles on calcule.\n\n00:00:29.492 --> 00:00:31.405\nChaque point a un temps et une valeur.\n\n00:00:32.105 --> 00:00:33.013\nVoici le flux.\n\n00:00:33.013 --> 00:00:34.957\nLes filtres viennent de l'URL.\n\n00:00:34.957 --> 00:00:38.912\nFlask construit une requête Flux, l'envoie avec la query API,\n\n00:00:38.912 --> 00:00:43.969\nreçoit des tables d'enregistrements, les regroupe en séries avec une fonction,\n\n00:00:43.969 --> 00:00:47.210\net les passe au template, où Chart.js les dessine.\n\n00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:51.459\nPour se connecter, il faut une URL, une organisation et un token.\n\n00:00:51.459 --> 00:00:54.572\nIls viennent de variables d'environnement avec os.getenv,\n\n00:00:54.572 --> 00:00:57.302\ndonc aucun secret n'est écrit dans le code Python.\n\n00:00:57.302 --> 00:00:59.650\nget_client renvoie None si le token manque,\n\n00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:02.981\npour que la page affiche un message clair au lieu de planter.\n\n00:01:03.681 --> 00:01:05.617\nUne requête Flux est un pipeline.\n\n00:01:05.617 --> 00:01:09.312\nDepuis un bucket, on enchaîne une plage de temps, obligatoire :\n\n00:01:09.312 --> 00:01:11.835\nInfluxDB lit toujours une fenêtre de temps.\n\n00:01:11.835 --> 00:01:15.941\nPuis des filtres sur la measurement, sur le tag ville et sur le field.\n\n00:01:15.941 --> 00:01:18.875\nEnfin aggregateWindow garde une moyenne par heure,\n\n00:01:18.875 --> 00:01:22.101\nce qui fait bien moins de points à envoyer et à tracer.\n\n00:01:22.801 --> 00:01:25.578\nquery_flux envoie la requête et aplatit la réponse.\n\n00:01:25.578 --> 00:01:28.844\nInfluxDB renvoie plusieurs tables, chacune avec des records,\n\n00:01:28.844 --> 00:01:33.145\ndonc on boucle deux fois et on transforme chaque record en simple dictionnaire.\n\n00:01:33.145 --> 00:01:34.779\nLa fonction renvoie un tuple :\n\n00:01:34.779 --> 00:01:36.412\nok, un message et les records.\n\n00:01:36.412 --> 00:01:40.931\nSi quelque chose échoue, on renvoie le message d'erreur pour que la page l'affiche.\n\n00:01:40.931 --> 00:01:43.817\nEt le bloc finally ferme le client dans tous les cas.\n\n00:01:44.517 --> 00:01:47.805\ngroup_time_series transforme la liste plate en séries pour le graphique.\n\n00:01:47.805 --> 00:01:49.586\nElle ignore les valeurs non numériques,\n\n00:01:49.586 --> 00:01:53.102\nnomme chaque série à partir de colonnes choisies, comme le field ou la ville,\n\n00:01:53.102 --> 00:01:54.838\net range chaque valeur par horodatage.\n\n00:01:54.838 --> 00:01:58.126\nPuis elle construit un axe du temps trié, partagé par toutes les séries.\n\n00:01:58.126 --> 00:02:00.958\nSi une série n'a pas de valeur à un instant, elle reçoit None,\n\n00:02:00.958 --> 00:02:02.693\net Chart.js laisse simplement un trou.\n\n00:02:03.393 --> 00:02:06.041\nLes listes déroulantes ne sont pas codées en dur.\n\n00:02:06.041 --> 00:02:09.500\nschema.tagValues demande à InfluxDB toutes les valeurs d'un tag,\n\n00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.986\npar exemple toutes les villes du bucket météo.\n\n00:02:11.986 --> 00:02:14.527\nDonc si une nouvelle ville est ajoutée en base,\n\n00:02:14.527 --> 00:02:17.175\nelle apparaît automatiquement dans le formulaire.\n\n00:02:17.875 --> 00:02:22.184\nLa page de requête prépare aussi un petit jeu de données pour le machine learning.\n\n00:02:22.184 --> 00:02:25.757\nElle fait glisser une fenêtre de trois valeurs le long de la série :\n\n00:02:25.757 --> 00:02:29.488\nles trois valeurs sont les entrées, et la valeur suivante est la cible.\n\n00:02:29.488 --> 00:02:33.061\nC'est exactement le format utilisé pour la prévision dans le TP3bis.\n\n00:02:33.761 --> 00:02:35.725\nLes points faibles, et ils comptent.\n\n00:02:35.725 --> 00:02:39.542\nLa ville vient de l'URL et est collée directement dans la chaîne Flux,\n\n00:02:39.542 --> 00:02:43.741\ndonc une valeur avec des guillemets pourrait casser ou détourner la requête :\n\n00:02:43.741 --> 00:02:44.832\nc'est une injection.\n\n00:02:44.832 --> 00:02:47.231\nUn nouveau client est créé à chaque requête,\n\n00:02:47.231 --> 00:02:51.158\net les cinq requêtes du tableau de bord s'exécutent l'une après l'autre,\n\n00:02:51.158 --> 00:02:52.412\ndonc la page est lente.\n\n00:02:52.412 --> 00:02:55.030\nEt le token est en clair dans le docker compose.\n\n00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:56.611\nLe TP3bis corrige les quatre.\n\n00:02:57.311 --> 00:02:58.490\nEntraînement à l'oral.\n\n00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:01.491\nPourquoi une base de séries temporelles plutôt que SQL ?\n\n00:03:01.491 --> 00:03:05.938\nParce que les données de capteurs arrivent en continu, horodatées, en gros volumes.\n\n00:03:05.938 --> 00:03:10.171\nInfluxDB est conçu pour des écritures rapides et des requêtes sur des plages de\n\n00:03:10.171 --> 00:03:13.386\ntemps, avec des agrégations comme aggregateWindow intégrées.\n\n00:03:14.086 --> 00:03:16.472\nQuelle différence entre un tag et un field ?\n\n00:03:16.472 --> 00:03:19.129\nUn tag est une étiquette indexée, comme la ville,\n\n00:03:19.129 --> 00:03:21.189\nsur laquelle on filtre et on regroupe.\n\n00:03:21.189 --> 00:03:24.062\nUn field est la valeur mesurée, comme la température,\n\n00:03:24.062 --> 00:03:26.719\nsur laquelle on calcule et qui n'est pas indexée.\n\n00:03:27.419 --> 00:03:28.010\nÀ retenir :\n\n00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:32.467\nle vocabulaire d'InfluxDB, une requête Flux comme un pipeline qui commence par from\n\n00:03:32.467 --> 00:03:36.280\net range, notre fonction de requête qui ne fait jamais planter la page,\n\n00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:38.805\net des secrets qui viennent de l'environnement.\n\n00:03:38.805 --> 00:03:39.718\nProchaine vidéo :\n\n00:03:39.718 --> 00:03:43.423\nle TP3bis, où on nettoie tout ça et on prévoit les valeurs suivantes.\n"}, "tp3_fr": {"en": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.103\nLab three.\n\n00:00:01.103 --> 00:00:05.817\nWe leave relational databases for a time series database, InfluxDB.\n\n00:00:05.817 --> 00:00:10.671\nThe IUT server collects weather, currency, crypto and system metrics.\n\n00:00:10.671 --> 00:00:16.369\nOur Flask app queries it in the Flux language and draws the curves with Chart.js,\n\n00:00:16.369 --> 00:00:17.706\na bit like Grafana.\n\n00:00:18.406 --> 00:00:20.023\nFirst, the vocabulary.\n\n00:00:20.023 --> 00:00:23.477\nA bucket is a database with a retention period.\n\n00:00:23.477 --> 00:00:25.682\nA measurement is like a table.\n\n00:00:25.682 --> 00:00:29.945\nTags are indexed labels, like the city, that we filter on.\n\n00:00:29.945 --> 00:00:35.311\nFields are the measured values, like the temperature, that we compute on.\n\n00:00:35.311 --> 00:00:37.883\nEvery point has a time and a value.\n\n00:00:38.583 --> 00:00:39.588\nHere is the flow.\n\n00:00:39.588 --> 00:00:41.362\nThe filters come from the URL.\n\n00:00:41.362 --> 00:00:45.028\nFlask builds a Flux query string, sends it with the query API,\n\n00:00:45.028 --> 00:00:49.167\nreceives tables of records, groups them into series with one function,\n\n00:00:49.167 --> 00:00:52.655\nand passes them to the template, where Chart.js draws them.\n\n00:00:53.355 --> 00:00:56.753\nTo connect we need a URL, an organisation and a token.\n\n00:00:56.753 --> 00:01:00.025\nThey come from environment variables with os.getenv,\n\n00:01:00.025 --> 00:01:02.730\nso no secret is written in the Python code.\n\n00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:05.750\nGet client returns None if the token is missing,\n\n00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:09.337\nso the page can show a clear message instead of crashing.\n\n00:01:10.037 --> 00:01:11.736\nA Flux query is a pipeline.\n\n00:01:11.736 --> 00:01:15.574\nFrom a bucket, pipe forward a time range, which is mandatory:\n\n00:01:15.574 --> 00:01:17.839\nInfluxDB always reads a time window.\n\n00:01:17.839 --> 00:01:22.055\nThen filters on the measurement, on the city tag, and on the field.\n\n00:01:22.055 --> 00:01:25.516\nFinally aggregate window keeps one mean value per hour,\n\n00:01:25.516 --> 00:01:28.411\nwhich means far fewer points to send and plot.\n\n00:01:29.111 --> 00:01:32.492\nQuery flux sends the query and flattens the answer.\n\n00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:35.873\nInfluxDB returns several tables, each with records,\n\n00:01:35.873 --> 00:01:39.984\nso we loop twice and turn each record into a plain dictionary.\n\n00:01:39.984 --> 00:01:41.907\nThe function returns a tuple:\n\n00:01:41.907 --> 00:01:43.962\nok, a message, and the records.\n\n00:01:43.962 --> 00:01:48.868\nIf anything fails, we return the error message so the page can display it.\n\n00:01:48.868 --> 00:01:52.448\nAnd the finally block closes the client in every case.\n\n00:01:53.148 --> 00:01:56.989\nGroup time series turns the flat list into chart series.\n\n00:01:56.989 --> 00:02:01.585\nIt skips non numeric values, names each series from chosen columns,\n\n00:02:01.585 --> 00:02:05.906\nlike the field or the city, and stores each value by timestamp.\n\n00:02:05.906 --> 00:02:09.953\nThen it builds one sorted time axis shared by every series.\n\n00:02:09.953 --> 00:02:13.519\nIf a series has no value at some time, it gets None,\n\n00:02:13.519 --> 00:02:15.783\nand Chart.js simply leaves a gap.\n\n00:02:16.483 --> 00:02:18.516\nThe dropdowns are not hard coded.\n\n00:02:18.516 --> 00:02:22.026\nSchema.tag values asks InfluxDB for every value of a tag,\n\n00:02:22.026 --> 00:02:24.983\nfor example all locations in the weather bucket.\n\n00:02:24.983 --> 00:02:29.910\nSo if a new city is added to the database, it appears in our form automatically.\n\n00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:34.561\nThe query page also prepares a small dataset for machine learning.\n\n00:02:34.561 --> 00:02:37.673\nIt slides a window of three values along the series:\n\n00:02:37.673 --> 00:02:41.623\nthe three values are the inputs, and the next value is the target.\n\n00:02:41.623 --> 00:02:45.274\nThis is exactly the format we use for forecasting in TP3 bis.\n\n00:02:45.974 --> 00:02:47.861\nWeak points, and they matter.\n\n00:02:47.861 --> 00:02:52.544\nThe city comes from the URL and is pasted directly into the Flux string,\n\n00:02:52.544 --> 00:02:56.121\nso a value with quotes could break or hijack the query:\n\n00:02:56.121 --> 00:02:57.487\nthat is an injection.\n\n00:02:57.487 --> 00:03:00.089\nA new client is created for every query,\n\n00:03:00.089 --> 00:03:05.033\nand the five dashboard queries run one after the other, so the page is slow.\n\n00:03:05.033 --> 00:03:08.350\nAnd the token sits in plain text in docker compose.\n\n00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:09.846\nTP3 bis fixes all four.\n\n00:03:10.546 --> 00:03:11.475\nOral practice.\n\n00:03:11.475 --> 00:03:14.262\nWhy a time series database instead of SQL?\n\n00:03:14.262 --> 00:03:19.040\nBecause sensor data arrives continuously, timestamped, in large volumes.\n\n00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.486\nInfluxDB is built for fast writes and for queries over time ranges,\n\n00:03:23.486 --> 00:03:26.737\nwith aggregations like aggregate window built in.\n\n00:03:27.437 --> 00:03:30.506\nWhat's the difference between a tag and a field?\n\n00:03:30.506 --> 00:03:35.238\nA tag is an indexed label, like the location, that we filter and group on.\n\n00:03:35.238 --> 00:03:38.562\nA field is the measured value, like the temperature,\n\n00:03:38.562 --> 00:03:41.440\nwhich we compute on and which is not indexed.\n\n00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:42.938\nTo remember:\n\n00:03:42.938 --> 00:03:48.328\nthe InfluxDB vocabulary, a Flux query as a pipeline starting with from and range,\n\n00:03:48.328 --> 00:03:51.455\nour query function that never crashes the page,\n\n00:03:51.455 --> 00:03:54.317\nand secrets that come from the environment.\n\n00:03:54.317 --> 00:03:55.049\nNext video:\n\n00:03:55.049 --> 00:03:59.108\nTP3 bis, where we clean this up and forecast the next values.\n", "fr": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:00.652\nTP3.\n\n00:00:00.652 --> 00:00:05.749\nOn quitte les bases relationnelles pour une base de séries temporelles, InfluxDB.\n\n00:00:05.749 --> 00:00:08.958\nLe serveur de l'IUT collecte la météo, les devises,\n\n00:00:08.958 --> 00:00:11.287\nles cryptos et des métriques système.\n\n00:00:11.287 --> 00:00:16.384\nNotre appli Flask l'interroge en langage Flux et trace les courbes avec Chart.js,\n\n00:00:16.384 --> 00:00:17.706\nun peu comme Grafana.\n\n00:00:18.406 --> 00:00:19.894\nD'abord, le vocabulaire.\n\n00:00:19.894 --> 00:00:23.058\nUn bucket est une base avec une durée de rétention.\n\n00:00:23.058 --> 00:00:25.291\nUne measurement est comme une table.\n\n00:00:25.291 --> 00:00:30.254\nLes tags sont des étiquettes indexées, comme la ville, sur lesquelles on filtre.\n\n00:00:30.254 --> 00:00:33.913\nLes fields sont les valeurs mesurées, comme la température,\n\n00:00:33.913 --> 00:00:35.526\nsur lesquelles on calcule.\n\n00:00:35.526 --> 00:00:37.883\nChaque point a un temps et une valeur.\n\n00:00:38.583 --> 00:00:39.429\nVoici le flux.\n\n00:00:39.429 --> 00:00:41.241\nLes filtres viennent de l'URL.\n\n00:00:41.241 --> 00:00:44.925\nFlask construit une requête Flux, l'envoie avec la query API,\n\n00:00:44.925 --> 00:00:49.636\nreçoit des tables d'enregistrements, les regroupe en séries avec une fonction,\n\n00:00:49.636 --> 00:00:52.655\net les passe au template, où Chart.js les dessine.\n\n00:00:53.355 --> 00:00:57.119\nPour se connecter, il faut une URL, une organisation et un token.\n\n00:00:57.119 --> 00:01:00.420\nIls viennent de variables d'environnement avec os.getenv,\n\n00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.315\ndonc aucun secret n'est écrit dans le code Python.\n\n00:01:03.315 --> 00:01:05.805\nget_client renvoie None si le token manque,\n\n00:01:05.805 --> 00:01:09.337\npour que la page affiche un message clair au lieu de planter.\n\n00:01:10.037 --> 00:01:11.968\nUne requête Flux est un pipeline.\n\n00:01:11.968 --> 00:01:15.654\nDepuis un bucket, on enchaîne une plage de temps, obligatoire :\n\n00:01:15.654 --> 00:01:18.170\nInfluxDB lit toujours une fenêtre de temps.\n\n00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:22.267\nPuis des filtres sur la measurement, sur le tag ville et sur le field.\n\n00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:25.192\nEnfin aggregateWindow garde une moyenne par heure,\n\n00:01:25.192 --> 00:01:28.411\nce qui fait bien moins de points à envoyer et à tracer.\n\n00:01:29.111 --> 00:01:32.194\nquery_flux envoie la requête et aplatit la réponse.\n\n00:01:32.194 --> 00:01:35.822\nInfluxDB renvoie plusieurs tables, chacune avec des records,\n\n00:01:35.822 --> 00:01:40.598\ndonc on boucle deux fois et on transforme chaque record en simple dictionnaire.\n\n00:01:40.598 --> 00:01:42.412\nLa fonction renvoie un tuple :\n\n00:01:42.412 --> 00:01:44.226\nok, un message et les records.\n\n00:01:44.226 --> 00:01:49.244\nSi quelque chose échoue, on renvoie le message d'erreur pour que la page l'affiche.\n\n00:01:49.244 --> 00:01:52.448\nEt le bloc finally ferme le client dans tous les cas.\n\n00:01:53.148 --> 00:01:57.243\ngroup_time_series transforme la liste plate en séries pour le graphique.\n\n00:01:57.243 --> 00:01:59.461\nElle ignore les valeurs non numériques,\n\n00:01:59.461 --> 00:02:03.840\nnomme chaque série à partir de colonnes choisies, comme le field ou la ville,\n\n00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.001\net range chaque valeur par horodatage.\n\n00:02:06.001 --> 00:02:10.096\nPuis elle construit un axe du temps trié, partagé par toutes les séries.\n\n00:02:10.096 --> 00:02:13.622\nSi une série n'a pas de valeur à un instant, elle reçoit None,\n\n00:02:13.622 --> 00:02:15.783\net Chart.js laisse simplement un trou.\n\n00:02:16.483 --> 00:02:19.063\nLes listes déroulantes ne sont pas codées en dur.\n\n00:02:19.063 --> 00:02:22.433\nschema.tagValues demande à InfluxDB toutes les valeurs d'un tag,\n\n00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:24.855\npar exemple toutes les villes du bucket météo.\n\n00:02:24.855 --> 00:02:27.330\nDonc si une nouvelle ville est ajoutée en base,\n\n00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:29.910\nelle apparaît automatiquement dans le formulaire.\n\n00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:34.771\nLa page de requête prépare aussi un petit jeu de données pour le machine learning.\n\n00:02:34.771 --> 00:02:38.221\nElle fait glisser une fenêtre de trois valeurs le long de la série :\n\n00:02:38.221 --> 00:02:41.824\nles trois valeurs sont les entrées, et la valeur suivante est la cible.\n\n00:02:41.824 --> 00:02:45.274\nC'est exactement le format utilisé pour la prévision dans le TP3bis.\n\n00:02:45.974 --> 00:02:48.025\nLes points faibles, et ils comptent.\n\n00:02:48.025 --> 00:02:52.013\nLa ville vient de l'URL et est collée directement dans la chaîne Flux,\n\n00:02:52.013 --> 00:02:56.400\ndonc une valeur avec des guillemets pourrait casser ou détourner la requête :\n\n00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:57.540\nc'est une injection.\n\n00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:00.047\nUn nouveau client est créé à chaque requête,\n\n00:03:00.047 --> 00:03:04.149\net les cinq requêtes du tableau de bord s'exécutent l'une après l'autre,\n\n00:03:04.149 --> 00:03:05.459\ndonc la page est lente.\n\n00:03:05.459 --> 00:03:08.194\nEt le token est en clair dans le docker compose.\n\n00:03:08.194 --> 00:03:09.846\nLe TP3bis corrige les quatre.\n\n00:03:10.546 --> 00:03:11.733\nEntraînement à l'oral.\n\n00:03:11.733 --> 00:03:14.756\nPourquoi une base de séries temporelles plutôt que SQL ?\n\n00:03:14.756 --> 00:03:19.235\nParce que les données de capteurs arrivent en continu, horodatées, en gros volumes.\n\n00:03:19.235 --> 00:03:23.499\nInfluxDB est conçu pour des écritures rapides et des requêtes sur des plages de\n\n00:03:23.499 --> 00:03:26.737\ntemps, avec des agrégations comme aggregateWindow intégrées.\n\n00:03:27.437 --> 00:03:30.081\nQuelle différence entre un tag et un field ?\n\n00:03:30.081 --> 00:03:33.026\nUn tag est une étiquette indexée, comme la ville,\n\n00:03:33.026 --> 00:03:35.310\nsur laquelle on filtre et on regroupe.\n\n00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:38.495\nUn field est la valeur mesurée, comme la température,\n\n00:03:38.495 --> 00:03:41.440\nsur laquelle on calcule et qui n'est pas indexée.\n\n00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:42.766\nÀ retenir :\n\n00:03:42.766 --> 00:03:47.492\nle vocabulaire d'InfluxDB, une requête Flux comme un pipeline qui commence par from\n\n00:03:47.492 --> 00:03:51.535\net range, notre fonction de requête qui ne fait jamais planter la page,\n\n00:03:51.535 --> 00:03:54.211\net des secrets qui viennent de l'environnement.\n\n00:03:54.211 --> 00:03:55.179\nProchaine vidéo :\n\n00:03:55.179 --> 00:03:59.108\nle TP3bis, où on nettoie tout ça et on prévoit les valeurs suivantes.\n"}, "tp3bis": {"en": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.366\nTP3 bis is the clean version of lab three.\n\n00:00:03.366 --> 00:00:07.108\nWe fix the injection risk, share one InfluxDB client,\n\n00:00:07.108 --> 00:00:11.768\nload the dashboard panels in parallel, keep secrets in a.env file,\n\n00:00:11.768 --> 00:00:16.358\nand we add a forecast page that compares three prediction models,\n\n00:00:16.358 --> 00:00:18.123\ncoded by hand with NumPy.\n\n00:00:18.823 --> 00:00:22.239\nThe single app file of TP3 is split into modules.\n\n00:00:22.239 --> 00:00:24.469\nInflux.py talks to the database.\n\n00:00:24.469 --> 00:00:26.352\nFlux.py builds the queries.\n\n00:00:26.352 --> 00:00:28.931\nSeries.py shapes the data for charts.\n\n00:00:28.931 --> 00:00:31.161\nForecast.py contains the models.\n\n00:00:31.161 --> 00:00:34.717\nRoutes.py only reads the request and renders pages.\n\n00:00:34.717 --> 00:00:38.551\nAnd secrets move to a.env file that is never committed.\n\n00:00:39.251 --> 00:00:40.224\nFirst, security.\n\n00:00:40.224 --> 00:00:45.151\nAny value that comes from the user goes through flux str, which calls json dumps.\n\n00:00:45.151 --> 00:00:49.774\nThat produces a properly quoted string with every special character escaped.\n\n00:00:49.774 --> 00:00:52.329\nSo even if someone puts quotes in the URL,\n\n00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:55.918\nthey can't close the string and inject their own Flux code.\n\n00:00:56.618 --> 00:00:59.517\nThe underscore in helper also matters for speed.\n\n00:00:59.517 --> 00:01:03.927\nTo filter on several values, we write a chain of equalities joined by or.\n\n00:01:03.927 --> 00:01:06.102\nInfluxDB can push that to its index.\n\n00:01:06.102 --> 00:01:09.122\nThe contains function would scan the whole bucket,\n\n00:01:09.122 --> 00:01:11.961\nand on forex and crypto that ended in timeouts.\n\n00:01:12.661 --> 00:01:15.784\nThe dashboard describes its five panels as a list of specs,\n\n00:01:15.784 --> 00:01:18.484\nthen maps build panel over them with a thread pool.\n\n00:01:18.484 --> 00:01:22.824\nEach query spends most of its time waiting for the network, so threads are enough:\n\n00:01:22.824 --> 00:01:27.006\nthe page now takes as long as the slowest query instead of the sum of all five.\n\n00:01:27.706 --> 00:01:29.211\nInputs are validated too.\n\n00:01:29.211 --> 00:01:33.005\nPick one only accepts a value that belongs to the allowed list,\n\n00:01:33.005 --> 00:01:37.943\nfor example the cities that really exist in InfluxDB, otherwise it uses a default.\n\n00:01:37.943 --> 00:01:42.279\nArg int converts a number and clamps it between a minimum and a maximum,\n\n00:01:42.279 --> 00:01:45.772\nand falls back to the default if the text is not a number.\n\n00:01:46.472 --> 00:01:48.193\nNow the forecasting part.\n\n00:01:48.193 --> 00:01:52.185\nSliding matrix turns one series into a supervised dataset.\n\n00:01:52.185 --> 00:01:54.870\nEach row of X holds window past values,\n\n00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:59.138\nand the same row of Y holds the horizon values that came next.\n\n00:01:59.138 --> 00:02:03.750\nWith ten, eleven, twelve, thirteen, fourteen and a window of three,\n\n00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:08.224\nthe first row is ten, eleven, twelve, and the target is thirteen.\n\n00:02:08.924 --> 00:02:12.933\nWe compare three models, all with the same fit and predict methods.\n\n00:02:12.933 --> 00:02:16.283\nThe naive model says the next value equals the last one:\n\n00:02:16.283 --> 00:02:18.916\nit is the baseline any real model must beat.\n\n00:02:18.916 --> 00:02:23.283\nThe linear trend fits a least squares line on each window and extends it.\n\n00:02:23.283 --> 00:02:27.890\nThe autoregressive model learns weights W and a bias b with gradient descent.\n\n00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:30.678\nInside fit, we first standardise X,\n\n00:02:30.678 --> 00:02:35.273\nbecause a crypto price around sixty thousand dollars would make the gradients\n\n00:02:35.273 --> 00:02:35.750\nexplode.\n\n00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:39.211\nThen, for each iteration, we compute the prediction error,\n\n00:02:39.211 --> 00:02:41.538\nits derivative with respect to W and b,\n\n00:02:41.538 --> 00:02:45.834\nwhich is the gradient of the mean squared error plus a small L2 penalty,\n\n00:02:45.834 --> 00:02:48.519\nand we take a step in the opposite direction.\n\n00:02:48.519 --> 00:02:53.471\nThe step size is one over L, computed from the data, so the descent never diverges.\n\n00:02:54.171 --> 00:02:55.816\nEvaluation follows one rule:\n\n00:02:55.816 --> 00:02:59.928\nnever shuffle a time series, or the model would learn from the future.\n\n00:02:59.928 --> 00:03:03.277\nWe train on the first eighty percent and test on the end.\n\n00:03:03.277 --> 00:03:07.448\nWe also drop the windows just before the test whose targets overlap it.\n\n00:03:07.448 --> 00:03:09.504\nThe best model has the lowest RMSE,\n\n00:03:09.504 --> 00:03:13.323\nand we show each model's gain over the naive baseline in percent.\n\n00:03:14.023 --> 00:03:14.941\nOral practice.\n\n00:03:14.941 --> 00:03:18.944\nMSE is the mean of squared errors, so big errors weigh a lot.\n\n00:03:18.944 --> 00:03:24.389\nRMSE is its square root, so it is in the same unit as the data, degrees or dollars.\n\n00:03:24.389 --> 00:03:28.785\nMAE is the mean absolute error, less sensitive to a single outlier.\n\n00:03:28.785 --> 00:03:30.557\nWe rank the models on RMSE.\n\n00:03:31.257 --> 00:03:31.981\nWeak points.\n\n00:03:31.981 --> 00:03:36.928\nA leftover file, chart forecast, is never imported, and it would crash if it were,\n\n00:03:36.928 --> 00:03:40.428\nbecause it passes an argument run forecast doesn't accept:\n\n00:03:40.428 --> 00:03:41.634\nwe should delete it.\n\n00:03:41.634 --> 00:03:45.978\nThe example env file contains the real token, which defeats its purpose.\n\n00:03:45.978 --> 00:03:49.840\nAnd the container runs Flask's development server in debug mode.\n\n00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:51.349\nTo remember:\n\n00:03:51.349 --> 00:03:55.194\nescape every user value, prefer indexed equality filters,\n\n00:03:55.194 --> 00:03:59.915\nuse threads for queries that wait on the network, and for forecasting,\n\n00:03:59.915 --> 00:04:05.177\nsliding windows, a chronological split, a naive baseline, and RMSE to compare.\n\n00:04:05.177 --> 00:04:05.918\nNext video:\n\n00:04:05.918 --> 00:04:08.077\nlab four, serving a Keras model.\n", "fr": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.759\nLe TP3bis est la version propre du TP3.\n\n00:00:02.759 --> 00:00:06.933\nOn corrige le risque d'injection, on partage un seul client InfluxDB,\n\n00:00:06.933 --> 00:00:10.260\non charge les panneaux du tableau de bord en parallèle,\n\n00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.800\non garde les secrets dans un fichier .env,\n\n00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:16.490\net on ajoute une page de prévision qui compare trois modèles,\n\n00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:18.123\ncodés à la main avec NumPy.\n\n00:00:18.823 --> 00:00:21.728\nLe fichier unique du TP3 est découpé en modules.\n\n00:00:21.728 --> 00:00:23.301\ninflux.py parle à la base.\n\n00:00:23.301 --> 00:00:25.177\nflux.py construit les requêtes.\n\n00:00:25.177 --> 00:00:28.505\nseries.py met les données en forme pour les graphiques.\n\n00:00:28.505 --> 00:00:30.502\nforecast.py contient les modèles.\n\n00:00:30.502 --> 00:00:34.375\nroutes.py se contente de lire la requête et de rendre les pages.\n\n00:00:34.375 --> 00:00:38.551\nEt les secrets passent dans un fichier .env qui n'est jamais commité.\n\n00:00:39.251 --> 00:00:40.383\nD'abord, la sécurité.\n\n00:00:40.383 --> 00:00:44.698\nToute valeur venant de l'utilisateur passe par flux_str, qui appelle json.dumps.\n\n00:00:44.698 --> 00:00:47.503\nÇa produit une chaîne correctement entre guillemets,\n\n00:00:47.503 --> 00:00:49.553\navec chaque caractère spécial échappé.\n\n00:00:49.553 --> 00:00:52.412\nDonc même si quelqu'un met des guillemets dans l'URL,\n\n00:00:52.412 --> 00:00:55.918\nil ne peut pas fermer la chaîne et injecter son propre code Flux.\n\n00:00:56.618 --> 00:00:59.194\nL'assistant _in compte aussi pour la vitesse.\n\n00:00:59.194 --> 00:01:03.832\nPour filtrer sur plusieurs valeurs, on écrit une suite d'égalités reliées par or.\n\n00:01:03.832 --> 00:01:06.465\nInfluxDB peut s'appuyer sur son index pour ça.\n\n00:01:06.465 --> 00:01:09.213\nLa fonction contains parcourrait tout le bucket,\n\n00:01:09.213 --> 00:01:11.961\net sur forex et crypto ça finissait en timeouts.\n\n00:01:12.661 --> 00:01:15.844\nLe tableau de bord décrit ses cinq panneaux comme une liste de specs,\n\n00:01:15.844 --> 00:01:18.473\npuis applique build_panel dessus avec un pool de threads.\n\n00:01:18.473 --> 00:01:21.563\nChaque requête passe l'essentiel de son temps à attendre le réseau,\n\n00:01:21.563 --> 00:01:22.855\ndonc des threads suffisent :\n\n00:01:22.855 --> 00:01:26.591\nla page prend maintenant le temps de la requête la plus lente au lieu de la somme\n\n00:01:26.591 --> 00:01:27.006\ndes cinq.\n\n00:01:27.706 --> 00:01:29.501\nLes entrées sont validées aussi.\n\n00:01:29.501 --> 00:01:32.587\npick_one n'accepte qu'une valeur de la liste autorisée,\n\n00:01:32.587 --> 00:01:35.897\npar exemple les villes qui existent vraiment dans InfluxDB,\n\n00:01:35.897 --> 00:01:38.085\nsinon elle prend une valeur par défaut.\n\n00:01:38.085 --> 00:01:42.069\narg_int convertit un nombre et le borne entre un minimum et un maximum,\n\n00:01:42.069 --> 00:01:45.772\net revient à la valeur par défaut si le texte n'est pas un nombre.\n\n00:01:46.472 --> 00:01:48.123\nPassons à la prévision.\n\n00:01:48.123 --> 00:01:52.717\nsliding_matrix transforme une série en jeu de données supervisé.\n\n00:01:52.717 --> 00:01:56.307\nChaque ligne de X contient window valeurs passées,\n\n00:01:56.307 --> 00:02:00.973\net la même ligne de Y contient les horizon valeurs qui ont suivi.\n\n00:02:00.973 --> 00:02:06.860\nAvec 10, 11, 12, 13, 14 et une fenêtre de trois, la première ligne est 10, 11, 12,\n\n00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:08.224\net la cible est 13.\n\n00:02:08.924 --> 00:02:12.622\nOn compare trois modèles, tous avec les mêmes méthodes fit et predict.\n\n00:02:12.622 --> 00:02:15.898\nLe modèle naïf dit que la prochaine valeur égale la dernière :\n\n00:02:15.898 --> 00:02:18.645\nc'est la référence que tout vrai modèle doit battre.\n\n00:02:18.645 --> 00:02:23.030\nLa tendance linéaire ajuste une droite des moindres carrés sur chaque fenêtre et la\n\n00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:23.505\nprolonge.\n\n00:02:23.505 --> 00:02:27.890\nLe modèle autorégressif apprend des poids W et un biais b par descente de gradient.\n\n00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:30.634\nDans fit, on standardise d'abord X,\n\n00:02:30.634 --> 00:02:34.956\ncar un prix de crypto autour de soixante mille dollars ferait exploser les\n\n00:02:34.956 --> 00:02:35.540\ngradients.\n\n00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:39.045\nPuis, à chaque itération, on calcule l'erreur de prédiction,\n\n00:02:39.045 --> 00:02:43.776\nsa dérivée par rapport à W et b, c'est-à-dire le gradient de l'erreur quadratique\n\n00:02:43.776 --> 00:02:48.507\nmoyenne plus une petite pénalité L2, et on fait un pas dans la direction opposée.\n\n00:02:48.507 --> 00:02:51.427\nLe pas vaut 1 sur L, calculé à partir des données,\n\n00:02:51.427 --> 00:02:53.471\ndonc la descente ne diverge jamais.\n\n00:02:54.171 --> 00:02:55.701\nL'évaluation suit une règle :\n\n00:02:55.701 --> 00:02:59.816\nne jamais mélanger une série temporelle, sinon le modèle apprendrait du futur.\n\n00:02:59.816 --> 00:03:02.824\nOn entraîne sur les premiers 80 % et on teste sur la fin.\n\n00:03:02.824 --> 00:03:07.044\nOn retire aussi les fenêtres juste avant le test dont les cibles le chevauchent.\n\n00:03:07.044 --> 00:03:09.208\nLe meilleur modèle a le RMSE le plus bas,\n\n00:03:09.208 --> 00:03:13.323\net on affiche le gain de chaque modèle sur la référence naïve, en pourcentage.\n\n00:03:14.023 --> 00:03:15.170\nEntraînement à l'oral.\n\n00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:17.413\nLe MSE est la moyenne des erreurs au carré,\n\n00:03:17.413 --> 00:03:19.552\ndonc les grosses erreurs pèsent beaucoup.\n\n00:03:19.552 --> 00:03:23.568\nLe RMSE est sa racine carrée, donc il est dans la même unité que les données,\n\n00:03:23.568 --> 00:03:24.507\ndegrés ou dollars.\n\n00:03:24.507 --> 00:03:28.783\nLe MAE est l'erreur absolue moyenne, moins sensible à une valeur aberrante isolée.\n\n00:03:28.783 --> 00:03:30.557\nOn classe les modèles sur le RMSE.\n\n00:03:31.257 --> 00:03:32.099\nPoints faibles.\n\n00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:35.243\nUn fichier oublié, chart_forecast, n'est jamais importé,\n\n00:03:35.243 --> 00:03:39.846\net il planterait s'il l'était, car il passe un argument que run_forecast n'accepte\n\n00:03:39.846 --> 00:03:40.127\npas :\n\n00:03:40.127 --> 00:03:41.531\nil faudrait le supprimer.\n\n00:03:41.531 --> 00:03:45.741\nLe fichier .env d'exemple contient le vrai token, ce qui va contre son but.\n\n00:03:45.741 --> 00:03:49.840\nEt le conteneur lance le serveur de développement de Flask en mode debug.\n\n00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:51.150\nÀ retenir :\n\n00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:55.312\néchapper chaque valeur utilisateur, préférer des filtres d'égalité indexés,\n\n00:03:55.312 --> 00:03:58.809\nutiliser des threads pour des requêtes qui attendent le réseau,\n\n00:03:58.809 --> 00:04:02.916\net pour la prévision, des fenêtres glissantes, un découpage chronologique,\n\n00:04:02.916 --> 00:04:05.413\nune référence naïve et le RMSE pour comparer.\n\n00:04:05.413 --> 00:04:06.356\nProchaine vidéo :\n\n00:04:06.356 --> 00:04:08.077\nle TP4, servir un modèle Keras.\n"}, "tp3bis_fr": {"en": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.280\nTP3 bis is the clean version of lab three.\n\n00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.915\nWe fix the injection risk, share one InfluxDB client,\n\n00:00:06.915 --> 00:00:11.440\nload the dashboard panels in parallel, keep secrets in a.env file,\n\n00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:15.898\nand we add a forecast page that compares three prediction models,\n\n00:00:15.898 --> 00:00:17.612\ncoded by hand with NumPy.\n\n00:00:18.312 --> 00:00:21.528\nThe single app file of TP3 is split into modules.\n\n00:00:21.528 --> 00:00:23.627\nInflux.py talks to the database.\n\n00:00:23.627 --> 00:00:25.399\nFlux.py builds the queries.\n\n00:00:25.399 --> 00:00:27.827\nSeries.py shapes the data for charts.\n\n00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:29.927\nForecast.py contains the models.\n\n00:00:29.927 --> 00:00:33.274\nRoutes.py only reads the request and renders pages.\n\n00:00:33.274 --> 00:00:36.883\nAnd secrets move to a.env file that is never committed.\n\n00:00:37.583 --> 00:00:38.624\nFirst, security.\n\n00:00:38.624 --> 00:00:43.894\nAny value that comes from the user goes through flux str, which calls json dumps.\n\n00:00:43.894 --> 00:00:48.839\nThat produces a properly quoted string with every special character escaped.\n\n00:00:48.839 --> 00:00:51.572\nSo even if someone puts quotes in the URL,\n\n00:00:51.572 --> 00:00:55.411\nthey can't close the string and inject their own Flux code.\n\n00:00:56.111 --> 00:00:59.100\nThe underscore in helper also matters for speed.\n\n00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.647\nTo filter on several values, we write a chain of equalities joined by or.\n\n00:01:03.647 --> 00:01:05.889\nInfluxDB can push that to its index.\n\n00:01:05.889 --> 00:01:09.003\nThe contains function would scan the whole bucket,\n\n00:01:09.003 --> 00:01:11.931\nand on forex and crypto that ended in timeouts.\n\n00:01:12.631 --> 00:01:16.431\nThe dashboard describes its five panels as a list of specs,\n\n00:01:16.431 --> 00:01:19.716\nthen maps build panel over them with a thread pool.\n\n00:01:19.716 --> 00:01:24.998\nEach query spends most of its time waiting for the network, so threads are enough:\n\n00:01:24.998 --> 00:01:30.087\nthe page now takes as long as the slowest query instead of the sum of all five.\n\n00:01:30.787 --> 00:01:32.305\nInputs are validated too.\n\n00:01:32.305 --> 00:01:36.130\nPick one only accepts a value that belongs to the allowed list,\n\n00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:41.109\nfor example the cities that really exist in InfluxDB, otherwise it uses a default.\n\n00:01:41.109 --> 00:01:45.481\nArg int converts a number and clamps it between a minimum and a maximum,\n\n00:01:45.481 --> 00:01:49.003\nand falls back to the default if the text is not a number.\n\n00:01:49.703 --> 00:01:51.305\nNow the forecasting part.\n\n00:01:51.305 --> 00:01:55.021\nSliding matrix turns one series into a supervised dataset.\n\n00:01:55.021 --> 00:01:57.520\nEach row of X holds window past values,\n\n00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.493\nand the same row of Y holds the horizon values that came next.\n\n00:02:01.493 --> 00:02:05.786\nWith ten, eleven, twelve, thirteen, fourteen and a window of three,\n\n00:02:05.786 --> 00:02:09.951\nthe first row is ten, eleven, twelve, and the target is thirteen.\n\n00:02:10.651 --> 00:02:15.315\nWe compare three models, all with the same fit and predict methods.\n\n00:02:15.315 --> 00:02:19.213\nThe naive model says the next value equals the last one:\n\n00:02:19.213 --> 00:02:22.276\nit is the baseline any real model must beat.\n\n00:02:22.276 --> 00:02:27.357\nThe linear trend fits a least squares line on each window and extends it.\n\n00:02:27.357 --> 00:02:32.717\nThe autoregressive model learns weights W and a bias b with gradient descent.\n\n00:02:33.417 --> 00:02:35.472\nInside fit, we first standardise X,\n\n00:02:35.472 --> 00:02:39.994\nbecause a crypto price around sixty thousand dollars would make the gradients\n\n00:02:39.994 --> 00:02:40.463\nexplode.\n\n00:02:40.463 --> 00:02:43.869\nThen, for each iteration, we compute the prediction error,\n\n00:02:43.869 --> 00:02:46.159\nits derivative with respect to W and b,\n\n00:02:46.159 --> 00:02:50.387\nwhich is the gradient of the mean squared error plus a small L2 penalty,\n\n00:02:50.387 --> 00:02:53.029\nand we take a step in the opposite direction.\n\n00:02:53.029 --> 00:02:57.903\nThe step size is one over L, computed from the data, so the descent never diverges.\n\n00:02:58.603 --> 00:03:00.352\nEvaluation follows one rule:\n\n00:03:00.352 --> 00:03:04.723\nnever shuffle a time series, or the model would learn from the future.\n\n00:03:04.723 --> 00:03:08.283\nWe train on the first eighty percent and test on the end.\n\n00:03:08.283 --> 00:03:12.717\nWe also drop the windows just before the test whose targets overlap it.\n\n00:03:12.717 --> 00:03:14.903\nThe best model has the lowest RMSE,\n\n00:03:14.903 --> 00:03:18.962\nand we show each model's gain over the naive baseline in percent.\n\n00:03:19.662 --> 00:03:20.730\nOral practice.\n\n00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:25.381\nMSE is the mean of squared errors, so big errors weigh a lot.\n\n00:03:25.381 --> 00:03:31.710\nRMSE is its square root, so it is in the same unit as the data, degrees or dollars.\n\n00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:36.819\nMAE is the mean absolute error, less sensitive to a single outlier.\n\n00:03:36.819 --> 00:03:38.878\nWe rank the models on RMSE.\n\n00:03:39.578 --> 00:03:40.330\nWeak points.\n\n00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:45.466\nA leftover file, chart forecast, is never imported, and it would crash if it were,\n\n00:03:45.466 --> 00:03:49.099\nbecause it passes an argument run forecast doesn't accept:\n\n00:03:49.099 --> 00:03:50.351\nwe should delete it.\n\n00:03:50.351 --> 00:03:54.861\nThe example env file contains the real token, which defeats its purpose.\n\n00:03:54.861 --> 00:03:58.869\nAnd the container runs Flask's development server in debug mode.\n\n00:03:59.569 --> 00:04:00.494\nTo remember:\n\n00:04:00.494 --> 00:04:04.889\nescape every user value, prefer indexed equality filters,\n\n00:04:04.889 --> 00:04:10.286\nuse threads for queries that wait on the network, and for forecasting,\n\n00:04:10.286 --> 00:04:16.299\nsliding windows, a chronological split, a naive baseline, and RMSE to compare.\n\n00:04:16.299 --> 00:04:17.147\nNext video:\n\n00:04:17.147 --> 00:04:19.614\nlab four, serving a Keras model.\n", "fr": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.691\nLe TP3bis est la version propre du TP3.\n\n00:00:02.691 --> 00:00:06.744\nOn corrige le risque d'injection, on partage un seul client InfluxDB,\n\n00:00:06.744 --> 00:00:09.975\non charge les panneaux du tableau de bord en parallèle,\n\n00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:12.443\non garde les secrets dans un fichier .env,\n\n00:00:12.443 --> 00:00:16.026\net on ajoute une page de prévision qui compare trois modèles,\n\n00:00:16.026 --> 00:00:17.612\ncodés à la main avec NumPy.\n\n00:00:18.312 --> 00:00:21.046\nLe fichier unique du TP3 est découpé en modules.\n\n00:00:21.046 --> 00:00:22.528\ninflux.py parle à la base.\n\n00:00:22.528 --> 00:00:24.293\nflux.py construit les requêtes.\n\n00:00:24.293 --> 00:00:27.427\nseries.py met les données en forme pour les graphiques.\n\n00:00:27.427 --> 00:00:29.306\nforecast.py contient les modèles.\n\n00:00:29.306 --> 00:00:32.952\nroutes.py se contente de lire la requête et de rendre les pages.\n\n00:00:32.952 --> 00:00:36.883\nEt les secrets passent dans un fichier .env qui n'est jamais commité.\n\n00:00:37.583 --> 00:00:38.794\nD'abord, la sécurité.\n\n00:00:38.794 --> 00:00:43.410\nToute valeur venant de l'utilisateur passe par flux_str, qui appelle json.dumps.\n\n00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:46.410\nÇa produit une chaîne correctement entre guillemets,\n\n00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.603\navec chaque caractère spécial échappé.\n\n00:00:48.603 --> 00:00:51.661\nDonc même si quelqu'un met des guillemets dans l'URL,\n\n00:00:51.661 --> 00:00:55.411\nil ne peut pas fermer la chaîne et injecter son propre code Flux.\n\n00:00:56.111 --> 00:00:58.767\nL'assistant _in compte aussi pour la vitesse.\n\n00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:03.548\nPour filtrer sur plusieurs valeurs, on écrit une suite d'égalités reliées par or.\n\n00:01:03.548 --> 00:01:06.264\nInfluxDB peut s'appuyer sur son index pour ça.\n\n00:01:06.264 --> 00:01:09.097\nLa fonction contains parcourrait tout le bucket,\n\n00:01:09.097 --> 00:01:11.931\net sur forex et crypto ça finissait en timeouts.\n\n00:01:12.631 --> 00:01:16.503\nLe tableau de bord décrit ses cinq panneaux comme une liste de specs,\n\n00:01:16.503 --> 00:01:19.703\npuis applique build_panel dessus avec un pool de threads.\n\n00:01:19.703 --> 00:01:23.463\nChaque requête passe l'essentiel de son temps à attendre le réseau,\n\n00:01:23.463 --> 00:01:25.035\ndonc des threads suffisent :\n\n00:01:25.035 --> 00:01:29.581\nla page prend maintenant le temps de la requête la plus lente au lieu de la somme\n\n00:01:29.581 --> 00:01:30.087\ndes cinq.\n\n00:01:30.787 --> 00:01:32.597\nLes entrées sont validées aussi.\n\n00:01:32.597 --> 00:01:35.708\npick_one n'accepte qu'une valeur de la liste autorisée,\n\n00:01:35.708 --> 00:01:39.046\npar exemple les villes qui existent vraiment dans InfluxDB,\n\n00:01:39.046 --> 00:01:41.252\nsinon elle prend une valeur par défaut.\n\n00:01:41.252 --> 00:01:45.269\narg_int convertit un nombre et le borne entre un minimum et un maximum,\n\n00:01:45.269 --> 00:01:49.003\net revient à la valeur par défaut si le texte n'est pas un nombre.\n\n00:01:49.703 --> 00:01:51.240\nPassons à la prévision.\n\n00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:55.517\nsliding_matrix transforme une série en jeu de données supervisé.\n\n00:01:55.517 --> 00:01:58.858\nChaque ligne de X contient window valeurs passées,\n\n00:01:58.858 --> 00:02:03.202\net la même ligne de Y contient les horizon valeurs qui ont suivi.\n\n00:02:03.202 --> 00:02:08.681\nAvec 10, 11, 12, 13, 14 et une fenêtre de trois, la première ligne est 10, 11, 12,\n\n00:02:08.681 --> 00:02:09.951\net la cible est 13.\n\n00:02:10.651 --> 00:02:14.954\nOn compare trois modèles, tous avec les mêmes méthodes fit et predict.\n\n00:02:14.954 --> 00:02:18.764\nLe modèle naïf dit que la prochaine valeur égale la dernière :\n\n00:02:18.764 --> 00:02:21.961\nc'est la référence que tout vrai modèle doit battre.\n\n00:02:21.961 --> 00:02:27.062\nLa tendance linéaire ajuste une droite des moindres carrés sur chaque fenêtre et la\n\n00:02:27.062 --> 00:02:27.615\nprolonge.\n\n00:02:27.615 --> 00:02:32.717\nLe modèle autorégressif apprend des poids W et un biais b par descente de gradient.\n\n00:02:33.417 --> 00:02:35.429\nDans fit, on standardise d'abord X,\n\n00:02:35.429 --> 00:02:39.682\ncar un prix de crypto autour de soixante mille dollars ferait exploser les\n\n00:02:39.682 --> 00:02:40.257\ngradients.\n\n00:02:40.257 --> 00:02:43.706\nPuis, à chaque itération, on calcule l'erreur de prédiction,\n\n00:02:43.706 --> 00:02:48.362\nsa dérivée par rapport à W et b, c'est-à-dire le gradient de l'erreur quadratique\n\n00:02:48.362 --> 00:02:53.017\nmoyenne plus une petite pénalité L2, et on fait un pas dans la direction opposée.\n\n00:02:53.017 --> 00:02:55.891\nLe pas vaut 1 sur L, calculé à partir des données,\n\n00:02:55.891 --> 00:02:57.903\ndonc la descente ne diverge jamais.\n\n00:02:58.603 --> 00:03:00.230\nL'évaluation suit une règle :\n\n00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:04.604\nne jamais mélanger une série temporelle, sinon le modèle apprendrait du futur.\n\n00:03:04.604 --> 00:03:07.801\nOn entraîne sur les premiers 80 % et on teste sur la fin.\n\n00:03:07.801 --> 00:03:12.288\nOn retire aussi les fenêtres juste avant le test dont les cibles le chevauchent.\n\n00:03:12.288 --> 00:03:14.588\nLe meilleur modèle a le RMSE le plus bas,\n\n00:03:14.588 --> 00:03:18.962\net on affiche le gain de chaque modèle sur la référence naïve, en pourcentage.\n\n00:03:19.662 --> 00:03:20.996\nEntraînement à l'oral.\n\n00:03:20.996 --> 00:03:23.602\nLe MSE est la moyenne des erreurs au carré,\n\n00:03:23.602 --> 00:03:26.088\ndonc les grosses erreurs pèsent beaucoup.\n\n00:03:26.088 --> 00:03:30.755\nLe RMSE est sa racine carrée, donc il est dans la même unité que les données,\n\n00:03:30.755 --> 00:03:31.847\ndegrés ou dollars.\n\n00:03:31.847 --> 00:03:36.817\nLe MAE est l'erreur absolue moyenne, moins sensible à une valeur aberrante isolée.\n\n00:03:36.817 --> 00:03:38.878\nOn classe les modèles sur le RMSE.\n\n00:03:39.578 --> 00:03:40.452\nPoints faibles.\n\n00:03:40.452 --> 00:03:43.716\nUn fichier oublié, chart_forecast, n'est jamais importé,\n\n00:03:43.716 --> 00:03:48.495\net il planterait s'il l'était, car il passe un argument que run_forecast n'accepte\n\n00:03:48.495 --> 00:03:48.787\npas :\n\n00:03:48.787 --> 00:03:50.244\nil faudrait le supprimer.\n\n00:03:50.244 --> 00:03:54.615\nLe fichier .env d'exemple contient le vrai token, ce qui va contre son but.\n\n00:03:54.615 --> 00:03:58.869\nEt le conteneur lance le serveur de développement de Flask en mode debug.\n\n00:03:59.569 --> 00:04:00.267\nÀ retenir :\n\n00:04:00.267 --> 00:04:05.025\néchapper chaque valeur utilisateur, préférer des filtres d'égalité indexés,\n\n00:04:05.025 --> 00:04:09.021\nutiliser des threads pour des requêtes qui attendent le réseau,\n\n00:04:09.021 --> 00:04:13.715\net pour la prévision, des fenêtres glissantes, un découpage chronologique,\n\n00:04:13.715 --> 00:04:16.570\nune référence naïve et le RMSE pour comparer.\n\n00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:17.648\nProchaine vidéo :\n\n00:04:17.648 --> 00:04:19.614\nle TP4, servir un modèle Keras.\n"}, "tp4": {"en": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.472\nLab four is our first AI feature.\n\n00:00:02.472 --> 00:00:07.056\nThe user draws a digit on a canvas, the drawing is sent to Flask as JSON,\n\n00:00:07.056 --> 00:00:11.012\nFlask turns it into the exact input the neural network expects,\n\n00:00:11.012 --> 00:00:16.160\nthe Keras model predicts, and the answer comes back as JSON to be displayed on the\n\n00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:16.474\npage.\n\n00:00:17.174 --> 00:00:18.433\nHere is the flow.\n\n00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:21.468\nThe canvas is exported as a PNG data URL.\n\n00:00:21.468 --> 00:00:26.650\nJavaScript posts it with fetch, as JSON, to slash predict slash mnist.\n\n00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:31.757\nFlask decodes the base64 image, preprocesses it, calls model.predict,\n\n00:00:31.757 --> 00:00:33.608\nand answers with jsonify.\n\n00:00:33.608 --> 00:00:35.311\nThe page never reloads.\n\n00:00:36.011 --> 00:00:39.753\nOn the page, toDataURL turns the canvas into a data URL:\n\n00:00:39.753 --> 00:00:42.225\nthe PNG file, encoded in base64 text.\n\n00:00:42.225 --> 00:00:44.765\nFetch posts it in a small JSON object.\n\n00:00:44.765 --> 00:00:50.178\nWhen the answer arrives, we read the class and the confidence and write them into\n\n00:00:50.178 --> 00:00:50.779\nthe page.\n\n00:00:51.479 --> 00:00:54.243\nAll models are described in one dictionary:\n\n00:00:54.243 --> 00:00:57.586\na display name, the path of the H5 file, and a type.\n\n00:00:57.586 --> 00:00:59.193\nThe type is what matters:\n\n00:00:59.193 --> 00:01:02.985\ngrayscale 28 for MNIST and Fashion MNIST, RGB 32 for CIFAR.\n\n00:01:02.985 --> 00:01:08.256\nAdding a model means adding one line, and the routes and the preprocessing follow.\n\n00:01:08.956 --> 00:01:14.054\nLoading an H5 file takes one or two seconds, far too slow to do on every request.\n\n00:01:14.054 --> 00:01:16.634\nSo we keep loaded models in a dictionary:\n\n00:01:16.634 --> 00:01:20.472\nthe first prediction loads the model, the next ones reuse it.\n\n00:01:20.472 --> 00:01:24.437\nAnd we load with compile equals False, because we only predict:\n\n00:01:24.437 --> 00:01:27.835\nthere is no need to rebuild the optimiser or the loss.\n\n00:01:28.535 --> 00:01:30.351\nThis is the heart of the lab.\n\n00:01:30.351 --> 00:01:34.170\nWe split off the header of the data URL and decode the image.\n\n00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:38.803\nMNIST digits are white on black, but we draw black on white, so we invert.\n\n00:01:38.803 --> 00:01:41.433\nWe resize to twenty eight by twenty eight,\n\n00:01:41.433 --> 00:01:46.317\ndivide by two hundred and fifty five because the network was trained on values\n\n00:01:46.317 --> 00:01:50.950\nbetween zero and one, and reshape to one, twenty eight, twenty eight, one:\n\n00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.643\na batch of one image with one grey channel.\n\n00:01:54.343 --> 00:01:57.091\nThe route checks the model name and the payload,\n\n00:01:57.091 --> 00:02:01.042\nreturning four hundred errors with a JSON message when they're wrong.\n\n00:02:01.042 --> 00:02:02.989\nThen it preprocesses and predicts.\n\n00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:05.910\nThe softmax output holds one probability per class:\n\n00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:10.032\nargmax gives the index of the largest one, which is the predicted class,\n\n00:02:10.032 --> 00:02:13.697\nand max gives that probability, which we show as the confidence.\n\n00:02:14.397 --> 00:02:17.697\nThis lab taught us the most important lesson of the module.\n\n00:02:17.697 --> 00:02:22.339\nThe code from the handout sent raw pixels, from zero to two hundred and fifty five,\n\n00:02:22.339 --> 00:02:24.016\nwithout inverting the colours.\n\n00:02:24.016 --> 00:02:26.645\nThe network, trained on zero to one, saturates:\n\n00:02:26.645 --> 00:02:30.392\none output always wins, with almost one hundred percent confidence.\n\n00:02:30.392 --> 00:02:32.293\nSo high confidence proves nothing:\n\n00:02:32.293 --> 00:02:34.698\nthe input must look like the training data.\n\n00:02:35.398 --> 00:02:36.124\nWeak points.\n\n00:02:36.124 --> 00:02:41.206\nWe don't crop or centre the digit, so a small digit in a corner is badly recognised.\n\n00:02:41.206 --> 00:02:44.231\nNearest neighbour resizing makes the digit jagged.\n\n00:02:44.231 --> 00:02:48.103\nThe Fashion and CIFAR model files are expected but not provided.\n\n00:02:48.103 --> 00:02:50.705\nAnd every exception returns a five hundred,\n\n00:02:50.705 --> 00:02:54.153\neven for a malformed image that should be a four hundred.\n\n00:02:54.153 --> 00:02:55.363\nTP4 bis fixes these.\n\n00:02:56.063 --> 00:02:57.438\nA typical live change:\n\n00:02:57.438 --> 00:02:59.751\nreturn the three most likely classes.\n\n00:02:59.751 --> 00:03:04.750\nArgsort gives the indices sorted by probability, we reverse them and keep three.\n\n00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:06.938\nNote the int and float conversions:\n\n00:03:06.938 --> 00:03:10.437\nNumPy number types can't be serialised to JSON directly.\n\n00:03:11.137 --> 00:03:12.010\nOral practice.\n\n00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:15.628\nWhat does reshape one, twenty eight, twenty eight, one do?\n\n00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:20.617\nKeras always predicts on a batch of images, with the shape batch, height, width,\n\n00:03:20.617 --> 00:03:21.178\nchannels.\n\n00:03:21.178 --> 00:03:23.735\nWe add a batch dimension of one in front,\n\n00:03:23.735 --> 00:03:28.350\nand a channel dimension of one at the end, because the image is greyscale.\n\n00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:29.823\nTo remember:\n\n00:03:29.823 --> 00:03:33.107\nthe round trip from canvas to JSON, caching models,\n\n00:03:33.107 --> 00:03:37.680\npreprocessing exactly like the training data, and argmax for the class.\n\n00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:38.388\nNext video:\n\n00:03:38.388 --> 00:03:43.733\nTP4 bis, where models are stored in PostgreSQL and the preprocessing becomes really\n\n00:03:43.733 --> 00:03:44.184\nrobust.\n", "fr": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.959\nLe TP4 est notre première fonctionnalité d'IA.\n\n00:00:02.959 --> 00:00:05.573\nL'utilisateur dessine un chiffre sur un canvas,\n\n00:00:05.573 --> 00:00:07.631\nle dessin est envoyé à Flask en JSON,\n\n00:00:07.631 --> 00:00:11.802\nFlask le transforme exactement en l'entrée qu'attend le réseau de neurones,\n\n00:00:11.802 --> 00:00:16.196\nle modèle Keras prédit, et la réponse revient en JSON pour être affichée sur la\n\n00:00:16.196 --> 00:00:16.474\npage.\n\n00:00:17.174 --> 00:00:18.274\nVoici le flux.\n\n00:00:18.274 --> 00:00:21.257\nLe canvas est exporté en data URL PNG.\n\n00:00:21.257 --> 00:00:26.046\nJavaScript l'envoie avec fetch, en JSON, vers /predict/mnist.\n\n00:00:26.046 --> 00:00:31.149\nFlask décode l'image base64, la prétraite, appelle model.predict,\n\n00:00:31.149 --> 00:00:32.955\net répond avec jsonify.\n\n00:00:32.955 --> 00:00:35.311\nLa page ne se recharge jamais.\n\n00:00:36.011 --> 00:00:39.803\nSur la page, toDataURL transforme le canvas en data URL :\n\n00:00:39.803 --> 00:00:42.397\nle fichier PNG, encodé en texte base64.\n\n00:00:42.397 --> 00:00:45.058\nfetch l'envoie dans un petit objet JSON.\n\n00:00:45.058 --> 00:00:50.447\nQuand la réponse arrive, on lit la classe et la confiance et on les écrit dans la\n\n00:00:50.447 --> 00:00:50.779\npage.\n\n00:00:51.479 --> 00:00:54.858\nTous les modèles sont décrits dans un seul dictionnaire :\n\n00:00:54.858 --> 00:00:58.119\nun nom d'affichage, le chemin du fichier H5 et un type.\n\n00:00:58.119 --> 00:00:59.660\nC'est le type qui compte :\n\n00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:03.217\ngrayscale_28 pour MNIST et Fashion-MNIST, rgb_32 pour CIFAR.\n\n00:01:03.217 --> 00:01:05.766\nAjouter un modèle, c'est ajouter une ligne,\n\n00:01:05.766 --> 00:01:08.256\net les routes et le prétraitement suivent.\n\n00:01:08.956 --> 00:01:11.760\nCharger un fichier H5 prend une ou deux secondes,\n\n00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.391\nbien trop lent pour le faire à chaque requête.\n\n00:01:14.391 --> 00:01:17.595\nOn garde donc les modèles chargés dans un dictionnaire :\n\n00:01:17.595 --> 00:01:21.599\nla première prédiction charge le modèle, les suivantes le réutilisent.\n\n00:01:21.599 --> 00:01:25.089\nEt on charge avec compile=False, car on ne fait que prédire :\n\n00:01:25.089 --> 00:01:27.835\ninutile de reconstruire l'optimiseur ou la loss.\n\n00:01:28.535 --> 00:01:29.970\nC'est le cœur du TP.\n\n00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:33.987\nOn sépare l'en-tête de la data URL et on décode l'image.\n\n00:01:33.987 --> 00:01:39.367\nLes chiffres MNIST sont blancs sur noir, mais on dessine en noir sur blanc,\n\n00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:40.515\ndonc on inverse.\n\n00:01:40.515 --> 00:01:46.325\nOn redimensionne en 28 par 28, on divise par 255 car le réseau a été entraîné sur\n\n00:01:46.325 --> 00:01:50.558\ndes valeurs entre 0 et 1, et on reshape en (1, 28, 28, 1) :\n\n00:01:50.558 --> 00:01:53.643\nun lot d'une image avec un seul canal gris.\n\n00:01:54.343 --> 00:01:57.578\nLa route vérifie le nom du modèle et les données reçues,\n\n00:01:57.578 --> 00:02:01.796\net renvoie des erreurs 400 avec un message JSON quand elles sont fausses.\n\n00:02:01.796 --> 00:02:03.529\nPuis elle prétraite et prédit.\n\n00:02:03.529 --> 00:02:06.706\nLa sortie softmax contient une probabilité par classe :\n\n00:02:06.706 --> 00:02:10.115\nargmax donne l'indice de la plus grande, la classe prédite,\n\n00:02:10.115 --> 00:02:13.697\net max donne cette probabilité, qu'on affiche comme confiance.\n\n00:02:14.397 --> 00:02:17.954\nCe TP nous a appris la leçon la plus importante du module.\n\n00:02:17.954 --> 00:02:22.922\nLe code fourni envoyait les pixels bruts, de 0 à 255, sans inverser les couleurs.\n\n00:02:22.922 --> 00:02:25.314\nLe réseau, entraîné sur 0 à 1, sature :\n\n00:02:25.314 --> 00:02:28.933\nune sortie gagne toujours, avec presque 100 % de confiance.\n\n00:02:28.933 --> 00:02:31.509\nDonc une confiance élevée ne prouve rien :\n\n00:02:31.509 --> 00:02:34.698\nl'entrée doit ressembler aux données d'entraînement.\n\n00:02:35.398 --> 00:02:36.235\nPoints faibles.\n\n00:02:36.235 --> 00:02:38.354\nOn ne recadre ni ne centre le chiffre,\n\n00:02:38.354 --> 00:02:41.198\ndonc un petit chiffre dans un coin est mal reconnu.\n\n00:02:41.198 --> 00:02:44.935\nLe redimensionnement au plus proche voisin rend le chiffre crénelé.\n\n00:02:44.935 --> 00:02:49.006\nLes fichiers des modèles Fashion et CIFAR sont attendus mais pas fournis.\n\n00:02:49.006 --> 00:02:50.958\nEt toute exception renvoie une 500,\n\n00:02:50.958 --> 00:02:54.192\nmême pour une image mal formée qui devrait donner une 400.\n\n00:02:54.192 --> 00:02:55.363\nLe TP4bis corrige ça.\n\n00:02:56.063 --> 00:02:57.892\nUne modification typique en direct :\n\n00:02:57.892 --> 00:03:00.228\nrenvoyer les trois classes les plus probables.\n\n00:03:00.228 --> 00:03:02.666\nargsort donne les indices triés par probabilité,\n\n00:03:02.666 --> 00:03:04.495\non les inverse et on en garde trois.\n\n00:03:04.495 --> 00:03:06.323\nNotez les conversions int et float :\n\n00:03:06.323 --> 00:03:10.437\nles types numériques de NumPy ne peuvent pas être sérialisés en JSON directement.\n\n00:03:11.137 --> 00:03:12.599\nEntraînement à l'oral.\n\n00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:14.726\nQue fait reshape(1, 28, 28, 1) ?\n\n00:03:14.726 --> 00:03:20.309\nKeras prédit toujours sur un lot d'images, de forme (lot, hauteur, largeur, canaux).\n\n00:03:20.309 --> 00:03:23.299\nOn ajoute une dimension de lot de 1 au début,\n\n00:03:23.299 --> 00:03:28.350\net une dimension de canal de 1 à la fin, car l'image est en niveaux de gris.\n\n00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:29.660\nÀ retenir :\n\n00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:33.152\nl'aller-retour du canvas au JSON, la mise en cache des modèles,\n\n00:03:33.152 --> 00:03:37.587\nun prétraitement identique aux données d'entraînement, et argmax pour la classe.\n\n00:03:37.587 --> 00:03:38.530\nProchaine vidéo :\n\n00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:42.798\nle TP4bis, où les modèles sont stockés dans PostgreSQL et où le prétraitement\n\n00:03:42.798 --> 00:03:44.184\ndevient vraiment robuste.\n"}, "tp4_fr": {"en": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.481\nLab four is our first AI feature.\n\n00:00:02.481 --> 00:00:07.085\nThe user draws a digit on a canvas, the drawing is sent to Flask as JSON,\n\n00:00:07.085 --> 00:00:11.058\nFlask turns it into the exact input the neural network expects,\n\n00:00:11.058 --> 00:00:16.229\nthe Keras model predicts, and the answer comes back as JSON to be displayed on the\n\n00:00:16.229 --> 00:00:16.544\npage.\n\n00:00:17.244 --> 00:00:18.515\nHere is the flow.\n\n00:00:18.515 --> 00:00:21.581\nThe canvas is exported as a PNG data URL.\n\n00:00:21.581 --> 00:00:26.816\nJavaScript posts it with fetch, as JSON, to slash predict slash mnist.\n\n00:00:26.816 --> 00:00:31.976\nFlask decodes the base64 image, preprocesses it, calls model.predict,\n\n00:00:31.976 --> 00:00:33.846\nand answers with jsonify.\n\n00:00:33.846 --> 00:00:35.566\nThe page never reloads.\n\n00:00:36.266 --> 00:00:39.992\nOn the page, toDataURL turns the canvas into a data URL:\n\n00:00:39.992 --> 00:00:42.454\nthe PNG file, encoded in base64 text.\n\n00:00:42.454 --> 00:00:44.982\nFetch posts it in a small JSON object.\n\n00:00:44.982 --> 00:00:50.372\nWhen the answer arrives, we read the class and the confidence and write them into\n\n00:00:50.372 --> 00:00:50.970\nthe page.\n\n00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:54.551\nAll models are described in one dictionary:\n\n00:00:54.551 --> 00:00:58.035\na display name, the path of the H5 file, and a type.\n\n00:00:58.035 --> 00:00:59.710\nThe type is what matters:\n\n00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:03.662\ngrayscale 28 for MNIST and Fashion MNIST, RGB 32 for CIFAR.\n\n00:01:03.662 --> 00:01:09.156\nAdding a model means adding one line, and the routes and the preprocessing follow.\n\n00:01:09.856 --> 00:01:14.831\nLoading an H5 file takes one or two seconds, far too slow to do on every request.\n\n00:01:14.831 --> 00:01:17.349\nSo we keep loaded models in a dictionary:\n\n00:01:17.349 --> 00:01:21.095\nthe first prediction loads the model, the next ones reuse it.\n\n00:01:21.095 --> 00:01:24.965\nAnd we load with compile equals False, because we only predict:\n\n00:01:24.965 --> 00:01:28.282\nthere is no need to rebuild the optimiser or the loss.\n\n00:01:28.982 --> 00:01:30.733\nThis is the heart of the lab.\n\n00:01:30.733 --> 00:01:34.416\nWe split off the header of the data URL and decode the image.\n\n00:01:34.416 --> 00:01:38.884\nMNIST digits are white on black, but we draw black on white, so we invert.\n\n00:01:38.884 --> 00:01:41.420\nWe resize to twenty eight by twenty eight,\n\n00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:46.130\ndivide by two hundred and fifty five because the network was trained on values\n\n00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:50.599\nbetween zero and one, and reshape to one, twenty eight, twenty eight, one:\n\n00:01:50.599 --> 00:01:53.195\na batch of one image with one grey channel.\n\n00:01:53.895 --> 00:01:56.599\nThe route checks the model name and the payload,\n\n00:01:56.599 --> 00:02:00.486\nreturning four hundred errors with a JSON message when they're wrong.\n\n00:02:00.486 --> 00:02:02.402\nThen it preprocesses and predicts.\n\n00:02:02.402 --> 00:02:05.275\nThe softmax output holds one probability per class:\n\n00:02:05.275 --> 00:02:09.331\nargmax gives the index of the largest one, which is the predicted class,\n\n00:02:09.331 --> 00:02:12.936\nand max gives that probability, which we show as the confidence.\n\n00:02:13.636 --> 00:02:17.062\nThis lab taught us the most important lesson of the module.\n\n00:02:17.062 --> 00:02:21.882\nThe code from the handout sent raw pixels, from zero to two hundred and fifty five,\n\n00:02:21.882 --> 00:02:23.624\nwithout inverting the colours.\n\n00:02:23.624 --> 00:02:26.353\nThe network, trained on zero to one, saturates:\n\n00:02:26.353 --> 00:02:30.244\none output always wins, with almost one hundred percent confidence.\n\n00:02:30.244 --> 00:02:32.218\nSo high confidence proves nothing:\n\n00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:34.715\nthe input must look like the training data.\n\n00:02:35.415 --> 00:02:36.233\nWeak points.\n\n00:02:36.233 --> 00:02:41.960\nWe don't crop or centre the digit, so a small digit in a corner is badly recognised.\n\n00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:45.368\nNearest neighbour resizing makes the digit jagged.\n\n00:02:45.368 --> 00:02:49.731\nThe Fashion and CIFAR model files are expected but not provided.\n\n00:02:49.731 --> 00:02:52.662\nAnd every exception returns a five hundred,\n\n00:02:52.662 --> 00:02:56.548\neven for a malformed image that should be a four hundred.\n\n00:02:56.548 --> 00:02:57.911\nTP4 bis fixes these.\n\n00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:00.120\nA typical live change:\n\n00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.657\nreturn the three most likely classes.\n\n00:03:02.657 --> 00:03:08.144\nArgsort gives the indices sorted by probability, we reverse them and keep three.\n\n00:03:08.144 --> 00:03:10.544\nNote the int and float conversions:\n\n00:03:10.544 --> 00:03:14.384\nNumPy number types can't be serialised to JSON directly.\n\n00:03:15.084 --> 00:03:15.969\nOral practice.\n\n00:03:15.969 --> 00:03:19.638\nWhat does reshape one, twenty eight, twenty eight, one do?\n\n00:03:19.638 --> 00:03:24.698\nKeras always predicts on a batch of images, with the shape batch, height, width,\n\n00:03:24.698 --> 00:03:25.267\nchannels.\n\n00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:27.860\nWe add a batch dimension of one in front,\n\n00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:32.541\nand a channel dimension of one at the end, because the image is greyscale.\n\n00:03:33.241 --> 00:03:34.014\nTo remember:\n\n00:03:34.014 --> 00:03:37.301\nthe round trip from canvas to JSON, caching models,\n\n00:03:37.301 --> 00:03:41.877\npreprocessing exactly like the training data, and argmax for the class.\n\n00:03:41.877 --> 00:03:42.586\nNext video:\n\n00:03:42.586 --> 00:03:47.935\nTP4 bis, where models are stored in PostgreSQL and the preprocessing becomes really\n\n00:03:47.935 --> 00:03:48.386\nrobust.\n", "fr": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.970\nLe TP4 est notre première fonctionnalité d'IA.\n\n00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:05.595\nL'utilisateur dessine un chiffre sur un canvas,\n\n00:00:05.595 --> 00:00:07.662\nle dessin est envoyé à Flask en JSON,\n\n00:00:07.662 --> 00:00:11.852\nFlask le transforme exactement en l'entrée qu'attend le réseau de neurones,\n\n00:00:11.852 --> 00:00:16.265\nle modèle Keras prédit, et la réponse revient en JSON pour être affichée sur la\n\n00:00:16.265 --> 00:00:16.544\npage.\n\n00:00:17.244 --> 00:00:18.354\nVoici le flux.\n\n00:00:18.354 --> 00:00:21.368\nLe canvas est exporté en data URL PNG.\n\n00:00:21.368 --> 00:00:26.207\nJavaScript l'envoie avec fetch, en JSON, vers /predict/mnist.\n\n00:00:26.207 --> 00:00:31.362\nFlask décode l'image base64, la prétraite, appelle model.predict,\n\n00:00:31.362 --> 00:00:33.187\net répond avec jsonify.\n\n00:00:33.187 --> 00:00:35.566\nLa page ne se recharge jamais.\n\n00:00:36.266 --> 00:00:40.041\nSur la page, toDataURL transforme le canvas en data URL :\n\n00:00:40.041 --> 00:00:42.625\nle fichier PNG, encodé en texte base64.\n\n00:00:42.625 --> 00:00:45.274\nfetch l'envoie dans un petit objet JSON.\n\n00:00:45.274 --> 00:00:50.639\nQuand la réponse arrive, on lit la classe et la confiance et on les écrit dans la\n\n00:00:50.639 --> 00:00:50.970\npage.\n\n00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:55.192\nTous les modèles sont décrits dans un seul dictionnaire :\n\n00:00:55.192 --> 00:00:58.590\nun nom d'affichage, le chemin du fichier H5 et un type.\n\n00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.197\nC'est le type qui compte :\n\n00:01:00.197 --> 00:01:03.904\ngrayscale_28 pour MNIST et Fashion-MNIST, rgb_32 pour CIFAR.\n\n00:01:03.904 --> 00:01:06.561\nAjouter un modèle, c'est ajouter une ligne,\n\n00:01:06.561 --> 00:01:09.156\net les routes et le prétraitement suivent.\n\n00:01:09.856 --> 00:01:12.592\nCharger un fichier H5 prend une ou deux secondes,\n\n00:01:12.592 --> 00:01:15.160\nbien trop lent pour le faire à chaque requête.\n\n00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:18.287\nOn garde donc les modèles chargés dans un dictionnaire :\n\n00:01:18.287 --> 00:01:22.195\nla première prédiction charge le modèle, les suivantes le réutilisent.\n\n00:01:22.195 --> 00:01:25.601\nEt on charge avec compile=False, car on ne fait que prédire :\n\n00:01:25.601 --> 00:01:28.282\ninutile de reconstruire l'optimiseur ou la loss.\n\n00:01:28.982 --> 00:01:30.365\nC'est le cœur du TP.\n\n00:01:30.365 --> 00:01:34.239\nOn sépare l'en-tête de la data URL et on décode l'image.\n\n00:01:34.239 --> 00:01:39.428\nLes chiffres MNIST sont blancs sur noir, mais on dessine en noir sur blanc,\n\n00:01:39.428 --> 00:01:40.535\ndonc on inverse.\n\n00:01:40.535 --> 00:01:46.139\nOn redimensionne en 28 par 28, on divise par 255 car le réseau a été entraîné sur\n\n00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:50.220\ndes valeurs entre 0 et 1, et on reshape en (1, 28, 28, 1) :\n\n00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:53.195\nun lot d'une image avec un seul canal gris.\n\n00:01:53.895 --> 00:01:57.078\nLa route vérifie le nom du modèle et les données reçues,\n\n00:01:57.078 --> 00:02:01.227\net renvoie des erreurs 400 avec un message JSON quand elles sont fausses.\n\n00:02:01.227 --> 00:02:02.933\nPuis elle prétraite et prédit.\n\n00:02:02.933 --> 00:02:06.059\nLa sortie softmax contient une probabilité par classe :\n\n00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:09.412\nargmax donne l'indice de la plus grande, la classe prédite,\n\n00:02:09.412 --> 00:02:12.936\net max donne cette probabilité, qu'on affiche comme confiance.\n\n00:02:13.636 --> 00:02:17.330\nCe TP nous a appris la leçon la plus importante du module.\n\n00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:22.488\nLe code fourni envoyait les pixels bruts, de 0 à 255, sans inverser les couleurs.\n\n00:02:22.488 --> 00:02:24.972\nLe réseau, entraîné sur 0 à 1, sature :\n\n00:02:24.972 --> 00:02:28.729\nune sortie gagne toujours, avec presque 100 % de confiance.\n\n00:02:28.729 --> 00:02:31.404\nDonc une confiance élevée ne prouve rien :\n\n00:02:31.404 --> 00:02:34.715\nl'entrée doit ressembler aux données d'entraînement.\n\n00:02:35.415 --> 00:02:36.358\nPoints faibles.\n\n00:02:36.358 --> 00:02:38.746\nOn ne recadre ni ne centre le chiffre,\n\n00:02:38.746 --> 00:02:41.951\ndonc un petit chiffre dans un coin est mal reconnu.\n\n00:02:41.951 --> 00:02:46.161\nLe redimensionnement au plus proche voisin rend le chiffre crénelé.\n\n00:02:46.161 --> 00:02:50.748\nLes fichiers des modèles Fashion et CIFAR sont attendus mais pas fournis.\n\n00:02:50.748 --> 00:02:52.947\nEt toute exception renvoie une 500,\n\n00:02:52.947 --> 00:02:56.592\nmême pour une image mal formée qui devrait donner une 400.\n\n00:02:56.592 --> 00:02:57.911\nLe TP4bis corrige ça.\n\n00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:00.618\nUne modification typique en direct :\n\n00:03:00.618 --> 00:03:03.182\nrenvoyer les trois classes les plus probables.\n\n00:03:03.182 --> 00:03:05.857\nargsort donne les indices triés par probabilité,\n\n00:03:05.857 --> 00:03:07.863\non les inverse et on en garde trois.\n\n00:03:07.863 --> 00:03:09.870\nNotez les conversions int et float :\n\n00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:14.384\nles types numériques de NumPy ne peuvent pas être sérialisés en JSON directement.\n\n00:03:15.084 --> 00:03:16.567\nEntraînement à l'oral.\n\n00:03:16.567 --> 00:03:18.724\nQue fait reshape(1, 28, 28, 1) ?\n\n00:03:18.724 --> 00:03:24.385\nKeras prédit toujours sur un lot d'images, de forme (lot, hauteur, largeur, canaux).\n\n00:03:24.385 --> 00:03:27.418\nOn ajoute une dimension de lot de 1 au début,\n\n00:03:27.418 --> 00:03:32.541\net une dimension de canal de 1 à la fin, car l'image est en niveaux de gris.\n\n00:03:33.241 --> 00:03:33.851\nÀ retenir :\n\n00:03:33.851 --> 00:03:37.346\nl'aller-retour du canvas au JSON, la mise en cache des modèles,\n\n00:03:37.346 --> 00:03:41.784\nun prétraitement identique aux données d'entraînement, et argmax pour la classe.\n\n00:03:41.784 --> 00:03:42.727\nProchaine vidéo :\n\n00:03:42.727 --> 00:03:46.999\nle TP4bis, où les modèles sont stockés dans PostgreSQL et où le prétraitement\n\n00:03:46.999 --> 00:03:48.386\ndevient vraiment robuste.\n"}, "tp4bis": {"en": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.750\nTP4 bis turns the digit demo into a real application.\n\n00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:08.680\nKeras models are uploaded or imported from a URL and stored inside PostgreSQL.\n\n00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:11.587\nYou predict on an uploaded image or a drawing,\n\n00:00:11.587 --> 00:00:14.494\nyou can compare several preprocessing methods,\n\n00:00:14.494 --> 00:00:17.212\nand every prediction is saved in a history.\n\n00:00:17.912 --> 00:00:19.777\nDocker compose starts two services:\n\n00:00:19.777 --> 00:00:22.549\nthe Flask web app and a PostgreSQL sixteen database.\n\n00:00:22.549 --> 00:00:26.120\nInside the app, routes.py only reads the request and renders pages,\n\n00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.092\nservices.py holds the business rules,\n\n00:00:28.092 --> 00:00:31.076\ninference.py does the image maths with Keras and Pillow,\n\n00:00:31.076 --> 00:00:32.941\nand models.py describes the tables.\n\n00:00:33.641 --> 00:00:37.075\nIn the compose file, the database URL points to the host db:\n\n00:00:37.075 --> 00:00:40.393\ninside the compose network, a service name is a host name.\n\n00:00:40.393 --> 00:00:45.085\nDepends on with condition service healthy waits for the health check, pg is ready,\n\n00:00:45.085 --> 00:00:47.831\nto succeed, not just for the container to start.\n\n00:00:47.831 --> 00:00:49.834\nCredentials come from the.env file,\n\n00:00:49.834 --> 00:00:52.752\nand a named volume keeps the data between restarts.\n\n00:00:53.452 --> 00:00:58.646\nThe model table uses the modern typed SQLAlchemy syntax, Mapped and mapped column.\n\n00:00:58.646 --> 00:01:02.446\nThe h5 file itself is a LargeBinary column, marked deferred:\n\n00:01:02.446 --> 00:01:06.182\nlisting models never reads the four megabytes of each file,\n\n00:01:06.182 --> 00:01:07.766\nonly accessing.data does.\n\n00:01:07.766 --> 00:01:13.022\nThe SHA two five six fingerprint is unique, so the same file can't be stored twice.\n\n00:01:13.022 --> 00:01:17.329\nInput shape and number of classes are read from the model at import.\n\n00:01:18.029 --> 00:01:22.717\nEach prediction points to its model with a foreign key declared on delete set null.\n\n00:01:22.717 --> 00:01:27.235\nIf we delete a model, its predictions are kept and the link simply becomes null,\n\n00:01:27.235 --> 00:01:30.681\nand we copied the model's name so the history stays readable.\n\n00:01:30.681 --> 00:01:33.166\nWe also store all the probabilities as JSON,\n\n00:01:33.166 --> 00:01:35.538\nand the image exactly as the model saw it.\n\n00:01:36.238 --> 00:01:38.692\nStoring a model starts with the fingerprint.\n\n00:01:38.692 --> 00:01:43.378\nIf a model with the same hash exists, we return it instead of inserting a duplicate.\n\n00:01:43.378 --> 00:01:47.896\nThen inspect model loads the file with Keras to read its input shape and classes,\n\n00:01:47.896 --> 00:01:50.797\nand raises a ValueError if it is not a usable model.\n\n00:01:50.797 --> 00:01:55.371\nEvery business error is a ValueError, and the route turns it into a flash message,\n\n00:01:55.371 --> 00:01:57.491\nso the user never sees a five hundred.\n\n00:01:58.191 --> 00:01:59.849\nThis is the big improvement.\n\n00:01:59.849 --> 00:02:02.099\nWe invert only if the border is light,\n\n00:02:02.099 --> 00:02:05.238\nso it works for paper photos and dark drawings alike.\n\n00:02:05.238 --> 00:02:08.317\nWe erase faint noise, crop tightly around the digit,\n\n00:02:08.317 --> 00:02:12.284\nscale its longest side into a twenty pixel box keeping proportions,\n\n00:02:12.284 --> 00:02:16.903\nwith smooth Lanczos resizing, and paste it into a black twenty eight by twenty\n\n00:02:16.903 --> 00:02:19.450\neight image, centred on its centre of mass.\n\n00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:22.706\nThat is exactly how the MNIST dataset itself was built.\n\n00:02:23.406 --> 00:02:26.906\nThe upload route accepts either a file, from request.files,\n\n00:02:26.906 --> 00:02:30.169\nor a drawing sent as a data URL in a hidden form field.\n\n00:02:30.169 --> 00:02:35.093\nThe requested preprocessing modes are filtered against the ones that fit the model,\n\n00:02:35.093 --> 00:02:35.983\ngrey or colour.\n\n00:02:35.983 --> 00:02:39.483\nRun inference predicts once per mode and saves each result.\n\n00:02:39.483 --> 00:02:42.686\nAny ValueError becomes a flash message and a redirect.\n\n00:02:43.386 --> 00:02:45.715\nA few details make it production like.\n\n00:02:45.715 --> 00:02:47.861\nLoaded models are cached in memory,\n\n00:02:47.861 --> 00:02:51.600\nand a lock stops two threads from loading the same one twice.\n\n00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.155\nRequest size is limited, with a clean four one three page,\n\n00:02:55.155 --> 00:02:59.814\nand Pillow refuses giant images, which protects against decompression bombs.\n\n00:02:59.814 --> 00:03:02.695\nUploaded file names go through secure filename.\n\n00:03:02.695 --> 00:03:06.434\nAnd at startup, create all is retried while PostgreSQL boots,\n\n00:03:06.434 --> 00:03:09.928\nfollowed by small alter table migrations for new columns.\n\n00:03:10.628 --> 00:03:11.441\nOral practice.\n\n00:03:11.441 --> 00:03:14.751\nWhy store the h5 in the database rather than in a folder?\n\n00:03:14.751 --> 00:03:16.899\nBecause there is one source of truth:\n\n00:03:16.899 --> 00:03:21.139\nthe model, its metadata and its history are backed up and moved together,\n\n00:03:21.139 --> 00:03:23.345\nand the web container stays stateless.\n\n00:03:23.345 --> 00:03:27.817\nThe cost is a bigger database, which is why the column is deferred and loaded\n\n00:03:27.817 --> 00:03:29.443\nmodels are cached in memory.\n\n00:03:30.143 --> 00:03:31.793\nWhat does create all not do?\n\n00:03:31.793 --> 00:03:36.332\nIt creates missing tables, but it never modifies a table that already exists.\n\n00:03:36.332 --> 00:03:38.336\nAdding a column needs a migration:\n\n00:03:38.336 --> 00:03:41.519\nhere we run a small alter table add column at startup,\n\n00:03:41.519 --> 00:03:45.469\nand in a bigger project we would use Alembic through Flask Migrate.\n\n00:03:46.169 --> 00:03:46.934\nTo remember:\n\n00:03:46.934 --> 00:03:52.165\nthe compose health check, binary columns that are deferred, deduplication by hash,\n\n00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:56.631\nset null to keep the history, value errors turned into flash messages,\n\n00:03:56.631 --> 00:03:59.757\nand preprocessing that rebuilds the MNIST format.\n\n00:03:59.757 --> 00:04:00.459\nNext video:\n\n00:04:00.459 --> 00:04:03.903\nlab five, five AI exercises organised with Blueprints.\n", "fr": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.007\nLe TP4bis transforme la démo de chiffres en vraie application.\n\n00:00:04.007 --> 00:00:08.253\nLes modèles Keras sont envoyés ou importés depuis une URL et stockés dans\n\n00:00:08.253 --> 00:00:08.893\nPostgreSQL.\n\n00:00:08.893 --> 00:00:11.511\nOn prédit sur une image envoyée ou un dessin,\n\n00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:13.954\non peut comparer plusieurs prétraitements,\n\n00:00:13.954 --> 00:00:17.212\net chaque prédiction est enregistrée dans un historique.\n\n00:00:17.912 --> 00:00:19.824\nDocker compose lance deux services :\n\n00:00:19.824 --> 00:00:21.948\nl'appli Flask et une base PostgreSQL 16.\n\n00:00:21.948 --> 00:00:26.091\nDans l'appli, routes.py se contente de lire la requête et de rendre les pages,\n\n00:00:26.091 --> 00:00:28.162\nservices.py contient les règles métier,\n\n00:00:28.162 --> 00:00:31.295\ninference.py fait les calculs d'image avec Keras et Pillow,\n\n00:00:31.295 --> 00:00:32.941\net models.py décrit les tables.\n\n00:00:33.641 --> 00:00:37.121\nDans le fichier compose, l'URL de la base pointe vers l'hôte db :\n\n00:00:37.121 --> 00:00:40.333\ndans le réseau compose, un nom de service est un nom d'hôte.\n\n00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:44.615\ndepends_on avec condition service_healthy attend que le healthcheck, pg_isready,\n\n00:00:44.615 --> 00:00:47.292\nréussisse, pas seulement que le conteneur démarre.\n\n00:00:47.292 --> 00:00:49.540\nLes identifiants viennent du fichier .env,\n\n00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:52.752\net un volume nommé garde les données entre les redémarrages.\n\n00:00:53.452 --> 00:00:57.113\nLa table des modèles utilise la syntaxe SQLAlchemy typée moderne,\n\n00:00:57.113 --> 00:00:58.464\nMapped et mapped_column.\n\n00:00:58.464 --> 00:01:02.406\nLe fichier h5 lui-même est une colonne LargeBinary, marquée deferred :\n\n00:01:02.406 --> 00:01:06.517\nlister les modèles ne lit jamais les quatre mégaoctets de chaque fichier,\n\n00:01:06.517 --> 00:01:08.150\nseul l'accès à .data le fait.\n\n00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:12.711\nL'empreinte SHA-256 est unique, donc le même fichier ne peut pas être stocké deux\n\n00:01:12.711 --> 00:01:12.993\nfois.\n\n00:01:12.993 --> 00:01:17.329\nLa forme d'entrée et le nombre de classes sont lus dans le modèle à l'import.\n\n00:01:18.029 --> 00:01:22.116\nChaque prédiction pointe vers son modèle avec une clé étrangère déclarée ondelete\n\n00:01:22.116 --> 00:01:22.570\nSET NULL.\n\n00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:26.405\nSi on supprime un modèle, ses prédictions sont conservées et le lien devient\n\n00:01:26.405 --> 00:01:30.593\nsimplement nul, et on a copié le nom du modèle pour que l'historique reste lisible.\n\n00:01:30.593 --> 00:01:33.015\nOn stocke aussi toutes les probabilités en JSON,\n\n00:01:33.015 --> 00:01:35.538\net l'image exactement telle que le modèle l'a vue.\n\n00:01:36.238 --> 00:01:38.467\nStocker un modèle commence par l'empreinte.\n\n00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:42.717\nSi un modèle avec le même hash existe, on le renvoie au lieu d'insérer un doublon.\n\n00:01:42.717 --> 00:01:46.916\nEnsuite inspect_model charge le fichier avec Keras pour lire sa forme d'entrée et\n\n00:01:46.916 --> 00:01:50.752\nses classes, et lève une ValueError si ce n'est pas un modèle exploitable.\n\n00:01:50.752 --> 00:01:52.826\nChaque erreur métier est une ValueError,\n\n00:01:52.826 --> 00:01:55.055\net la route la transforme en message flash,\n\n00:01:55.055 --> 00:01:57.491\ndonc l'utilisateur ne voit jamais d'erreur 500.\n\n00:01:58.191 --> 00:01:59.713\nC'est la grosse amélioration.\n\n00:01:59.713 --> 00:02:01.708\nOn n'inverse que si le bord est clair,\n\n00:02:01.708 --> 00:02:05.278\ndonc ça marche pour une photo de papier comme pour un dessin sombre.\n\n00:02:05.278 --> 00:02:08.480\nOn efface le bruit léger, on recadre au plus près du chiffre,\n\n00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:12.260\non ramène son plus grand côté dans une boîte de 20 pixels en gardant les\n\n00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:15.042\nproportions, avec un redimensionnement Lanczos lisse,\n\n00:02:15.042 --> 00:02:19.294\net on le colle dans une image noire de 28 par 28, centré sur son centre de masse.\n\n00:02:19.294 --> 00:02:22.706\nC'est exactement ainsi que le jeu MNIST lui-même a été construit.\n\n00:02:23.406 --> 00:02:26.725\nLa route d'envoi accepte soit un fichier, depuis request.files,\n\n00:02:26.725 --> 00:02:30.465\nsoit un dessin envoyé comme data URL dans un champ caché du formulaire.\n\n00:02:30.465 --> 00:02:34.574\nLes modes de prétraitement demandés sont filtrés selon ceux qui conviennent au\n\n00:02:34.574 --> 00:02:35.838\nmodèle, gris ou couleur.\n\n00:02:35.838 --> 00:02:39.473\nrun_inference prédit une fois par mode et enregistre chaque résultat.\n\n00:02:39.473 --> 00:02:42.686\nToute ValueError devient un message flash et une redirection.\n\n00:02:43.386 --> 00:02:46.157\nQuelques détails la rendent proche de la production.\n\n00:02:46.157 --> 00:02:48.769\nLes modèles chargés sont mis en cache en mémoire,\n\n00:02:48.769 --> 00:02:52.127\net un verrou empêche deux threads de charger le même deux fois.\n\n00:02:52.127 --> 00:02:55.378\nLa taille des requêtes est limitée, avec une page 413 propre,\n\n00:02:55.378 --> 00:02:59.641\net Pillow refuse les images géantes, ce qui protège des bombes de décompression.\n\n00:02:59.641 --> 00:03:02.679\nLes noms de fichiers envoyés passent par secure_filename.\n\n00:03:02.679 --> 00:03:06.304\nEt au démarrage, create_all est relancé tant que PostgreSQL démarre,\n\n00:03:06.304 --> 00:03:09.928\nsuivi de petites migrations ALTER TABLE pour les nouvelles colonnes.\n\n00:03:10.628 --> 00:03:11.800\nEntraînement à l'oral.\n\n00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:15.212\nPourquoi stocker le h5 dans la base plutôt que dans un dossier ?\n\n00:03:15.212 --> 00:03:17.557\nParce qu'il y a une seule source de vérité :\n\n00:03:17.557 --> 00:03:21.981\nle modèle, ses métadonnées et son historique sont sauvegardés et déplacés ensemble,\n\n00:03:21.981 --> 00:03:23.900\net le conteneur web reste sans état.\n\n00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:25.818\nLe coût, c'est une base plus grosse,\n\n00:03:25.818 --> 00:03:29.443\nd'où la colonne deferred et le cache des modèles chargés en mémoire.\n\n00:03:30.143 --> 00:03:31.721\nQue ne fait pas create_all ?\n\n00:03:31.721 --> 00:03:35.890\nIl crée les tables manquantes, mais ne modifie jamais une table existante.\n\n00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:38.313\nAjouter une colonne demande une migration :\n\n00:03:38.313 --> 00:03:41.581\nici on lance un petit ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN au démarrage,\n\n00:03:41.581 --> 00:03:45.469\net dans un plus gros projet on utiliserait Alembic via Flask-Migrate.\n\n00:03:46.169 --> 00:03:46.813\nÀ retenir :\n\n00:03:46.813 --> 00:03:50.207\nle healthcheck de compose, des colonnes binaires deferred,\n\n00:03:50.207 --> 00:03:53.778\nla déduplication par hash, SET NULL pour garder l'historique,\n\n00:03:53.778 --> 00:03:56.470\nles ValueError transformées en messages flash,\n\n00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:59.514\net un prétraitement qui reconstruit le format MNIST.\n\n00:03:59.514 --> 00:04:00.509\nProchaine vidéo :\n\n00:04:00.509 --> 00:04:03.903\nle TP5, cinq exercices d'IA organisés avec des Blueprints.\n"}, "tp4bis_fr": {"en": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.757\nTP4 bis turns the digit demo into a real application.\n\n00:00:03.757 --> 00:00:08.697\nKeras models are uploaded or imported from a URL and stored inside PostgreSQL.\n\n00:00:08.697 --> 00:00:11.610\nYou predict on an uploaded image or a drawing,\n\n00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:14.523\nyou can compare several preprocessing methods,\n\n00:00:14.523 --> 00:00:17.247\nand every prediction is saved in a history.\n\n00:00:17.947 --> 00:00:20.207\nDocker compose starts two services:\n\n00:00:20.207 --> 00:00:23.565\nthe Flask web app and a PostgreSQL sixteen database.\n\n00:00:23.565 --> 00:00:27.892\nInside the app, routes.py only reads the request and renders pages,\n\n00:00:27.892 --> 00:00:30.281\nservices.py holds the business rules,\n\n00:00:30.281 --> 00:00:33.897\ninference.py does the image maths with Keras and Pillow,\n\n00:00:33.897 --> 00:00:36.157\nand models.py describes the tables.\n\n00:00:36.857 --> 00:00:40.304\nIn the compose file, the database URL points to the host db:\n\n00:00:40.304 --> 00:00:43.636\ninside the compose network, a service name is a host name.\n\n00:00:43.636 --> 00:00:48.346\nDepends on with condition service healthy waits for the health check, pg is ready,\n\n00:00:48.346 --> 00:00:51.103\nto succeed, not just for the container to start.\n\n00:00:51.103 --> 00:00:53.114\nCredentials come from the.env file,\n\n00:00:53.114 --> 00:00:56.043\nand a named volume keeps the data between restarts.\n\n00:00:56.743 --> 00:01:02.187\nThe model table uses the modern typed SQLAlchemy syntax, Mapped and mapped column.\n\n00:01:02.187 --> 00:01:06.170\nThe h5 file itself is a LargeBinary column, marked deferred:\n\n00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:10.086\nlisting models never reads the four megabytes of each file,\n\n00:01:10.086 --> 00:01:11.746\nonly accessing.data does.\n\n00:01:11.746 --> 00:01:17.256\nThe SHA two five six fingerprint is unique, so the same file can't be stored twice.\n\n00:01:17.256 --> 00:01:21.770\nInput shape and number of classes are read from the model at import.\n\n00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:27.515\nEach prediction points to its model with a foreign key declared on delete set null.\n\n00:01:27.515 --> 00:01:32.378\nIf we delete a model, its predictions are kept and the link simply becomes null,\n\n00:01:32.378 --> 00:01:36.086\nand we copied the model's name so the history stays readable.\n\n00:01:36.086 --> 00:01:38.760\nWe also store all the probabilities as JSON,\n\n00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.313\nand the image exactly as the model saw it.\n\n00:01:42.013 --> 00:01:44.651\nStoring a model starts with the fingerprint.\n\n00:01:44.651 --> 00:01:49.688\nIf a model with the same hash exists, we return it instead of inserting a duplicate.\n\n00:01:49.688 --> 00:01:54.544\nThen inspect model loads the file with Keras to read its input shape and classes,\n\n00:01:54.544 --> 00:01:57.662\nand raises a ValueError if it is not a usable model.\n\n00:01:57.662 --> 00:02:02.579\nEvery business error is a ValueError, and the route turns it into a flash message,\n\n00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:04.857\nso the user never sees a five hundred.\n\n00:02:05.557 --> 00:02:07.398\nThis is the big improvement.\n\n00:02:07.398 --> 00:02:09.897\nWe invert only if the border is light,\n\n00:02:09.897 --> 00:02:13.381\nso it works for paper photos and dark drawings alike.\n\n00:02:13.381 --> 00:02:16.800\nWe erase faint noise, crop tightly around the digit,\n\n00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:21.206\nscale its longest side into a twenty pixel box keeping proportions,\n\n00:02:21.206 --> 00:02:26.334\nwith smooth Lanczos resizing, and paste it into a black twenty eight by twenty\n\n00:02:26.334 --> 00:02:29.162\neight image, centred on its centre of mass.\n\n00:02:29.162 --> 00:02:32.778\nThat is exactly how the MNIST dataset itself was built.\n\n00:02:33.478 --> 00:02:37.257\nThe upload route accepts either a file, from request.files,\n\n00:02:37.257 --> 00:02:40.780\nor a drawing sent as a data URL in a hidden form field.\n\n00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:46.096\nThe requested preprocessing modes are filtered against the ones that fit the model,\n\n00:02:46.096 --> 00:02:47.057\ngrey or colour.\n\n00:02:47.057 --> 00:02:50.836\nRun inference predicts once per mode and saves each result.\n\n00:02:50.836 --> 00:02:54.295\nAny ValueError becomes a flash message and a redirect.\n\n00:02:54.995 --> 00:02:57.414\nA few details make it production like.\n\n00:02:57.414 --> 00:02:59.642\nLoaded models are cached in memory,\n\n00:02:59.642 --> 00:03:03.525\nand a lock stops two threads from loading the same one twice.\n\n00:03:03.525 --> 00:03:07.217\nRequest size is limited, with a clean four one three page,\n\n00:03:07.217 --> 00:03:12.055\nand Pillow refuses giant images, which protects against decompression bombs.\n\n00:03:12.055 --> 00:03:15.047\nUploaded file names go through secure filename.\n\n00:03:15.047 --> 00:03:18.930\nAnd at startup, create all is retried while PostgreSQL boots,\n\n00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:22.559\nfollowed by small alter table migrations for new columns.\n\n00:03:23.259 --> 00:03:24.173\nOral practice.\n\n00:03:24.173 --> 00:03:27.896\nWhy store the h5 in the database rather than in a folder?\n\n00:03:27.896 --> 00:03:30.312\nBecause there is one source of truth:\n\n00:03:30.312 --> 00:03:35.080\nthe model, its metadata and its history are backed up and moved together,\n\n00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.562\nand the web container stays stateless.\n\n00:03:37.562 --> 00:03:42.590\nThe cost is a bigger database, which is why the column is deferred and loaded\n\n00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:44.419\nmodels are cached in memory.\n\n00:03:45.119 --> 00:03:46.920\nWhat does create all not do?\n\n00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:51.871\nIt creates missing tables, but it never modifies a table that already exists.\n\n00:03:51.871 --> 00:03:54.057\nAdding a column needs a migration:\n\n00:03:54.057 --> 00:03:57.530\nhere we run a small alter table add column at startup,\n\n00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:01.838\nand in a bigger project we would use Alembic through Flask Migrate.\n\n00:04:02.538 --> 00:04:03.382\nTo remember:\n\n00:04:03.382 --> 00:04:09.147\nthe compose health check, binary columns that are deferred, deduplication by hash,\n\n00:04:09.147 --> 00:04:14.069\nset null to keep the history, value errors turned into flash messages,\n\n00:04:14.069 --> 00:04:17.514\nand preprocessing that rebuilds the MNIST format.\n\n00:04:17.514 --> 00:04:18.287\nNext video:\n\n00:04:18.287 --> 00:04:22.084\nlab five, five AI exercises organised with Blueprints.\n", "fr": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.014\nLe TP4bis transforme la démo de chiffres en vraie application.\n\n00:00:04.014 --> 00:00:08.270\nLes modèles Keras sont envoyés ou importés depuis une URL et stockés dans\n\n00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:08.911\nPostgreSQL.\n\n00:00:08.911 --> 00:00:11.534\nOn prédit sur une image envoyée ou un dessin,\n\n00:00:11.534 --> 00:00:13.982\non peut comparer plusieurs prétraitements,\n\n00:00:13.982 --> 00:00:17.247\net chaque prédiction est enregistrée dans un historique.\n\n00:00:17.947 --> 00:00:20.263\nDocker compose lance deux services :\n\n00:00:20.263 --> 00:00:22.837\nl'appli Flask et une base PostgreSQL 16.\n\n00:00:22.837 --> 00:00:27.856\nDans l'appli, routes.py se contente de lire la requête et de rendre les pages,\n\n00:00:27.856 --> 00:00:30.366\nservices.py contient les règles métier,\n\n00:00:30.366 --> 00:00:34.163\ninference.py fait les calculs d'image avec Keras et Pillow,\n\n00:00:34.163 --> 00:00:36.157\net models.py décrit les tables.\n\n00:00:36.857 --> 00:00:40.351\nDans le fichier compose, l'URL de la base pointe vers l'hôte db :\n\n00:00:40.351 --> 00:00:43.575\ndans le réseau compose, un nom de service est un nom d'hôte.\n\n00:00:43.575 --> 00:00:47.874\ndepends_on avec condition service_healthy attend que le healthcheck, pg_isready,\n\n00:00:47.874 --> 00:00:50.562\nréussisse, pas seulement que le conteneur démarre.\n\n00:00:50.562 --> 00:00:52.819\nLes identifiants viennent du fichier .env,\n\n00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:56.043\net un volume nommé garde les données entre les redémarrages.\n\n00:00:56.743 --> 00:01:00.580\nLa table des modèles utilise la syntaxe SQLAlchemy typée moderne,\n\n00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:01.996\nMapped et mapped_column.\n\n00:01:01.996 --> 00:01:06.128\nLe fichier h5 lui-même est une colonne LargeBinary, marquée deferred :\n\n00:01:06.128 --> 00:01:10.437\nlister les modèles ne lit jamais les quatre mégaoctets de chaque fichier,\n\n00:01:10.437 --> 00:01:12.149\nseul l'accès à .data le fait.\n\n00:01:12.149 --> 00:01:16.930\nL'empreinte SHA-256 est unique, donc le même fichier ne peut pas être stocké deux\n\n00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:17.225\nfois.\n\n00:01:17.225 --> 00:01:21.770\nLa forme d'entrée et le nombre de classes sont lus dans le modèle à l'import.\n\n00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:26.868\nChaque prédiction pointe vers son modèle avec une clé étrangère déclarée ondelete\n\n00:01:26.868 --> 00:01:27.357\nSET NULL.\n\n00:01:27.357 --> 00:01:31.484\nSi on supprime un modèle, ses prédictions sont conservées et le lien devient\n\n00:01:31.484 --> 00:01:35.992\nsimplement nul, et on a copié le nom du modèle pour que l'historique reste lisible.\n\n00:01:35.992 --> 00:01:38.598\nOn stocke aussi toutes les probabilités en JSON,\n\n00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:41.313\net l'image exactement telle que le modèle l'a vue.\n\n00:01:42.013 --> 00:01:44.409\nStocker un modèle commence par l'empreinte.\n\n00:01:44.409 --> 00:01:48.978\nSi un modèle avec le même hash existe, on le renvoie au lieu d'insérer un doublon.\n\n00:01:48.978 --> 00:01:53.491\nEnsuite inspect_model charge le fichier avec Keras pour lire sa forme d'entrée et\n\n00:01:53.491 --> 00:01:57.614\nses classes, et lève une ValueError si ce n'est pas un modèle exploitable.\n\n00:01:57.614 --> 00:01:59.843\nChaque erreur métier est une ValueError,\n\n00:01:59.843 --> 00:02:02.238\net la route la transforme en message flash,\n\n00:02:02.238 --> 00:02:04.857\ndonc l'utilisateur ne voit jamais d'erreur 500.\n\n00:02:05.557 --> 00:02:07.247\nC'est la grosse amélioration.\n\n00:02:07.247 --> 00:02:09.462\nOn n'inverse que si le bord est clair,\n\n00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:13.426\ndonc ça marche pour une photo de papier comme pour un dessin sombre.\n\n00:02:13.426 --> 00:02:16.982\nOn efface le bruit léger, on recadre au plus près du chiffre,\n\n00:02:16.982 --> 00:02:21.178\non ramène son plus grand côté dans une boîte de 20 pixels en gardant les\n\n00:02:21.178 --> 00:02:24.268\nproportions, avec un redimensionnement Lanczos lisse,\n\n00:02:24.268 --> 00:02:28.989\net on le colle dans une image noire de 28 par 28, centré sur son centre de masse.\n\n00:02:28.989 --> 00:02:32.778\nC'est exactement ainsi que le jeu MNIST lui-même a été construit.\n\n00:02:33.478 --> 00:02:37.061\nLa route d'envoi accepte soit un fichier, depuis request.files,\n\n00:02:37.061 --> 00:02:41.099\nsoit un dessin envoyé comme data URL dans un champ caché du formulaire.\n\n00:02:41.099 --> 00:02:45.536\nLes modes de prétraitement demandés sont filtrés selon ceux qui conviennent au\n\n00:02:45.536 --> 00:02:46.901\nmodèle, gris ou couleur.\n\n00:02:46.901 --> 00:02:50.826\nrun_inference prédit une fois par mode et enregistre chaque résultat.\n\n00:02:50.826 --> 00:02:54.295\nToute ValueError devient un message flash et une redirection.\n\n00:02:54.995 --> 00:02:57.873\nQuelques détails la rendent proche de la production.\n\n00:02:57.873 --> 00:03:00.585\nLes modèles chargés sont mis en cache en mémoire,\n\n00:03:00.585 --> 00:03:04.072\net un verrou empêche deux threads de charger le même deux fois.\n\n00:03:04.072 --> 00:03:07.449\nLa taille des requêtes est limitée, avec une page 413 propre,\n\n00:03:07.449 --> 00:03:11.876\net Pillow refuse les images géantes, ce qui protège des bombes de décompression.\n\n00:03:11.876 --> 00:03:15.031\nLes noms de fichiers envoyés passent par secure_filename.\n\n00:03:15.031 --> 00:03:18.795\nEt au démarrage, create_all est relancé tant que PostgreSQL démarre,\n\n00:03:18.795 --> 00:03:22.559\nsuivi de petites migrations ALTER TABLE pour les nouvelles colonnes.\n\n00:03:23.259 --> 00:03:24.578\nEntraînement à l'oral.\n\n00:03:24.578 --> 00:03:28.414\nPourquoi stocker le h5 dans la base plutôt que dans un dossier ?\n\n00:03:28.414 --> 00:03:31.051\nParce qu'il y a une seule source de vérité :\n\n00:03:31.051 --> 00:03:36.027\nle modèle, ses métadonnées et son historique sont sauvegardés et déplacés ensemble,\n\n00:03:36.027 --> 00:03:38.185\net le conteneur web reste sans état.\n\n00:03:38.185 --> 00:03:40.343\nLe coût, c'est une base plus grosse,\n\n00:03:40.343 --> 00:03:44.419\nd'où la colonne deferred et le cache des modèles chargés en mémoire.\n\n00:03:45.119 --> 00:03:46.840\nQue ne fait pas create_all ?\n\n00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:51.389\nIl crée les tables manquantes, mais ne modifie jamais une table existante.\n\n00:03:51.389 --> 00:03:54.032\nAjouter une colonne demande une migration :\n\n00:03:54.032 --> 00:03:57.597\nici on lance un petit ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN au démarrage,\n\n00:03:57.597 --> 00:04:01.838\net dans un plus gros projet on utiliserait Alembic via Flask-Migrate.\n\n00:04:02.538 --> 00:04:03.248\nÀ retenir :\n\n00:04:03.248 --> 00:04:06.989\nle healthcheck de compose, des colonnes binaires deferred,\n\n00:04:06.989 --> 00:04:10.924\nla déduplication par hash, SET NULL pour garder l'historique,\n\n00:04:10.924 --> 00:04:13.891\nles ValueError transformées en messages flash,\n\n00:04:13.891 --> 00:04:17.246\net un prétraitement qui reconstruit le format MNIST.\n\n00:04:17.246 --> 00:04:18.342\nProchaine vidéo :\n\n00:04:18.342 --> 00:04:22.084\nle TP5, cinq exercices d'IA organisés avec des Blueprints.\n"}, "tp5": {"en": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.436\nSessions four and five gather five machine learning labs into one Flask application:\n\n00:00:05.436 --> 00:00:09.033\na random forest with scikit learn, a dense network on MNIST,\n\n00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:11.731\na convolutional network on CIFAR one hundred,\n\n00:00:11.731 --> 00:00:14.669\nthe same network in PyTorch on different devices,\n\n00:00:14.669 --> 00:00:18.686\nand land cover classification of satellite images with VGG sixteen.\n\n00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:23.043\nEach lab lives in its own folder as a Flask blueprint with a URL prefix,\n\n00:00:23.043 --> 00:00:24.669\nslash p w one to slash p w five.\n\n00:00:24.669 --> 00:00:27.565\nApp.py creates the app and registers the five blueprints.\n\n00:00:27.565 --> 00:00:30.562\nIn each folder, routes.py handles HTTP and form validation,\n\n00:00:30.562 --> 00:00:33.254\nand a core file holds the pure machine learning code,\n\n00:00:33.254 --> 00:00:35.896\nwhich also runs from the command line without Flask.\n\n00:00:36.596 --> 00:00:40.477\nA blueprint is a mini application with a name and a URL prefix.\n\n00:00:40.477 --> 00:00:43.311\nThe train route validates the form with parse,\n\n00:00:43.311 --> 00:00:46.453\nwhich raises a ValueError on a bad value, and then,\n\n00:00:46.453 --> 00:00:50.827\ninstead of training inside the request, it submits a job and redirects.\n\n00:00:50.827 --> 00:00:52.614\nNotice url for p w one.index:\n\n00:00:52.614 --> 00:00:55.509\nendpoints are prefixed with the blueprint name.\n\n00:00:56.209 --> 00:01:00.008\nA training can take minutes, far longer than an HTTP request should.\n\n00:01:00.008 --> 00:01:02.019\nSo common.py has a tiny job manager:\n\n00:01:02.019 --> 00:01:05.538\na queue and one worker thread that runs jobs one after another.\n\n00:01:05.538 --> 00:01:09.224\nErrors are caught and stored on the job, so the worker never dies.\n\n00:01:09.224 --> 00:01:13.749\nMeanwhile the page polls slash jobs slash id.json every second to show a progress\n\n00:01:13.749 --> 00:01:15.872\nbar, and reloads when the job is done.\n\n00:01:16.572 --> 00:01:18.139\nLab one uses scikit learn.\n\n00:01:18.139 --> 00:01:20.731\nA random forest trains many decision trees,\n\n00:01:20.731 --> 00:01:24.588\neach on a random sample of the data and features, and they vote.\n\n00:01:24.588 --> 00:01:29.350\nN estimators is the number of trees, max depth limits how deep each tree grows,\n\n00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:30.857\nwhich fights overfitting.\n\n00:01:30.857 --> 00:01:34.292\nThe sensitivity study repeats each setting several times,\n\n00:01:34.292 --> 00:01:36.583\nbecause one random run proves nothing.\n\n00:01:37.283 --> 00:01:39.377\nLab two is a dense network on MNIST.\n\n00:01:39.377 --> 00:01:44.032\nEach image is flattened into seven hundred and eighty four values and divided by\n\n00:01:44.032 --> 00:01:46.476\ntwo five five; labels are one hot encoded.\n\n00:01:46.476 --> 00:01:49.676\nThe course baseline had no hidden layer and raw pixels,\n\n00:01:49.676 --> 00:01:53.109\nso it was just a linear classifier at about ninety percent.\n\n00:01:53.109 --> 00:01:56.426\nNormalising and adding hidden layers of one twenty eight,\n\n00:01:56.426 --> 00:01:59.975\nsixty four and thirty two neurons reaches about ninety eight.\n\n00:02:00.675 --> 00:02:04.124\nLab three uses a convolutional network on CIFAR one hundred,\n\n00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:06.480\nsmall colour images in a hundred classes.\n\n00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:09.641\nConvolutions detect local patterns with shared weights,\n\n00:02:09.641 --> 00:02:13.089\nmax pooling halves the size, and dropout fights overfitting.\n\n00:02:13.089 --> 00:02:16.192\nBecause each block shrinks the thirty two pixel image,\n\n00:02:16.192 --> 00:02:20.847\nthe app checks the architecture first and refuses a stack that would reduce it to\n\n00:02:20.847 --> 00:02:21.307\nnothing.\n\n00:02:22.007 --> 00:02:27.030\nLab four trains the same network in PyTorch on each available device and times it.\n\n00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:29.420\nIn PyTorch you write the loop yourself:\n\n00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:31.931\nzero grad, forward, loss, backward, step.\n\n00:02:31.931 --> 00:02:35.546\nThe model and every batch must be moved to the same device,\n\n00:02:35.546 --> 00:02:40.569\notherwise you get the classic expected all tensors to be on the same device error.\n\n00:02:40.569 --> 00:02:45.470\nAnd because GPU calls are asynchronous, we synchronize before reading the clock.\n\n00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:47.736\nFor inference, eval mode and no grad.\n\n00:02:48.436 --> 00:02:50.259\nLab five is transfer learning.\n\n00:02:50.259 --> 00:02:52.750\nWe reuse VGG sixteen trained on ImageNet,\n\n00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:56.335\nfrozen at first so only the new classification head learns,\n\n00:02:56.335 --> 00:03:00.952\nthen we unfreeze the last block and fine tune it with a small learning rate.\n\n00:03:00.952 --> 00:03:05.995\nTo map a whole satellite image, we cut it into sixty four pixel patches and predict\n\n00:03:05.995 --> 00:03:07.210\na whole row at once.\n\n00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:08.060\nScale matters:\n\n00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:12.313\nwe resample the image to ten metres per pixel, like the training data.\n\n00:03:13.013 --> 00:03:13.863\nOral practice.\n\n00:03:13.863 --> 00:03:16.655\nWhy not train directly inside the Flask route?\n\n00:03:16.655 --> 00:03:18.658\nBecause a training lasts minutes:\n\n00:03:18.658 --> 00:03:21.874\nthe request would time out and block a server thread.\n\n00:03:21.874 --> 00:03:25.819\nInstead we submit a job to a worker thread, redirect immediately,\n\n00:03:25.819 --> 00:03:28.369\nand the page polls the job status in JSON.\n\n00:03:29.069 --> 00:03:30.944\nHow do you spot overfitting?\n\n00:03:30.944 --> 00:03:35.833\nTraining accuracy keeps rising while validation accuracy stalls or falls.\n\n00:03:35.833 --> 00:03:36.636\nTo fight it:\n\n00:03:36.636 --> 00:03:42.061\ndropout, a smaller model, data augmentation, early stopping, or simply more data.\n\n00:03:42.761 --> 00:03:43.545\nTo remember:\n\n00:03:43.545 --> 00:03:47.791\nblueprints with URL prefixes, background jobs for long trainings,\n\n00:03:47.791 --> 00:03:53.147\nnormalised inputs, the PyTorch training loop with every tensor on the same device,\n\n00:03:53.147 --> 00:03:56.087\nand transfer learning, freeze then fine tune.\n\n00:03:56.087 --> 00:03:56.806\nLast video:\n\n00:03:56.806 --> 00:03:59.288\nthe graded lab, the Velonecy quadtree.\n", "fr": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.463\nLes séances 4 et 5 réunissent cinq TP de machine learning dans une seule\n\n00:00:04.463 --> 00:00:05.536\napplication Flask :\n\n00:00:05.536 --> 00:00:09.204\nune forêt aléatoire avec scikit-learn, un réseau dense sur MNIST,\n\n00:00:09.204 --> 00:00:11.180\nun réseau convolutif sur CIFAR-100,\n\n00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.058\nle même réseau en PyTorch sur différents matériels,\n\n00:00:14.058 --> 00:00:18.686\net la classification de l'occupation des sols sur des images satellite avec VGG16.\n\n00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:23.381\nChaque TP vit dans son propre dossier sous forme de Blueprint Flask avec un préfixe\n\n00:00:23.381 --> 00:00:24.440\nd'URL, de /pw1 à /pw5.\n\n00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:27.039\napp.py crée l'appli et enregistre les cinq Blueprints.\n\n00:00:27.039 --> 00:00:30.649\nDans chaque dossier, routes.py gère le HTTP et la validation du formulaire,\n\n00:00:30.649 --> 00:00:33.537\net un fichier core contient le code de machine learning pur,\n\n00:00:33.537 --> 00:00:35.896\nqui tourne aussi en ligne de commande sans Flask.\n\n00:00:36.596 --> 00:00:40.645\nUn Blueprint est une mini-application avec un nom et un préfixe d'URL.\n\n00:00:40.645 --> 00:00:43.363\nLa route train valide le formulaire avec parse,\n\n00:00:43.363 --> 00:00:46.486\nqui lève une ValueError sur une mauvaise valeur, puis,\n\n00:00:46.486 --> 00:00:50.766\nau lieu d'entraîner pendant la requête, elle soumet une tâche et redirige.\n\n00:00:50.766 --> 00:00:52.212\nNotez url_for pw1.index :\n\n00:00:52.212 --> 00:00:55.509\nles noms de routes sont préfixés par le nom du Blueprint.\n\n00:00:56.209 --> 00:00:59.885\nUn entraînement peut prendre des minutes, bien plus qu'une requête HTTP ne devrait.\n\n00:00:59.885 --> 00:01:02.409\ncommon.py contient donc un petit gestionnaire de tâches :\n\n00:01:02.409 --> 00:01:05.775\nune file et un thread de travail qui exécute les tâches l'une après l'autre.\n\n00:01:05.775 --> 00:01:09.495\nLes erreurs sont attrapées et stockées sur la tâche, donc le thread ne meurt jamais.\n\n00:01:09.495 --> 00:01:13.126\nPendant ce temps, la page interroge /jobs/id.json chaque seconde pour afficher une\n\n00:01:13.126 --> 00:01:15.872\nbarre de progression, et se recharge quand la tâche est finie.\n\n00:01:16.572 --> 00:01:18.012\nLe TP1 utilise scikit-learn.\n\n00:01:18.012 --> 00:01:21.099\nUne forêt aléatoire entraîne de nombreux arbres de décision,\n\n00:01:21.099 --> 00:01:25.214\nchacun sur un échantillon aléatoire des données et des variables, et ils votent.\n\n00:01:25.214 --> 00:01:27.066\nn_estimators est le nombre d'arbres,\n\n00:01:27.066 --> 00:01:31.284\nmax_depth limite la profondeur de chaque arbre, ce qui combat le surapprentissage.\n\n00:01:31.284 --> 00:01:34.371\nL'étude de sensibilité répète chaque réglage plusieurs fois,\n\n00:01:34.371 --> 00:01:36.583\ncar un seul essai aléatoire ne prouve rien.\n\n00:01:37.283 --> 00:01:39.689\nLe TP2 est un réseau dense sur MNIST.\n\n00:01:39.689 --> 00:01:43.590\nChaque image est aplatie en 784 valeurs et divisée par 255 ;\n\n00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:46.191\nles étiquettes sont encodées en one-hot.\n\n00:01:46.191 --> 00:01:50.482\nLa base du cours n'avait pas de couche cachée et des pixels bruts,\n\n00:01:50.482 --> 00:01:54.254\ndonc c'était juste un classifieur linéaire à environ 90 %.\n\n00:01:54.254 --> 00:01:57.440\nNormaliser et ajouter des couches cachées de 128,\n\n00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.975\n64 et 32 neurones atteint environ 98 %.\n\n00:02:00.675 --> 00:02:03.419\nLe TP3 utilise un réseau convolutif sur CIFAR-100,\n\n00:02:03.419 --> 00:02:05.724\nde petites images couleur en cent classes.\n\n00:02:05.724 --> 00:02:09.510\nLes convolutions détectent des motifs locaux avec des poids partagés,\n\n00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:14.064\nle max pooling divise la taille par deux, et le dropout combat le surapprentissage.\n\n00:02:14.064 --> 00:02:16.588\nComme chaque bloc réduit l'image de 32 pixels,\n\n00:02:16.588 --> 00:02:21.033\nl'appli vérifie d'abord l'architecture et refuse un empilement qui la réduirait à\n\n00:02:21.033 --> 00:02:21.307\nrien.\n\n00:02:22.007 --> 00:02:26.600\nLe TP4 entraîne le même réseau en PyTorch sur chaque matériel disponible et le\n\n00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:27.306\nchronomètre.\n\n00:02:27.306 --> 00:02:29.720\nEn PyTorch, on écrit la boucle soi-même :\n\n00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.369\nzero_grad, passe avant, loss, backward, step.\n\n00:02:32.369 --> 00:02:35.667\nLe modèle et chaque lot doivent être sur le même device,\n\n00:02:35.667 --> 00:02:40.553\nsinon on obtient l'erreur classique « expected all tensors to be on the same device\n\n00:02:40.553 --> 00:02:40.671\n».\n\n00:02:40.671 --> 00:02:45.440\nEt comme les appels GPU sont asynchrones, on synchronise avant de lire l'horloge.\n\n00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.736\nPour l'inférence, mode eval et no_grad.\n\n00:02:48.436 --> 00:02:50.281\nLe TP5, c'est du transfer learning.\n\n00:02:50.281 --> 00:02:52.442\nOn réutilise VGG16 entraîné sur ImageNet,\n\n00:02:52.442 --> 00:02:56.237\nd'abord gelé pour que seule la nouvelle tête de classification apprenne,\n\n00:02:56.237 --> 00:03:00.507\npuis on dégèle le dernier bloc et on l'affine avec un petit taux d'apprentissage.\n\n00:03:00.507 --> 00:03:02.878\nPour cartographier toute une image satellite,\n\n00:03:02.878 --> 00:03:06.990\non la découpe en carrés de 64 pixels et on prédit une ligne entière d'un coup.\n\n00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:07.938\nL'échelle compte :\n\n00:03:07.938 --> 00:03:12.313\non rééchantillonne l'image à 10 mètres par pixel, comme les données d'entraînement.\n\n00:03:13.013 --> 00:03:14.100\nEntraînement à l'oral.\n\n00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.013\nPourquoi ne pas entraîner directement dans la route Flask ?\n\n00:03:17.013 --> 00:03:19.136\nParce qu'un entraînement dure des minutes :\n\n00:03:19.136 --> 00:03:21.950\nla requête expirerait et bloquerait un thread du serveur.\n\n00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:25.999\nÀ la place, on soumet une tâche à un thread de travail, on redirige immédiatement,\n\n00:03:25.999 --> 00:03:28.369\net la page interroge l'état de la tâche en JSON.\n\n00:03:29.069 --> 00:03:30.889\nComment repérer le surapprentissage ?\n\n00:03:30.889 --> 00:03:34.974\nLa précision d'entraînement continue de monter alors que la précision de validation\n\n00:03:34.974 --> 00:03:35.811\nstagne ou baisse.\n\n00:03:35.811 --> 00:03:36.746\nPour le combattre :\n\n00:03:36.746 --> 00:03:40.584\ndropout, un modèle plus petit, de l'augmentation de données, l'arrêt anticipé,\n\n00:03:40.584 --> 00:03:42.061\nou simplement plus de données.\n\n00:03:42.761 --> 00:03:43.383\nÀ retenir :\n\n00:03:43.383 --> 00:03:45.364\ndes Blueprints avec préfixes d'URL,\n\n00:03:45.364 --> 00:03:49.949\ndes tâches en arrière-plan pour les longs entraînements, des entrées normalisées,\n\n00:03:49.949 --> 00:03:54.024\nla boucle d'entraînement PyTorch avec chaque tenseur sur le même device,\n\n00:03:54.024 --> 00:03:56.515\net le transfer learning, geler puis affiner.\n\n00:03:56.515 --> 00:03:57.420\nDernière vidéo :\n\n00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.288\nle TP noté, le Quadtree Velonecy.\n"}, "tp5_fr": {"en": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.644\nSessions four and five gather five machine learning labs into one Flask application:\n\n00:00:05.644 --> 00:00:09.390\na random forest with scikit learn, a dense network on MNIST,\n\n00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.199\na convolutional network on CIFAR one hundred,\n\n00:00:12.199 --> 00:00:15.258\nthe same network in PyTorch on different devices,\n\n00:00:15.258 --> 00:00:19.440\nand land cover classification of satellite images with VGG sixteen.\n\n00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:24.926\nEach lab lives in its own folder as a Flask blueprint with a URL prefix,\n\n00:00:24.926 --> 00:00:27.053\nslash p w one to slash p w five.\n\n00:00:27.053 --> 00:00:30.841\nApp.py creates the app and registers the five blueprints.\n\n00:00:30.841 --> 00:00:34.762\nIn each folder, routes.py handles HTTP and form validation,\n\n00:00:34.762 --> 00:00:38.285\nand a core file holds the pure machine learning code,\n\n00:00:38.285 --> 00:00:41.741\nwhich also runs from the command line without Flask.\n\n00:00:42.441 --> 00:00:46.632\nA blueprint is a mini application with a name and a URL prefix.\n\n00:00:46.632 --> 00:00:49.692\nThe train route validates the form with parse,\n\n00:00:49.692 --> 00:00:53.085\nwhich raises a ValueError on a bad value, and then,\n\n00:00:53.085 --> 00:00:57.808\ninstead of training inside the request, it submits a job and redirects.\n\n00:00:57.808 --> 00:00:59.737\nNotice url for p w one.index:\n\n00:00:59.737 --> 00:01:02.864\nendpoints are prefixed with the blueprint name.\n\n00:01:03.564 --> 00:01:08.229\nA training can take minutes, far longer than an HTTP request should.\n\n00:01:08.229 --> 00:01:10.699\nSo common.py has a tiny job manager:\n\n00:01:10.699 --> 00:01:15.021\na queue and one worker thread that runs jobs one after another.\n\n00:01:15.021 --> 00:01:19.549\nErrors are caught and stored on the job, so the worker never dies.\n\n00:01:19.549 --> 00:01:25.106\nMeanwhile the page polls slash jobs slash id.json every second to show a progress\n\n00:01:25.106 --> 00:01:27.713\nbar, and reloads when the job is done.\n\n00:01:28.413 --> 00:01:30.182\nLab one uses scikit learn.\n\n00:01:30.182 --> 00:01:33.107\nA random forest trains many decision trees,\n\n00:01:33.107 --> 00:01:37.460\neach on a random sample of the data and features, and they vote.\n\n00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:42.833\nN estimators is the number of trees, max depth limits how deep each tree grows,\n\n00:01:42.833 --> 00:01:44.534\nwhich fights overfitting.\n\n00:01:44.534 --> 00:01:48.411\nThe sensitivity study repeats each setting several times,\n\n00:01:48.411 --> 00:01:50.995\nbecause one random run proves nothing.\n\n00:01:51.695 --> 00:01:54.018\nLab two is a dense network on MNIST.\n\n00:01:54.018 --> 00:01:59.181\nEach image is flattened into seven hundred and eighty four values and divided by\n\n00:01:59.181 --> 00:02:01.891\ntwo five five; labels are one hot encoded.\n\n00:02:01.891 --> 00:02:05.440\nThe course baseline had no hidden layer and raw pixels,\n\n00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:09.247\nso it was just a linear classifier at about ninety percent.\n\n00:02:09.247 --> 00:02:12.925\nNormalising and adding hidden layers of one twenty eight,\n\n00:02:12.925 --> 00:02:16.861\nsixty four and thirty two neurons reaches about ninety eight.\n\n00:02:17.561 --> 00:02:21.281\nLab three uses a convolutional network on CIFAR one hundred,\n\n00:02:21.281 --> 00:02:23.823\nsmall colour images in a hundred classes.\n\n00:02:23.823 --> 00:02:27.233\nConvolutions detect local patterns with shared weights,\n\n00:02:27.233 --> 00:02:30.953\nmax pooling halves the size, and dropout fights overfitting.\n\n00:02:30.953 --> 00:02:34.300\nBecause each block shrinks the thirty two pixel image,\n\n00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:39.322\nthe app checks the architecture first and refuses a stack that would reduce it to\n\n00:02:39.322 --> 00:02:39.818\nnothing.\n\n00:02:40.518 --> 00:02:46.153\nLab four trains the same network in PyTorch on each available device and times it.\n\n00:02:46.153 --> 00:02:48.834\nIn PyTorch you write the loop yourself:\n\n00:02:48.834 --> 00:02:51.651\nzero grad, forward, loss, backward, step.\n\n00:02:51.651 --> 00:02:55.706\nThe model and every batch must be moved to the same device,\n\n00:02:55.706 --> 00:03:01.341\notherwise you get the classic expected all tensors to be on the same device error.\n\n00:03:01.341 --> 00:03:06.839\nAnd because GPU calls are asynchronous, we synchronize before reading the clock.\n\n00:03:06.839 --> 00:03:09.382\nFor inference, eval mode and no grad.\n\n00:03:10.082 --> 00:03:12.248\nLab five is transfer learning.\n\n00:03:12.248 --> 00:03:15.207\nWe reuse VGG sixteen trained on ImageNet,\n\n00:03:15.207 --> 00:03:19.466\nfrozen at first so only the new classification head learns,\n\n00:03:19.466 --> 00:03:24.953\nthen we unfreeze the last block and fine tune it with a small learning rate.\n\n00:03:24.953 --> 00:03:30.944\nTo map a whole satellite image, we cut it into sixty four pixel patches and predict\n\n00:03:30.944 --> 00:03:32.388\na whole row at once.\n\n00:03:32.388 --> 00:03:33.399\nScale matters:\n\n00:03:33.399 --> 00:03:38.452\nwe resample the image to ten metres per pixel, like the training data.\n\n00:03:39.152 --> 00:03:40.142\nOral practice.\n\n00:03:40.142 --> 00:03:43.397\nWhy not train directly inside the Flask route?\n\n00:03:43.397 --> 00:03:45.731\nBecause a training lasts minutes:\n\n00:03:45.731 --> 00:03:49.480\nthe request would time out and block a server thread.\n\n00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:54.079\nInstead we submit a job to a worker thread, redirect immediately,\n\n00:03:54.079 --> 00:03:57.050\nand the page polls the job status in JSON.\n\n00:03:57.750 --> 00:03:59.898\nHow do you spot overfitting?\n\n00:03:59.898 --> 00:04:05.499\nTraining accuracy keeps rising while validation accuracy stalls or falls.\n\n00:04:05.499 --> 00:04:06.420\nTo fight it:\n\n00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:12.635\ndropout, a smaller model, data augmentation, early stopping, or simply more data.\n\n00:04:13.335 --> 00:04:14.160\nTo remember:\n\n00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.633\nblueprints with URL prefixes, background jobs for long trainings,\n\n00:04:18.633 --> 00:04:24.276\nnormalised inputs, the PyTorch training loop with every tensor on the same device,\n\n00:04:24.276 --> 00:04:27.372\nand transfer learning, freeze then fine tune.\n\n00:04:27.372 --> 00:04:28.129\nLast video:\n\n00:04:28.129 --> 00:04:30.744\nthe graded lab, the Velonecy quadtree.\n", "fr": "WEBVTT\n\n00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.631\nLes séances 4 et 5 réunissent cinq TP de machine learning dans une seule\n\n00:00:04.631 --> 00:00:05.748\napplication Flask :\n\n00:00:05.748 --> 00:00:09.568\nune forêt aléatoire avec scikit-learn, un réseau dense sur MNIST,\n\n00:00:09.568 --> 00:00:11.624\nun réseau convolutif sur CIFAR-100,\n\n00:00:11.624 --> 00:00:14.622\nle même réseau en PyTorch sur différents matériels,\n\n00:00:14.622 --> 00:00:19.440\net la classification de l'occupation des sols sur des images satellite avec VGG16.\n\n00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:25.367\nChaque TP vit dans son propre dossier sous forme de Blueprint Flask avec un préfixe\n\n00:00:25.367 --> 00:00:26.753\nd'URL, de /pw1 à /pw5.\n\n00:00:26.753 --> 00:00:30.154\napp.py crée l'appli et enregistre les cinq Blueprints.\n\n00:00:30.154 --> 00:00:34.877\nDans chaque dossier, routes.py gère le HTTP et la validation du formulaire,\n\n00:00:34.877 --> 00:00:38.655\net un fichier core contient le code de machine learning pur,\n\n00:00:38.655 --> 00:00:41.741\nqui tourne aussi en ligne de commande sans Flask.\n\n00:00:42.441 --> 00:00:46.813\nUn Blueprint est une mini-application avec un nom et un préfixe d'URL.\n\n00:00:46.813 --> 00:00:49.748\nLa route train valide le formulaire avec parse,\n\n00:00:49.748 --> 00:00:53.121\nqui lève une ValueError sur une mauvaise valeur, puis,\n\n00:00:53.121 --> 00:00:57.742\nau lieu d'entraîner pendant la requête, elle soumet une tâche et redirige.\n\n00:00:57.742 --> 00:00:59.304\nNotez url_for pw1.index :\n\n00:00:59.304 --> 00:01:02.864\nles noms de routes sont préfixés par le nom du Blueprint.\n\n00:01:03.564 --> 00:01:08.078\nUn entraînement peut prendre des minutes, bien plus qu'une requête HTTP ne devrait.\n\n00:01:08.078 --> 00:01:11.178\ncommon.py contient donc un petit gestionnaire de tâches :\n\n00:01:11.178 --> 00:01:15.312\nune file et un thread de travail qui exécute les tâches l'une après l'autre.\n\n00:01:15.312 --> 00:01:19.881\nLes erreurs sont attrapées et stockées sur la tâche, donc le thread ne meurt jamais.\n\n00:01:19.881 --> 00:01:24.341\nPendant ce temps, la page interroge /jobs/id.json chaque seconde pour afficher une\n\n00:01:24.341 --> 00:01:27.713\nbarre de progression, et se recharge quand la tâche est finie.\n\n00:01:28.413 --> 00:01:30.039\nLe TP1 utilise scikit-learn.\n\n00:01:30.039 --> 00:01:33.522\nUne forêt aléatoire entraîne de nombreux arbres de décision,\n\n00:01:33.522 --> 00:01:38.166\nchacun sur un échantillon aléatoire des données et des variables, et ils votent.\n\n00:01:38.166 --> 00:01:40.256\nn_estimators est le nombre d'arbres,\n\n00:01:40.256 --> 00:01:45.016\nmax_depth limite la profondeur de chaque arbre, ce qui combat le surapprentissage.\n\n00:01:45.016 --> 00:01:48.499\nL'étude de sensibilité répète chaque réglage plusieurs fois,\n\n00:01:48.499 --> 00:01:50.995\ncar un seul essai aléatoire ne prouve rien.\n\n00:01:51.695 --> 00:01:54.363\nLe TP2 est un réseau dense sur MNIST.\n\n00:01:54.363 --> 00:01:58.690\nChaque image est aplatie en 784 valeurs et divisée par 255 ;\n\n00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:01.574\nles étiquettes sont encodées en one-hot.\n\n00:02:01.574 --> 00:02:06.333\nLa base du cours n'avait pas de couche cachée et des pixels bruts,\n\n00:02:06.333 --> 00:02:10.516\ndonc c'était juste un classifieur linéaire à environ 90 %.\n\n00:02:10.516 --> 00:02:14.049\nNormaliser et ajouter des couches cachées de 128,\n\n00:02:14.049 --> 00:02:16.861\n64 et 32 neurones atteint environ 98 %.\n\n00:02:17.561 --> 00:02:20.521\nLe TP3 utilise un réseau convolutif sur CIFAR-100,\n\n00:02:20.521 --> 00:02:23.007\nde petites images couleur en cent classes.\n\n00:02:23.007 --> 00:02:27.091\nLes convolutions détectent des motifs locaux avec des poids partagés,\n\n00:02:27.091 --> 00:02:32.004\nle max pooling divise la taille par deux, et le dropout combat le surapprentissage.\n\n00:02:32.004 --> 00:02:34.727\nComme chaque bloc réduit l'image de 32 pixels,\n\n00:02:34.727 --> 00:02:39.522\nl'appli vérifie d'abord l'architecture et refuse un empilement qui la réduirait à\n\n00:02:39.522 --> 00:02:39.818\nrien.\n\n00:02:40.518 --> 00:02:45.670\nLe TP4 entraîne le même réseau en PyTorch sur chaque matériel disponible et le\n\n00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:46.463\nchronomètre.\n\n00:02:46.463 --> 00:02:49.171\nEn PyTorch, on écrit la boucle soi-même :\n\n00:02:49.171 --> 00:02:52.143\nzero_grad, passe avant, loss, backward, step.\n\n00:02:52.143 --> 00:02:55.842\nLe modèle et chaque lot doivent être sur le même device,\n\n00:02:55.842 --> 00:03:01.324\nsinon on obtient l'erreur classique « expected all tensors to be on the same device\n\n00:03:01.324 --> 00:03:01.456\n».\n\n00:03:01.456 --> 00:03:06.806\nEt comme les appels GPU sont asynchrones, on synchronise avant de lire l'horloge.\n\n00:03:06.806 --> 00:03:09.382\nPour l'inférence, mode eval et no_grad.\n\n00:03:10.082 --> 00:03:12.274\nLe TP5, c'est du transfer learning.\n\n00:03:12.274 --> 00:03:14.842\nOn réutilise VGG16 entraîné sur ImageNet,\n\n00:03:14.842 --> 00:03:19.351\nd'abord gelé pour que seule la nouvelle tête de classification apprenne,\n\n00:03:19.351 --> 00:03:24.424\npuis on dégèle le dernier bloc et on l'affine avec un petit taux d'apprentissage.\n\n00:03:24.424 --> 00:03:27.242\nPour cartographier toute une image satellite,\n\n00:03:27.242 --> 00:03:32.127\non la découpe en carrés de 64 pixels et on prédit une ligne entière d'un coup.\n\n00:03:32.127 --> 00:03:33.254\nL'échelle compte :\n\n00:03:33.254 --> 00:03:38.452\non rééchantillonne l'image à 10 mètres par pixel, comme les données d'entraînement.\n\n00:03:39.152 --> 00:03:40.418\nEntraînement à l'oral.\n\n00:03:40.418 --> 00:03:43.814\nPourquoi ne pas entraîner directement dans la route Flask ?\n\n00:03:43.814 --> 00:03:46.288\nParce qu'un entraînement dure des minutes :\n\n00:03:46.288 --> 00:03:49.569\nla requête expirerait et bloquerait un thread du serveur.\n\n00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:54.288\nÀ la place, on soumet une tâche à un thread de travail, on redirige immédiatement,\n\n00:03:54.288 --> 00:03:57.050\net la page interroge l'état de la tâche en JSON.\n\n00:03:57.750 --> 00:03:59.836\nComment repérer le surapprentissage ?\n\n00:03:59.836 --> 00:04:04.516\nLa précision d'entraînement continue de monter alors que la précision de validation\n\n00:04:04.516 --> 00:04:05.474\nstagne ou baisse.\n\n00:04:05.474 --> 00:04:06.545\nPour le combattre :\n\n00:04:06.545 --> 00:04:10.943\ndropout, un modèle plus petit, de l'augmentation de données, l'arrêt anticipé,\n\n00:04:10.943 --> 00:04:12.635\nou simplement plus de données.\n\n00:04:13.335 --> 00:04:13.990\nÀ retenir :\n\n00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:16.077\ndes Blueprints avec préfixes d'URL,\n\n00:04:16.077 --> 00:04:20.907\ndes tâches en arrière-plan pour les longs entraînements, des entrées normalisées,\n\n00:04:20.907 --> 00:04:25.199\nla boucle d'entraînement PyTorch avec chaque tenseur sur le même device,\n\n00:04:25.199 --> 00:04:27.823\net le transfer learning, geler puis affiner.\n\n00:04:27.823 --> 00:04:28.777\nDernière vidéo :\n\n00:04:28.777 --> 00:04:30.744\nle TP noté, le Quadtree Velonecy.\n"}};
